# ai.wing — AI working context for Smaya Events
#
# Purpose: everything an AI session needs to pick this project up cold, plus the
# reasoning behind decisions that the code cannot explain on its own.
#
# READ THIS FIRST. Re-read `contracts` before touching animation or layout code.
#
# NOTE ON SCOPE: this file carries *knowledge*, not *history*. It cannot roll
# code back — see `restore` for what actually can.

project:
  name: Smaya Events
  tagline: Every Celebration Deserves a Touch of Magic.
  positioning: Creators of Feeling
  founder: Manasa Raj
  what_it_is: >
    Marketing site for a full-spectrum luxury experience company. NOT a wedding
    planner — seven equal practices: celebrations, corporate, fitness &
    wellness, adventure, entertainment, community, workshops & retreats. The
    nav, hero and home page must all communicate that breadth.
  root: C:\Users\pavit\Downloads\smaya events
  origin: >
    Migrated from a Vite + React Router build at ..\smaya-events (still on disk,
    read-only reference). Consolidated 17 routes to 9.

stack:
  framework: Next.js 15.5 (App Router)
  language: TypeScript (strict)
  styling: Tailwind CSS v4 (@theme tokens in app/globals.css — no tailwind.config)
  motion: framer-motion, gsap + ScrollTrigger + SplitText, lenis
  icons: lucide-react
  email: resend
  package_manager: npm

commands:
  dev: npm run dev            # port 3000
  build: npm run build
  start: npm start
  typecheck: npm run typecheck
  # No local .bin shims at times — fall back to:
  #   node node_modules/typescript/bin/tsc --noEmit
  #   node node_modules/next/dist/bin/next build

gotchas:
  - The dev server and `next build` both write .next. Running a build while dev
    is up produces a bogus "Cannot find module for page: /sitemap.xml" error.
    Stop dev, `rm -rf .next`, then build.
  - node_modules has arrived corrupt before (next/package.json missing, no
    .bin/). If imports resolve as `any`, reinstall before debugging types.
  - Directory name contains a space. Quote paths in shell commands.

routes:
  "/": Home — video hero, verticals grid, about teaser, portfolio, stats, testimonials, CTA
  "/about": About + Vision/Mission + core values + impact stats, merged
  "/services": Hub — five grouped capability categories linking into the verticals
  "/services/[slug]": All seven verticals, one shared template, generateStaticParams
  "/gallery": Filterable masonry portfolio
  "/events": Upcoming events + Event JSON-LD
  "/testimonials": Six reviews + Review JSON-LD
  "/blog": Six post cards
  "/contact": Contact + Book merged behind a Free Consultation / General Inquiry toggle
  "/api/contact": The only non-static route
  removed:
    - /vision  → absorbed into /about
    - /book    → absorbed into /contact; links go to /contact?type=consultation

data:
  principle: >
    All copy lives in typed modules under data/. Nothing is inline in a
    component. data/verticals.ts is the single source of truth for the seven
    practices — it drives the dynamic route, the navbar mega-menu, the mobile
    accordion, the services hub, the home grid and the footer.
  icons: >
    Referenced BY NAME as strings ('Waves', not the component) so data modules
    stay serialisable across the server/client boundary. lib/icons.ts is the
    allow-list — add an icon there before using it in data.

contracts:
  # Violating either of these ships a blank page. Both have already broken once.

  no_js_reveal:
    rule: >
      Every CSS rule that HIDES content until an observer fires must be scoped
      to [data-motion="on"]. That attribute is set by a blocking inline script
      in app/layout.tsx. Without JS the attribute never appears and content
      renders normally.
    applies_to: .reveal-line, .icon-draw, .path-draw
    why: >
      Previously these were hidden unconditionally, so a JS failure left the
      page permanently invisible.
    related: >
      <html> carries suppressHydrationWarning because that script stamps the
      attribute before React hydrates.

  page_transition_initial:
    rule: >
      PageTransition arms its clip-path wipe only AFTER the first client render
      (`mounted` state). Never let the clipped `initial` reach SSR markup.
    why: >
      framer-motion serialises `initial` into the SSR HTML. A clipped <main>
      shipped the entire site as a blank page with JS disabled.
    also: >
      The resting clip-path is dropped once the wipe lands, because a resting
      clip-path makes <main> a containing block for every position:fixed
      descendant.

  tilt_card_height:
    rule: >
      TiltCard's outer `[perspective:1200px]` wrapper MUST keep `h-full`.
    why: >
      CSS perspective must sit on the transformed element's parent, so that
      wrapper is an extra box between the caller and the card. Without a height
      it computes to `auto`, and callers sizing with `h-full` against
      `auto-rows-*` (portfolio, gallery) collapse the whole subtree — images
      included — to zero. This shipped, and the gallery rendered blank.

  button_full_width:
    rule: >
      To stretch a Button/ButtonLink, pass `wrapperClassName`, not `className`.
    why: >
      Both wrap the control in an `inline-block` motion.div. A `w-full` on the
      control itself resolves to 100% of that shrink-wrapped box, so it appears
      to work and silently does nothing. Shipped in the home hero CTA, which
      measured 274px inside a 335px mobile column.

  pin_needs_a_block_parent:
    rule: >
      A ScrollTrigger-pinned element must NOT have a flex container as its
      parent. components/home/Hero.tsx wraps its <section> in a plain
      `<div className="relative z-30">` for exactly this. Do not flatten it.
    why: >
      `pinSpacing` reserves the pin's scroll distance by adding padding-bottom
      to the spacer ScrollTrigger injects. When the spacer's parent is flex it
      cannot, and silently falls back to a fixed `height` equal to the element
      with `padding: 0`. <main> is `flex flex-col`, so pinning the hero section
      directly reserved ZERO space: the hero sat position:fixed while
      ServicesGrid scrolled straight up over it and sliced the headline in half.
      This SHIPPED and the user caught it on screen.
    how_to_check: >
      Read the pin-spacer's inline style. Correct looks like
      `height: <el>px; padding-bottom: <distance>px`. Broken looks like
      `height: <el>px; padding: 0px`. Then sample the next section's rect at
      several scroll positions and assert it never overlaps the pinned one.
    also: >
      ServicesGrid is z-20 and a pinned section defaults to z-auto, so give the
      pinned wrapper a higher z-index for the unpin frame regardless.

  never_put_a_class_in_a_comment:
    rule: >
      Never write a Tailwind class containing an asset reference into a code
      comment. Not as an example, not as a "here is how to swap this later".
    why: >
      Tailwind's scanner is REGEX-BASED and does not skip comments. A comment
      explaining how to swap the damask for a real PNG contained a literal
      background-image utility pointing at /damask.png. Tailwind extracted it,
      emitted a real CSS reference to a file that does not exist, and the whole
      stylesheet failed to resolve — "Module not found: Can't resolve './...'
      Import trace: ./app/globals.css". That is a 500 on EVERY route, caused by
      a comment. It happened twice in one turn, because the first fix still
      contained the token.
    how_to_check: >
      grep components and app for `bg-[url` before restarting. The error names
      globals.css, not the file that actually caused it, so it does not point at
      the comment.

  stop_the_dev_server_before_deleting_next:
    rule: >
      Stop the dev server, THEN delete .next. Never delete it underneath a
      running server.
    why: >
      Deleting it live corrupts the webpack pack cache — a stream of
      "[PackFileCacheStrategy] Caching failed ... ENOENT ... rename 0.pack.gz_"
      — after which the server keeps serving a STALE compiled module and will
      not pick up source edits. It reported `HeroMandala is not defined` from a
      file that no longer contained the reference, which sends you hunting a bug
      that is not in the source.

  parallax_units:
    rule: >
      Scroll parallax on SIBLING blocks inside one section must use PIXEL
      values from a SHARED motion value. Never percentages, and never a
      transform on one sibling but not the other.
    why: >
      A percentage translate resolves against the element's OWN height. The home
      hero had `y: 0 -> 42%` on the 516px copy column and nothing on the rail
      below it, so by mid-scroll the copy had slid ~90px into the rail and the
      CTAs, stats and rail collapsed into one unreadable band. Even giving both
      the same percentage would still diverge, because the blocks are different
      heights. This shipped and was reported on sight.
    also: >
      Depth reads backwards if the background moves LESS than the foreground.
      The ground should be the slower-parting layer.
    see: components/home/Hero.tsx → the `drift` object.

  motion_style_beats_animate:
    rule: >
      An element cannot carry a scroll-driven `style` motion value AND an
      `initial`/`animate` entrance for the same property. `style` wins, so the
      entrance silently never plays.
    fix: >
      Split them across two elements — outer carries the scroll `style`, inner
      carries the entrance. See the practice rail in components/home/Hero.tsx.

  desktop_only_effects:
    rule: >
      3D tilt, magnetic buttons and the particle field gate on
      matchMedia('(pointer: fine)') and must not attach listeners on touch —
      not merely render invisibly.
    hooks: lib/hooks.ts → usePointerFine, usePrefersReducedMotion
    note: The custom cursor is gone — see decisions.custom-cursor-removed.

  hero_intro_replay:
    rule: >
      The hero opening sequence is gated on a MODULE-level flag, not
      sessionStorage.
    why: >
      A full page reload IS an initial page load and should replay. Only
      client-side navigation back to Home should skip. sessionStorage suppressed
      it for the whole tab lifetime, so it was effectively never seen.
    watchdog: >
      HeroIntro tears its overlay down after 4200ms regardless of GSAP, because
      the doors cover the hero until they part.

design:
  tokens: app/globals.css @theme
  palette:
    smaya-plum: "#3D1F5C"       # primary
    smaya-plum-light: "#5B3480"
    smaya-plum-deep: "#231F36"  # sampled from the supplied logo monogram
    smaya-gold: "#D4AF37"       # universal accent, never changes
    smaya-gold-light: "#F0D584"
    smaya-gold-deep: "#887023"  # gold TEXT on light grounds ONLY — see contrast note
    smaya-coral: "#FF6F61"      # energy verticals only, as accent not dominant
    smaya-ivory: "#FAF9F6"
    smaya-charcoal: "#1C1B1F"
  hero_ground: >
    The HOME hero alone sits on #0D0D0E, a hard-coded near-black that is NOT a
    token. It came in with the user's written hero spec (see
    decisions.hero-asymmetric-spec) and plum survives there only as radial depth
    over the black. Every other dark surface still uses smaya-charcoal.
  accent_rule: >
    plum for celebrations / corporate / workshops (luxury); coral for fitness /
    adventure / entertainment / community (energy). Gold is universal.
  logo_decision: >
    The supplied monogram's navy samples to #231F36 — deeper and far less
    saturated than the brief's plum. Swapping the primary would have broken the
    plum→gold and plum→coral gradient system, so the sampled value is exposed
    as --color-smaya-plum-deep and used for the deepest stops and hero overlays.
    The logo therefore always sits in-family without recolouring the brand.
  logo_assets: >
    public/logo/smaya logo design.jpeg is the untouched original.
    smaya-monogram.png and smaya-monogram-light.png are derived — tight crop,
    ivory background keyed out, navy strokes recoloured to ivory for the light
    variant. Regenerate from the original if the source art changes.
  fonts: >
    next/font (Inter + Outfit), NOT the Google Fonts @import from the brief —
    the extra render-blocking stylesheet was too costly for the mobile
    Lighthouse target. @theme maps --font-sans/--font-serif onto the next/font
    CSS variables.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS BEFORE TOUCHING THE HOME HERO.
#
# It was rebuilt SIX times on 2026-08-04. The decisions list below is
# newest-first, but several hero entries in it are dead — acting on one of those
# would undo work the user asked for. This is the index.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
hero_history:
  current: hero-asymmetric-spec  (+ hud-and-vertical-hero-cta-removed)
  file: components/home/Hero.tsx
  timeline:
    - 1 home-hero-rebuild — original. Static confetti stock photo.
      Rejected: "a generic template hero".
    - 2 home-hero-showcase — cycled the seven practice video clips.
      Rejected: "remove that cinimatic clip ... without any video at the
      background". DEAD. HeroShowcase.tsx deleted.
    - 3 home-hero-typographic — type-led, StrokeText etch, 2x2 gold stats grid.
      Fixed mid-life by hero-scroll-collision-fix. Rejected: "too generic".
      DEAD as a design, but its BUG FIXES are still live — see contracts.
    - 4 hero-aperture-rebuild — kinetic masthead, spine, drawn aperture dial.
      Rejected. DEAD.
    - 5 hero-stagelight-refine — light shafts, bordered index.
      Rejected: "the hero section is horrible". DEAD, reverted to 3.
    - 6 hero-asymmetric-spec — CURRENT. Built from the user's own written
      brief, the only one that was not inference. Asymmetric 8/4 grid, vertical
      practice index, stats in a bottom bar, #0D0D0E ground, font-mono details.
  what_the_user_rejected_every_time: >
    Four heroes died before a written spec arrived. Two lessons worth more than
    the entries themselves:
      · The 2x2 grid of oversized gold statistics was named as the template
        giveaway twice. It is now a bottom bar. Do not reinstate the grid.
      · Large thin decorative graphics layered OVER the headline (the etch, the
        aperture dial) were rejected both times they were tried.
    If a fifth redesign comes up, ask for a reference the user likes before
    building. Inference failed four times here at real cost.
  also_now_gone:
    # 2026-08-04, latest pass. Both deliberate, both on instruction.
    - the right-column "Seven Practices" list (and its PracticeLink component)
    - components/home/HeroBackdrop.tsx — the hero ground is flat #0A0A0B now
  do_not_resurrect:
    - components/home/HeroShowcase.tsx
    - components/home/HeroAperture.tsx
    - components/home/KineticHeadline.tsx
    - lib/usePointerParallax.ts
    - the .hero-bloom-* / .hero-iris / .hero-shaft-* / .hero-sheen CSS block
    # All deleted. globals.css keeps only .hero-glow-a / .hero-glow-b.

decisions:
  # Things a future session would otherwise re-litigate or silently undo.

  - id: shared-vertical-template
    date: 2026-08-02
    decision: >
      All seven verticals render through one shared VerticalTemplate.
    status: current
    history: >
      Was briefly replaced with seven distinct per-vertical layouts (ritual,
      ledger, rail, trail, stage, mosaic, syllabus) plus six rebuilt pages, on
      request for visual uniqueness. That work was then reverted on request.
      It is NOT lost — see restore.snapshots. Do not rebuild it from scratch;
      lift it from the snapshot.

  - id: home-hero-rebuild
    date: 2026-08-02
    decision: >
      Home hero is an asymmetric editorial composition: gradient backdrop (no
      stock photo), left-aligned headline with GSAP SplitText character
      assembly, a numbered rail of the seven practices, magnetic + shimmer CTAs,
      and a first-load sequence of monogram → split doors → black hole.
    status: current
    why: >
      The earlier centred-headline-over-stock-photo version read as a generic
      template. Explicit standing instruction from the user: when filling a gap
      the spec leaves open, default to the more distinctive choice, not the
      safest one.

  - id: no-stock-photo-hero
    date: 2026-08-02
    decision: >
      The hero must never fall back to a stock photograph. With no video
      present it shows a charcoal → plum gradient with drifting light wells.
    status: current

  - id: contact-field-swap
    date: 2026-08-02
    decision: >
      The consultation/general field sets swap by KEYED REMOUNT, not
      AnimatePresence.
    why: >
      mode="wait" deadlocked and left the wrong inputs mounted — the form
      claimed consultation while still rendering the general `message` field.
      These are form controls; exactly one set must exist at any moment.

  - id: gold-on-light-contrast
    date: 2026-08-03
    decision: >
      A new token --color-smaya-gold-deep (#887023) exists for GOLD TEXT ON A
      LIGHT GROUND. Bright --color-smaya-gold stays for dark grounds and for
      non-text accents (rules, borders, icons, CTA fills).
    status: current
    severity: >
      SYSTEMIC, and only partly fixed. The brand's signature
      "italic gold accent word inside a serif headline" pattern measured
      2.00:1 on ivory — it fails even the 3:1 LARGE-TEXT bar, let alone 4.5:1.
      Anywhere that pattern sits on ivory or white, the accent word is
      effectively illegible.
    measured:
      gold_on_ivory: 2.00   # fails
      gold_on_charcoal: 8.15  # fine — do not change gold on dark
      goldDeep_on_ivory: 4.55
      goldDeep_on_white: 4.79
      derivation: >
        Solved by darkening #D4AF37 along its own hue until it cleared 4.5:1 on
        ivory, keeping it as gold-looking as possible. 64% of original
        brightness. (#AC8E2D would clear only the 3:1 large-text bar.)
    applied_so_far: >
      ONLY the home services headline ("Elevate Every Emotion"), now 4.54:1.
    STILL_UNFIXED: >
      Every other gold-on-light accent word on the site is still at ~2:1 —
      SectionHeading's `accent` prop on light sections, the gold "EXPLORE" /
      "Read More" labels on white cards, gold eyebrows on ivory, and similar.
      Auditing and converting those was NOT in scope for this change. When
      doing it: swap text-smaya-gold -> text-smaya-gold-deep ONLY where the
      ground is light. Do not blanket-replace; gold on charcoal is correct.

  - id: premium-service-cards
    date: 2026-08-03
    decision: >
      The vertical service grid is an editorial hairline layout
      (components/services/ServiceCardGrid.tsx), replacing the boxed tiles.
      Applied to the SHARED VerticalTemplate, so all seven verticals get it —
      consistent by design, per the earlier revert of per-page layouts.
    status: current
    added:
      - components/icons/CelebrationIcons.tsx — 7 bespoke line-art glyphs
        (rings, engagement, sangeeth, haldi, birthday, babyShower, seemantha).
        Drawn because lucide has no seemantha/haldi/sangeeth. 24x24,
        currentColor, fill=none, so they inherit gold and work with the shared
        .icon-draw stroke reveal.
      - VerticalService gained OPTIONAL `artIcon` and `longDesc`. Absent on the
        other six verticals, which keep lucide + short copy. VERIFIED fallback.
      - components/services/VerticalCtaLink.tsx — typed CTA handler.
      - lib/theme.ts — see decisions.theme-constants-scope.
    card_design: >
      `gap-px` over a charcoal/8 background, so the GAPS are the rules and
      neighbouring cards never double-border. Per card: gold hairline that
      scales in from the left on hover, tabular ordinal, bespoke icon, gold wash
      rising from the foot, spring lift (desktop + non-reduced only).
    cta_note: >
      The CTA stays a real <a>. It was NOT converted to a <button> with an
      onClick navigate — that would cost middle-click, cmd-click, open-in-new-tab,
      status-bar URL preview and crawlability. The typed handler runs ALONGSIDE
      navigation and never calls preventDefault. NOTE: next/link itself calls
      preventDefault for client-side routing, so `defaultPrevented` reads true —
      that is next/link, not this handler. Navigation verified to still work.
    accessibility_audit:
      note: >
        Contrast was MEASURED in-browser (composite alpha over the real card
        background, WCAG relative-luminance formula), not eyeballed. Two genuine
        defects were found and fixed in a follow-up pass:
      fixed:
        - Ordinal numeral measured 1.83:1 (needs 4.5) AND was not aria-hidden,
          so screen readers announced "01, 02, 03..." as content. Now
          aria-hidden and at /60 -> 5.74:1. NOTE aria-hidden is NOT a contrast
          exemption; visible text still has to pass, which is why /45 (3.35:1)
          was still not good enough.
        - Icon stroke was gold-on-white at 2.1:1 (needs 3 for a graphic).
          Icons now sit on a tinted chip with a PLUM stroke -> 10.31:1, warming
          to gold on hover only once the chip has darkened behind it.
      measured_after: title 17.13, description 8.52, ordinal 5.74, icon 10.31 — all pass.
      assumption_corrected: >
        The description was ASSUMED to be failing at charcoal/60. Measurement
        showed 5.74:1 — it already passed. It was raised to /70 (8.52) for
        comfort, not to fix a defect. Measure before claiming.
      cards_are_not_links: >
        Deliberate. No ritual has its own page, so linking all seven at /contact
        would give screen-reader users seven identical meaningless links. They
        stay <ul>/<li> with headings. Consequently the hover treatment is purely
        decorative — nothing is conveyed by hover alone, so there is no keyboard
        gap to close. Do not "fix" this by adding tabindex.
      no_images_in_cards: >
        Lazy loading was requested but is N/A — these cards carry SVG icons
        inlined into the RSC payload, no raster images.
    verified: >
      7 cards, all using bespoke icons; all rendering longDesc.
      /services/fitness confirmed on the fallback path (8 cards, 4 columns,
      lucide icons, short desc, coral chip). Row heights uniform (300px x3 rows
      celebrations; 305/275 across fitness' two rows), cards uniform 389px,
      1px hairline gap. CTA emits {vertical,label,destination,surface} and still
      navigates. 375px: single column, 335px cards, 32px padding, 22px title,
      15px/25.5px description, all text fits, zero overflow. Build clean, 21/21.

  - id: theme-constants-scope
    date: 2026-08-03
    decision: >
      lib/theme.ts holds the palette as typed TS constants, but app/globals.css
      (@theme) REMAINS the single source of truth for styling.
    status: current
    why: >
      A request came in to move the colour scheme into theme.ts. Done literally,
      that would have broken things: Tailwind generates bg-smaya-plum /
      text-smaya-gold etc. by STATICALLY SCANNING class strings, 33 files depend
      on those utilities, and routing styling through JS constants would defeat
      the scan and duplicate the palette in two drifting places.
      So theme.ts is scoped to what Tailwind classes genuinely cannot reach:
      canvas 2D fills and computed gradient strings.
    real_win: >
      ParticleField and BlackHoleTransition had hardcoded rgba() literals —
      9 in BlackHoleTransition alone. Those now call alpha('gold', x) etc.
      NOTE BlackHoleTransition has a local numeric `alpha`, so the import is
      aliased: `import { alpha as withAlpha }`.
    drift_guard: >
      assertThemeInSync() runs from SmoothScrollProvider in dev only. It reads
      the computed @theme custom properties and console.warns if any TS constant
      disagrees. Change a colour in one place and the browser tells you.

  - id: styled-components-declined
    date: 2026-08-03
    decision: >
      CSS Modules / styled-components were requested and NOT adopted. The
      project stays on Tailwind v4.
    status: current
    why: >
      styled-components carries a runtime, needs a client-side registry, and
      fights React Server Components — most of this app's pages are server
      components. Mixing it in would fragment styling across two systems and
      cost the static-extraction and RSC benefits. Tailwind already gives
      colocated, scoped styling. If this comes up again, the honest answer is
      that the cost is architectural, not stylistic.

  - id: founder-photo
    date: 2026-08-03
    decision: >
      The About page leadership portrait is the REAL founder photograph:
      public/images/manasa-raj.jpg. It is no longer a stock placeholder.
    status: current
    provenance: >
      Recovered from the original Vite build at
      ..\smaya-events\src\assets\images\manasa_snow_trek_1780387802962.png —
      which despite the .png extension is actually a 1408x768 JPEG. It was
      listed for weeks under open_items as "never supplied"; it had in fact been
      sitting in the source repo the whole time. Re-encoded with mozjpeg q84
      progressive: 910 KB -> 194 KB.
    framing: >
      Landscape 1408x768 source rendered into a 4:5 portrait frame (448x560) via
      object-cover + object-center. Verified arithmetically that the crop keeps
      the full frame height and source x 397..1011 — the subject sits at ~730
      and her raised arms span ~530..980, so BOTH ARMS SURVIVE the crop.
      Do NOT switch to object-top (cuts her legs) or object-bottom (loses the
      summit). If the frame aspect ever changes, redo that check.
    do_not_confuse: >
      The Unsplash URL photo-1551632811-561732d1e306 still appears in
      data/blog.ts, data/events.ts, data/portfolio.ts and data/verticals.ts.
      That is generic trekking stock for those cards — NOT the founder. Only
      components/about/AboutContent.tsx carries the portrait.
    revert_how: >
      Point FOUNDER_IMAGE in components/about/AboutContent.tsx back at the
      Unsplash URL. Nothing else references the local asset.

  - id: card-video-backgrounds
    date: 2026-08-03
    decision: >
      ALL SEVEN tiles in the home services grid are video-backed. Started as
      Celebrations only; the remaining six were added the same day.
    status: current
    revert_how: >
      Remove `backgroundVideo: cardVideo(vertical.slug)` from the SERVICE_CARDS
      map in components/home/ServicesGrid.tsx — every video branch is gated on
      `hasVideo`, so all seven revert to white cards in one edit. For a single
      tile, make cardVideo() return undefined for that slug. Optionally delete
      public/videos/*-card*.
    source_footage: >
      Seven Gemini clips in C:\Users\pavit\Downloads — Generate_a_second_video_fe.mp4
      (celebrations), corporate.mp4, fitness&wellness.mp4, adventure.mp4,
      entertainment.mp4, community.mp4, workshops&retreats.mp4.
      All identical specs: 1280x720 h264, 24fps, 10.005s, ~2.1-2.4 Mbps, with
      an audio track that is stripped at encode.
    asset_naming: >
      Paths are DERIVED, not listed — cardVideo(slug) returns
      /videos/<slug>-card.mp4, -card-mobile.mp4 and -card-poster.jpg. A new
      vertical only needs those three files to appear; no code change.
    encoded_assets: >
      854x480 CRF 34 desktop (309-445 KB each, 2660 KB total), 640x360 CRF 35
      mobile (180-259 KB each, 1552 KB total), posters 26-38 KB (244 KB total).
      All 8s, audio stripped, +faststart.
      NOTE the aggregate: ~2.6 MB if a visitor scrolls the whole grid on
      desktop. Acceptable only because of preload="none" + play-on-visible; do
      not weaken that gating without re-measuring.
    seamless_loop: >
      Every clip is a TRUE seamless loop, not `loop` over a hard cut. Recipe —
      base = source[X..D], head = source[0..X], xfade(base, head,
      offset = D-2X, duration = X), X = 2s. Output starts AND ends on source t=X.
      Verified by extracting frame 0 vs frame N-1 and measuring mean absolute
      pixel difference against the adjacent-frame baseline:
        celebrations 5.98/3.58   corporate 8.50/3.95   fitness 6.63/2.47
        adventure 13.59/7.99     entertainment 13.85/7.33
        community 6.27/3.34      workshops 4.83/2.21
      For scale, a NON-seamless cut measured 54.86 against a control of 55.17.
      A hand-rolled blend/concat attempt was tried first and was WRONG — it put
      the fade at the start instead of bridging the wrap and scored no better
      than the control. Use xfade.
    regenerate: >
      scripts/gen-card-videos.mjs — probes duration, builds the seamless loop,
      emits mobile + poster, and measures every seam. Edit its JOBS array to
      point at new source files, then: node scripts/gen-card-videos.mjs <tmpdir>
      It resolves `sharp` via createRequire against the project package.json.
    critical_notes:
      - Playback binds to a CONTINUOUS observer (useIsVisible), not the latching
        useNearViewport used for the Lottie fetch. Clips start when a card
        scrolls in and PAUSE when it leaves — with seven of them that gating is
        what keeps the page viable, not a nicety.
      - preload="none" means nothing downloads until a card is actually reached.
      - The poster <Image> sits underneath permanently. Reduced motion,
        saveData, 2g/slow-2g, a refused codec or a non-firing observer all leave
        a static poster tile — never an empty black card.
      - Card copy INVERTS on a video tile: white title, white/80 description,
        translucent glass badge, brighter ordinal. Scrim is bottom-weighted
        (charcoal -> charcoal/75 -> charcoal/25); the footage is busy and often
        bright, and a flat tint was not enough.
      - Tiles are min-h-[19rem]; with all seven video-backed every row is now a
        uniform 304px.
      - The Lottie badges still run on top of the footage, as do Adventure's
        GSAP zoom and Entertainment's DJ lights.
    verified: >
      7/7 tiles carry video, each correctly matched to its slug. All playing,
      readyState 4, and ALL SEVEN observed to LOOP (currentTime wrapped on every
      one). All titles white; rows uniform 304px. At 375px: single column,
      335x304 tiles, all seven correctly selecting the mobile cut, titles fit,
      no overflow. Build clean, 21/21 static.
    NOT_verified: >
      Pause-on-scroll-away — IntersectionObserver does not fire in the AI
      session's non-compositing browser, so `visible` never toggles; play() was
      driven manually instead. Also unmeasured: real on-screen text contrast
      over moving footage, and the CPU/battery cost of up to three simultaneous
      decodes on a low-end device.

