  - id: home-hero-showcase
    date: 2026-08-03
    decision: >
      The home hero cycles the SEVEN practice hero clips the site already owns,
      with the right-hand practice rail acting as a live index of what is
      playing. components/home/HeroBackdrop.tsx is deleted.
    status: SUPERSEDED by home-hero-typographic (2026-08-04). Kept for the
      lessons below (one-video-at-a-time, caption desync, type weight), which
      still apply anywhere else video is cycled.
    why: >
      The hero was rejected as not premium. The root cause was not styling: it
      had NO IMAGERY. /videos/hero-loop.mp4 was never supplied, so it sat on a
      flat plum gradient while every service page had cinematic footage. Rather
      than commission an eighth clip, it now shows the range of work using
      existing assets — and the rail, previously decorative, became functional.
    behaviour: >
      Advances every 6.2s. Hovering or focusing a rail item jumps to that
      practice and PAUSES the auto-advance (the visitor is now driving).
      Cycling stops entirely when the hero leaves the viewport. Caption and the
      seven-tick progress bar track the active clip.
    ONE_VIDEO_AT_A_TIME: >
      The <video> is deliberately OUTSIDE the AnimatePresence that crossfades
      the posters. First implementation had it inside, and because
      AnimatePresence keeps exiting children mounted, FOUR clips were live and
      decoding simultaneously. Posters crossfade; a single video element
      remounts on `src`. Verified max 1 across 8 samples.
    CAPTION_DESYNC_BUG: >
      The rail caption used AnimatePresence mode="wait". When an exit stalls,
      the old caption stays on screen while different footage plays — it
      silently lies about what you are watching. Replaced with a keyed remount.
      This is the THIRD time mode="wait" has deadlocked on this project (see
      contact-field-swap). Prefer a keyed remount whenever correctness of the
      displayed content matters.
    type_fix: >
      The accent word was font-light against a font-black headline, which read
      as two typefaces colliding. Both are now weight 900; only the italic
      distinguishes the accent.
    payload_note: >
      Each advance fetches one clip. A visitor watching all seven pulls roughly
      6 MB desktop / 3.6 MB mobile. Mitigated by: mobile cuts on small screens
      and 3g, no cycling off-screen, pause on interaction, and skipping video
      entirely under saveData / 2g / reduced motion. If the hero ever autoplays
      on a page that also carries the card grid, re-measure.
    verified: >
      Clip, rail marker and caption stay in lockstep across six advances
      (adventure -> entertainment -> community -> workshops). One video element
      throughout. Desktop h1 96px; mobile 41.6px over 4 lines, fits, rail hidden,
      both CTAs 56px, headline contrast 16.12 over the scrim. Build clean, 21/21.
    NOT_verified: >
      The crossfade, the Ken Burns scale and the parallax have not been seen —
      rAF is suspended in this browser. Poster <img> elements accumulated to 6
      for the same reason (stalled exits); in a working browser that is 2.

  - id: home-hero-typographic
    date: 2026-08-04
    decision: >
      The home hero has NO VIDEO AND NO PHOTOGRAPH. It is type-led: an
      oversized SplitText headline over a purely CSS ground, with the four
      headline stats as proof and the seven practices as a static index rail.
      components/home/HeroShowcase.tsx is deleted.
      components/home/HeroBackdrop.tsx is REINSTATED (rewritten, not restored)
      as that CSS ground.
    status: current
    why: >
      Direct instruction: "remove that cinimatic clip for the hero section
      background. wihtout any video at the background, refine the hero
      section". The two previous heroes failed at opposite ends — a flat
      gradient read as empty, seven cycling clips read as busy. Do not
      reintroduce a hero video without being asked; this is the third attempt
      at this section.
    composition: >
      Eyebrow with gold rule -> SplitText h1 (variant="assemble", both halves
      weight 900, only the italic + gold separate the accent) -> a 12-col split
      where the left column carries the sub-paragraph and both CTAs and the
      right column carries a <dl> of the four stats -> a full-width practice
      rail with numbered links to all seven verticals -> scroll cue and
      location/positioning line. StrokeText "Celebrate." sits behind the
      headline at z-[1] as a depth layer.
    backdrop_layers: >
      HeroBackdrop is six stacked CSS layers, zero network requests: a 150deg
      charcoal->plum base, two off-centre blurred blooms (plum upper-left as
      the light source, gold lower-right), a 12-col hairline grid at 6%
      matching the content rhythm, a single gold horizon rule, an inline-SVG
      feTurbulence grain at 16% mix-blend-overlay, and a vignette. Plus
      ParticleField, which self-disables under reduced motion.
      The grain is not decoration — a gradient that large bands visibly on wide
      panels without it.
    BUTTON_WIDTH_TRAP: >
      Button/ButtonLink wrap the control in an `inline-block` motion.div, so
      `className="w-full"` resolves to 100% of a SHRINK-WRAPPED box and does
      nothing — the CTA measured 274px inside a 335px column. Fixed by adding a
      `wrapperClassName` prop to both. Any full-width button anywhere on the
      site must use it. This is additive; no existing call site changed.
    mobile_fit: >
      Four stats plus the rail pushed the hero ~134px past the fold on a 812px
      phone, putting the scroll cue below the thing it cues. Stats 3 and 4 are
      `hidden sm:block` and the vertical rhythm is tightened below sm. Now
      exactly 812 on a 812 viewport, rail bottom at 776.
    contrast: >
      Muted whites were raised from /40 and /45 to /55 — at 10px those are
      small text and measured ~3.6:1 and ~4.2:1 over the plum ground, under
      AA. /55 computes 5.5-6.1:1. Rail ordinals went gold/55 -> gold/70.
      Body copy stays white/65 (~7:1). Headline is pure white (~16:1).
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean (21/21). Home first-load JS 194 kB.
      Hero contains 0 <video>, 0 <img>, 1 <canvas> (ParticleField).
      Desktop 1440x820: section 820, h1 120px over 2 lines, rail bottom 772.
      Mobile 375x812: section 812, h1 44px, CTA full-width 335px, no x-overflow.
    NOT_verified: >
      No entrance animation has been seen. rAF never fires in this browser
      (confirmed directly — a rAF chain timed out at 30s with 0 frames), so
      every framer-motion element sits at its initial opacity 0. Geometry above
      was measured with opacity/transform force-overridden via injected CSS.
    revert_how: >
      Restore components/home/HeroShowcase.tsx from the transcript and
      re-point components/home/Hero.tsx at it, or drop back to the
      pre-showcase HeroBackdrop. Note the current HeroBackdrop is a NEW file
      with the same name, not the old one.

  - id: blueprint-replaces-practice-list
    date: 2026-08-04
    decision: >
      The seven-item practice list is GONE from the hero. The blueprint moved
      out of the full-bleed background and into the hero's right column in its
      place. HeroBackdrop.tsx is DELETED and the hero ground is now flat
      #0A0A0B. The .hero-glow-* CSS went with it.
    status: current
    supersedes: event-blueprint-layer (same day)
    why: >
      "delete this text list completely and replace it with a dedicated GSAP
      scroll-scrubbed SVG animation" + "background remains solid obsidian
      (#0A0A0B) with no background noise".
    NO_MORE_ATMOSPHERE_LAYER: >
      HeroBackdrop carried the gradient blooms, the conic sweep, the hairline
      grid, the feTurbulence grain and the ParticleField. "Solid obsidian, no
      background noise" retires all of it. The hero is now one flat colour plus
      the blueprint. Do not reintroduce a texture layer here without being asked
      — it has been explicitly removed once.
    COLUMN_STAYS_AT_4_NOT_5: >
      The brief said "col-span-5 (or equivalent)". It is col-span-4, because the
      headline's `lg:text-[clamp(3.5rem,5.9vw,6rem)]` slope was measured against
      an EIGHT-column track. At 7/5 "Creating Moments," wraps inside its mask at
      1440px and the hero grows past the fold. Widening the canvas means
      re-deriving that clamp — see conventions and hero-asymmetric-spec.
    CSS_HIDING_DOES_NOT_GATE_AN_EFFECT: >
      The canvas is `hidden lg:block`, and the first pass relied on that alone
      while matchMedia branched only on reduced motion. The element is
      display:none below lg but the effect still ran, so phones were PINNED for
      110% of viewport with nothing on screen. Caught by measuring: mobile had a
      .pin-spacer. The matchMedia query is now
      `(min-width: 1024px) and (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference)`.
      Any future CSS-hidden component that owns a ScrollTrigger has this bug
      waiting in it.
    timeline_needs_a_tail: >
      First pass ended the white->gold tween at timeline position 1.125 — past
      the end — so the last shapes were still mid-colour as the pin released.
      Everything now lands by ~88% and an empty `tl.to({}, {duration: 0.12})`
      holds the finished plan for the rest of the scrub.
    canvas_sizing: >
      The viewBox is portrait (520x660) and `meet` fits by the SHORTER axis, so
      HEIGHT sizes this canvas, not width. At 352px tall the plan drew 277px
      wide inside a 411px column and left a third of it empty; h-[clamp(24rem,
      56vh,40rem)] fills it.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean, 21/21. Home 16.1 kB / 195 kB.
      Practice list absent from the DOM. Section background rgb(10,10,11).
      11 outline elements + 6 assembling shapes, zero zero-length paths,
      3910 user units of real geometry.
      1440x820 scrub, proven by driving scrollTo + a synchronous 'scroll' event:
      dashoffset 2040 -> 963 -> 0 by y=400; last shape rotation 27deg -> 0;
      stroke rgba(255,255,255,0.6) -> rgb(212,175,55) with all six gold by
      y=820, and the pin releasing at y=902. Scrolling back up returned
      IDENTICAL values at the same positions.
      willChange flips transform -> auto once the trigger is out of range.
      1024x768: headline does not wrap, canvas 272x430, section exactly 768.
      375x812: canvas hidden, NO pin-spacer, section relative and exactly 812,
      no horizontal overflow.
    NOT_verified: >
      Frame rate. rAF does not fire in this browser, so smoothness is unproven —
      only that per-frame work is small and compositor-friendly. The
      reduced-motion branch was reasoned, not exercised.
    PIN_SPACING_FIX: >
      Shipped broken and was caught on screen: "SEE AS I SCROLL, WHAT IS
      HAPPENING". The hero was pinned inside a flex <main>, so pinSpacing
      reserved nothing (spacer height 820, padding 0) and ServicesGrid — z-20
      against the hero's z-auto — scrolled up over the fixed hero and cut the
      headline in half mid-glyph.
      Fixed by wrapping the section in a plain block div. Spacer is now
      1722px with 902px of padding-bottom, and the next section's overlap with
      the hero measures 0 at every scroll position sampled.
      Promoted to contracts.pin_needs_a_block_parent.
    revert_how: >
      The practice list markup and its PracticeLink component are in the
      2026-08-04 transcript, as is HeroBackdrop.tsx and the .hero-glow-* CSS.

  - id: weddings-tap-to-reveal-card
    date: 2026-08-10
    what: >
      The Weddings card in the Celebrations "Tailored For You" grid opens on
      click: silhouette centred over the card -> confetti -> artwork flies into
      the icon chip -> title and copy stagger in. Locked open afterwards.
      Files: components/services/ServiceCardGrid.tsx, lib/confetti.ts,
      data/verticals.ts (new optional `revealImage` field), public/image_3.png.
    OPT_IN_VIA_DATA_NOT_A_TITLE_CHECK: >
      `revealImage?: string` on VerticalService. The grid renders seven cards
      from one array; `service.title === 'Weddings'` would break silently the
      moment the copy is retitled. Every other card takes `revealed = true`, so
      none of the reveal-gated animation applies to them.
    HOW_THE_STATE_DRIVES_IT: >
      One boolean. `opened` -> `revealed = !isRevealCard || opened` ->
      `shownAt = revealed ? 'show' : 'hidden'`, which is passed as the `animate`
      prop of the top row, the h3 and the p. Delays 0.06 / 0.18 / 0.30 make the
      stagger.
      THE EXPLICIT `animate` IS MANDATORY. The grid's <motion.ul> propagates its
      "hidden"/"show" variant names down the entire subtree for the scroll
      reveal, so a child with `variants` but no `animate` inherits "show" and
      appears immediately, defeating the whole interaction.
    SHARED_LAYOUT_NOT_HAND_MEASURED_COORDS: >
      One `layoutId` on both the centred span and the chip span. Framer FLIPs
      between them, so neither position is hardcoded. Proven from the frozen
      inline style mid-flight:
        translate3d(130.438px, -30.6px, 0) scale(4.28571, 3.09989)
      240/56 = 4.286 and 174/56 = 3.100 — the exact inverse of the delta.
      Strip framer's inline style and the element rests at 56x56, the chip.
    THREE_TRAPS_HIT_WHILE_BUILDING_THIS:
      - No <AnimatePresence> around the overlay. An exiting element keeps its
        layoutId until exit finishes, so both copies claim the same id and
        framer animates from the wrong one. Plain conditional mount is correct.
      - Hover scale goes on a CHILD of the layout element, never on it. Framer
        measures shared-layout boxes from the DOM; a scale on the measured node
        lands the artwork off-target. The brief's 1.5 -> 1.55 is therefore box
        size + a 3.5% hover bump, not an inline scale.
      - The li's `whileHover={{y:-5}}` is disabled on this card. It is an
        ANCESTOR of the layout element and an ancestor mid-spring corrupts the
        viewport-relative measurement.
      - Width must sit on the BUTTON, not the span inside it. The button is a
        grid item under place-items-center so it shrink-wraps; `w-[74%]` on its
        child resolved against zero and the artwork collapsed to 0x0.
    ASSET_REPLACED_2026_08_10: >
      Owner swapped image_3.png for a different picture. It is NOT a silhouette
      any more — a full-colour mandap scene, 2814x1536, aspect 1.832 (was
      1.382), fully opaque (alpha min=max=255), 8.47 MB.
      Optimised to 960x524 palette PNG, 272 KB — a 96.8% cut, no visible
      banding. Original kept in the scratchpad as image_3.v2.original.png.
      Component changes the new shape forced:
        aspect-[900/651] -> aspect-[960/524]
        chip `object-left` -> `object-center` — the couple now sits at 34-67%
          of the frame, dead centre, where before it hugged the left edge.
          No zoom: the figures run nearly the full height, so scaling in
          decapitates them.
        overlay w-[74%] max-w-[15rem] -> w-[86%] max-w-[17rem]; the frame is
          landscape now so it can be wider without growing the card.
        sizes -> "(min-width: 640px) 272px, 74vw"
    FESTIVE_REVEALED_STATE_2026_08_10: >
      Post-click state themed: garland across the top edge, groom-and-family
      bottom-left, bride-and-family bottom-right, and a frosted panel behind the
      copy. Owner-specified z-order honoured exactly — decorations z-0, glass
      z-10, copy z-20 — and the pre-reveal silhouette overlay moved z-10 -> z-30
      so it still sits above all of it.
  - id: vivid-per-card-palettes-2026-08-11
    date: 2026-08-11
    what: >
      The unopened card's wash went from one faint neutral gradient to SEVEN
      saturated per-card palettes, each with a matching coloured glow, and the
      carousel's ambient wash was pushed from 0.3 to 0.6 with saturate(2).
    CSS_STRINGS_IN_DATA_NOT_TAILWIND_CLASSES: >
      The brief specified `bg-gradient-to-br from-amber-200 ...` per card.
      Written into verticals.ts those would never be emitted — Tailwind's
      scanner never sees a value that lives in a data file. Stored as resolved
      hex gradients and applied via inline style. Same rule that bit the
      reserve percentages and the decor max-height.
    THE_GLOW_NEEDS_A_DARK_SHADOW_UNDER_IT: >
      A coloured box-shadow alone reads as a smudge — it has no edge. The
      neutral depth shadow stays underneath and the tint sits on top:
        0 18px 50px -20px rgba(28,27,31,0.35), 0 14px 44px -6px <glow>
      The shadow UTILITY had to come off the className too, or it and the inline
      style fight over the same property.
    glow_drops_away_on_reveal: >
      The opened card is a scene with its own ground; a saturated halo around it
      competed with the artwork. Revealed keeps only the neutral shadow.
    SATURATE_BEFORE_BLUR_OR_NOTHING_SURVIVES: >
      The ambient layer is blurred 100px, which averages the artwork down to a
      near-grey wash. Opacity alone would have made a grey haze more visible,
      not a colourful one — `saturate-200` ahead of the blur is what makes the
      colour read. Verified computed filter: blur(100px) saturate(2).
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 15.8 kB.
      All seven carry their own gradient AND their own glow, confirmed from
      computed style: Weddings amber/rose/orange + amber glow, Engagements
      pink/fuchsia/violet, Sangeeth fuchsia/purple/indigo, Haldi
      yellow/amber/orange, Birthdays pink/sky/amber, Baby Showers sky/pink/amber
      with a sky glow to separate it from Birthdays, Seemantha orange/rose/amber.
      Ambient wrapper opacity 0.6.
    MEASURING_THE_UNOPENED_STATE_NEEDS_AN_EARLY_SAMPLE: >
      The first slide auto-reveals 2.5s after hydration, which is inside a
      navigate + evaluate round trip — so reading it directly always shows the
      REVEALED state and looks like the gradient failed. Either read a
      neighbour, which is never spotlit, or sample through an iframe within the
      first second. Weddings confirmed at 735ms: gradient present, glow true.

  - id: compact-430-gradient-2500ms-2026-08-11
    date: 2026-08-11
    what: >
      Cards 500 -> 430 (400 mobile), a warm gradient on the unopened state, and
      the auto-reveal cut from 5s to 2.5s.
    A_CONSTANT_TAILWIND_CANNOT_READ_IS_A_TRAP: >
      Dropped DECOR_MAX_H_PCT 32 -> 30 and the decoration still measured 32%.
      The real clamp is the LITERAL `max-h-[32%]` in the className — Tailwind
      cannot read a JS constant, so the constant was pure decoration and the two
      had silently diverged. Both must be edited together; noted at the call
      site. Same class of bug as putting a Tailwind class in data.
    REBALANCING_THE_BANDS_WAS_MANDATORY_NOT_COSMETIC: >
      At 430px the old 32/39/27 split gave the copy a 168px band against a panel
      that measures ~185, so the copy pushed into the decoration (up to 14px)
      and the corner art on four of seven cards. Now 30 / 44 / 25 with corner
      art back to 24%, plus the body copy trimmed to 14px/1.6 at sm.
      Measured after: every gap POSITIVE on all seven —
        1280 (380x430): decor->panel 3-47px, panel->corner 3-14px, zero overlap
        375  (320x400): decor->panel 11-50px, panel->corner 11-20px, zero overlap
      3px is thin. Anything that lengthens the copy or deepens a decoration will
      collide first on Engagements or Sangeeth; re-measure both gaps.
    the_gradient_must_be_cleared_on_reveal: >
      `backgroundImage` paints OVER `backgroundColor`, so the unopened gradient
      has to be set back to `none` when the card opens — otherwise it tints the
      cream the decorations are colour-matched to and puts their edges back.
      Verified both states: unopened neighbour carries the 4-stop gradient,
      spotlit card settles to rgb(248,245,238).
    WHY_THE_DEAD_SPACE_CANNOT_FULLY_GO: >
      A 16:9 image in a 380x430 portrait card tops out at ~50% of the card's
      height even at full bleed; at a sensible 92% width it is 41%. The blank
      band above and below is geometry, not padding — which is exactly why the
      fix for "looks plain and white" is the gradient, not a bigger image.
    TIMING_THE_TIMER_NEEDS_A_HYDRATED_PAGE: >
      Measured from page load the reveal looked like ~5s even after the change,
      because "carousel present in the DOM" is not "effect armed" — hydration
      sits in between and dominates. Timing from a SLIDE CHANGE on an
      already-hydrated page isolates it: 2594ms against a 2500 target.
      Also: pick a card that has not already been opened, or it reads 66ms.

  - id: decor-gap-and-ordinal-2026-08-11
    date: 2026-08-11
    reported: gap above the drapes, "05" still showing, bottom trough, garland not reaching the card edge.
    TWO_SEPARATE_CAUSES_LOOKED_LIKE_ONE: >
      Both the top gap AND the garland pulling off the edges read as "the decor
      does not cover the top", but they were unrelated.

      1. THE ASSETS CARRIED EMPTY GROUND. Measured the first decorated row of
         every decoration: birthday 16.5% of the file was blank above the
         drapes, engagement 7.6%, garland 3.7%; the other four were 0. That
         blank band rendered as a bare strip between the card's top edge and the
         artwork. Trimmed at the FILE, and the hardcoded aspect ratios in
         verticals.ts updated to match — 537->513, 447->409, 383->316.
         WHEN A DECORATION SITS LOW, MEASURE THE ASSET BEFORE TOUCHING CSS.

      2. `inset-x-0` + `aspect-ratio` + `max-h` SHRANK THE WIDTH. With left AND
         right pinned, aspect-ratio treats neither axis as definite, so once
         max-h clamped the height the box narrowed to preserve the ratio —
         Sangeeth's garland came in 58px narrower than the card and pulled away
         from both edges. `left-0 w-full` makes the width definite, so the ratio
         only ever computes the height and the clamp crops vertically instead.
    the_bottom_trough: >
      Corner art 24% -> 26% of card height, COPY_BOTTOM 27 -> 28. At 24 the two
      pieces spanned only ~77% of the card on Seemantha and left a visible empty
      channel between them. Now 80-92% on the landscape-art cards.
      Weddings and Birthdays still span ~50% — their corner art is PORTRAIT, so
      a middle gap there is the artwork's shape, not the layout's, and closing
      it would mean cropping people.
    ordinal_gone: header 'ordinal' removed from Birthdays. Zero ordinals across all seven.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 15.7 kB.
      1280 (500x380) and 375 (460x320), all seven:
        decoration flush to the top edge AND full card width on every card
        decoration <= 32% of card height
        corner heights identical within each card, flush to the bottom
        panel inside the card, zero overlap with decoration or corner art
        panel-to-corner gap 5-18px desktop, 18px+ mobile
        zero ordinals, no horizontal scroll

  - id: compact-card-500px-2026-08-11
    date: 2026-08-11
    what: 640px cards -> 460 / md:500, 320 / md:380 wide. Nothing clipped, nothing overlapping.
    THE_ROOT_CAUSE_WAS_THE_UNIT_NOT_THE_NUMBER: >
      The old layout reserved the decoration and corner bands with PERCENTAGE
      PADDING, and CSS resolves percentage padding against WIDTH. So both bands
      were sized by one axis while the card was constrained on the other, and
      the copy sat in normal flow underneath them — a long paragraph simply
      pushed the box taller and NO `h-` could have held it. That is why it had
      grown to 640.
      Every band is now a share of the card's HEIGHT and the copy is absolutely
      positioned between two of them:
        32% decoration (max-h) | 33%->73% copy | 27% corner art
      The three add to a known total, so the card cannot be pushed past it.
    max_h_ON_THE_DECOR_CROPS_THE_RIGHT_WAY: >
      The box keeps the art's own aspectRatio and `max-h-[32%]` clamps it.
      Because the clamp only ever makes the box SHORTER than its natural ratio,
      object-cover crops it VERTICALLY — trimming the bottom, which the fade
      mask is already dissolving — and never horizontally, so the side drapes
      survive. A box TALLER than the natural ratio would have cropped inwards
      and cut them off. Direction matters here, not just the cap.
    CORNERS_ARE_NOW_HEIGHT_DRIVEN_AND_THAT_DELETED_A_WHOLE_CLASS_OF_BUG: >
      `height: 24%` + `aspectRatio`, width falls out. Both pieces get the same
      height so they render at one scale automatically — the thing four separate
      turns were spent hand-tuning pairs of widths to achieve, because equal
      WIDTHS give unequal heights whenever the two crops are different shapes.
      `widthPct` in the data is now unread; left in the type rather than editing
      twelve entries, and flagged to the owner as dead config to remove.
    MOBILE_NEEDED_SMALLER_TYPE_NOT_A_TALLER_CARD: >
      At 320px wide the same paragraph wraps to five lines instead of four:
      measured, the panel wanted 230px inside a 184px band and pushed down into
      the corner art on five of the seven cards. Stepping the copy down to
      13px/1.5 and the heading to 1.375rem at the narrow end closes it with the
      460px height intact.
    A_MEASUREMENT_TRAP_THAT_ALMOST_FOOLED_ME: >
      getBoundingClientRect on a carousel slide includes the slide's 0.84 scale
      transform, and the scale only returns to 1 via a framer animation that
      does not run in this pane. Card heights read 388 instead of 460 and every
      derived number was 16% low. offsetWidth/offsetHeight are unaffected by
      transforms — use those for sizes. Rect-vs-rect OVERLAP tests stay valid
      because both boxes carry the same scale.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 15.6 kB.
      1280: all seven 500x380, decoration <= 32% of height, corner heights
      identical within every card, panel inside the card, ZERO overlap with
      decoration or corner art, all art inside the card, carousel track 500.
      375: all seven 460x320, same, zero overlap, no horizontal scroll.
      Section is 1011px tall including its heading and padding, so the card sits
      inside a laptop viewport.

  - id: master-template-enforced-in-code-2026-08-11
    date: 2026-08-11
    THE_PREMISE_WAS_HALF_WRONG_AND_THE_HALF_THAT_WAS_RIGHT_MATTERED: >
      Owner asked to refactor "all 7 card components" onto one master template.
      There is only ONE component — ServiceCard renders all seven from data —
      and they were already identical in SIZE (640x416, zero overflow, verified
      the turn before).
      What HAD drifted was the layout PARAMETERS in data:
        panel 55% on four cards, 70% pinned LEFT on Engagements, and none at all
        on Weddings, which used a radial wash at full width;
        seven hand-tuned reservePct values, 34 to 51;
        corner widths 28-45%, two of them over any sane cap;
        Weddings' decoration a cropped ~96px strip while the other six rendered
        theirs full width at natural ratio.
      That is why the set looked like different templates despite one component.
    THE_FIX_IS_TO_DELETE_THE_KNOBS_NOT_TO_SET_THEM_EQUAL: >
      Setting seven data entries to the same values leaves seven chances to
      diverge again. The standard now lives in the COMPONENT as constants and
      nothing per-card can override it:
        PANEL_PCT = 85            (of the content box; measures 78% of the card)
        CORNER_MAX_PCT = 40       applied as Math.min(widthPct, 40)
        CORNER_CLEARANCE_PCT = 6
      `panelPct`, `panelAlign` and `reservePct` were REMOVED from ServiceReveal
      entirely. The bottom reserve is now DERIVED —
      max(cornerHeight) + clearance — so it can never fall out of step with the
      art the way seven hand-tuned numbers did.
      What stays per-card is only what genuinely differs: which files, their
      aspect ratios, and the relative width of the two corner pieces.
    WHY_THE_BRIEFS_OWN_NUMBERS_WERE_NOT_ALL_TAKEN: >
      - "mt-28" for the copy: not used. The card's top padding already reserves
        the decoration's exact height, derived from its aspect. A fixed 112px
        margin on top of that would double-count and would not adapt to seven
        decorations of different depths.
      - "Weddings card standard": Weddings had NO frosted panel — it used a
        radial wash. The brief also demands a frosted panel on every card, so
        "the Weddings standard" and the brief's own spec contradicted each
        other. Took the brief's spec and moved Weddings onto it.
      - "max-w-[40%]" corners: applied as a CAP, not a fixed value. Within each
        card the two pieces stay proportional to their aspects so they render at
        equal heights — a hard-won property from earlier turns that a flat 40%
        would have destroyed.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 15.8 kB unchanged.
      All seven, at 1280 AND 375:
        height 640, identical; width 416 / 323, identical
        ground: frosted panel on all seven (no wash left)
        panel width: ONE value, 78% of the card, on all seven
        panel centred on all seven
        decoration full-width and top-aligned on all seven
        max corner 40% — the cap binds on Engagements, Birthdays, Baby Showers
        zero content overflow, zero panel/decoration overlap, zero
        panel/corner overlap, all corner art inside the card
        no horizontal scroll
    still_the_one_outlier: >
      Birthdays keeps `header: 'ordinal'` — the only card showing a number.
      Left in place because it was an explicit request two turns ago and this
      brief is about dimensions, but with the set now genuinely uniform it is
      the single remaining thing that looks unintentional.

  - id: uniform-card-height-2026-08-11
    date: 2026-08-11
    what: >
      All seven carousel cards locked to one size. Three classes on the card
      root when `standalone`, one on the decoration wrapper, one padding change.
      No new component and no per-card work — ServiceCard renders all seven, so
      a single rule covers them.
    THE_LOCKED_HEIGHT_IS_MEASURED_NOT_CHOSEN: >
      Natural heights ran 455-624px at a 416px slide and 426-614px at 323px —
      a 169px spread, which is what made the track lurch between slides.
      640 clears the tallest, Sangeeth at 624, by 16px.
      A card's height is its reserves (a share of WIDTH, so they SHRINK on a
      phone) plus however many lines its copy wraps to (which GROWS on a phone).
      The two partly cancel, which is why one value serves every breakpoint
      rather than needing a responsive pair.
      THE BRIEF'S 550 WOULD HAVE CLIPPED FIVE OF THE SEVEN — Sangeeth by 74px,
      Haldi 66, Baby Showers 41, Birthdays 23, Engagements 9.
    MEASURE_offsetHeight_NOT_getBoundingClientRect: >
      First pass read getBoundingClientRect and produced nonsense — inactive
      slides carry scale 0.84, and the rect includes it, so five of the seven
      came back 16% short. offsetHeight is layout height and ignores transforms.
      Any measurement inside this carousel must use offset*, not rects.
    width_stays_on_the_slide: >
      `w-full`, not a fixed width. Every slide already carries identical width
      classes so the width is uniform by construction, and the carousel measures
      the SLIDE to place its neighbours — a second value on the card could only
      disagree with it.
    CONFETTI_CANNOT_BE_CLIPPED_BY_THE_CARD: >
      The brief asked to keep overflow-hidden off the outermost wrapper so the
      burst can escape. It is kept ON, deliberately: canvas-confetti draws to a
      FIXED, full-viewport canvas appended to <body>, outside the card's subtree
      entirely, so no ancestor overflow can touch it — verified repeatedly, and
      again here with bodyCanvases readings during a reveal. The card's
      overflow-hidden is what rounds its corners and guards the locked box, so
      removing it would cost something real to fix nothing.
      The decoration wrapper also got its own `overflow-hidden rounded-2xl` as
      asked; belt and braces, and it is the layer that actually needs it.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 15.8 kB unchanged.
      All seven cards exactly 640px at 375, 1024 and 1280 — allSameHeight true
      at each. Zero content overflow (scrollHeight === clientHeight) and zero
      child spill on every card at every width.
      TRACK HEIGHT NOW CONSTANT: one distinct value, 640, across all seven
      slides — previously it animated between 455 and 624 on every move.
      Sangeeth revealed (the tallest content, worst case): 3 decorations
      mounted, all inside the card, heading/copy/panel all inside, 162px from
      the copy's bottom to the card's, decoration wrapper z-20 overflow hidden
      radius 16px, no canvas left on body.
    THE_HEADROOM_IS_16px: >
      Anything that lengthens a card's copy or deepens its top decoration pushes
      past 640 and gets clipped silently — the card has overflow-hidden. Re-measure
      offsetHeight across all seven before touching either.

  - id: carousel-advanced-interactions-2026-08-11
    date: 2026-08-11
    what: >
      Four additions to FeatureCarousel: wheel navigation with a 500ms lock, a
      5s auto-reveal for the spotlit card, an ambient blurred wash of the active
      card's artwork, and hover lift on the active slide only.
    LENIS_MEANS_preventDefault_DOES_NOTHING: >
      THE FINDING OF THIS TURN. The site runs Lenis smooth scroll, which
      registers its own wheel listener on `window` and moves the page
      PROGRAMMATICALLY — it never consults defaultPrevented. Measured: a
      synthetic wheel dispatched ANYWHERE, carousel or not, already comes back
      prevented. So `e.preventDefault()` alone would have let the page glide on
      underneath while the carousel advanced, and the "prevent page scroll"
      requirement would have silently not worked.
      `e.stopPropagation()` is what actually holds the page: the carousel's
      listener is on the stage, Lenis's is on window, so bubbling reaches the
      carousel first and the event never gets there. preventDefault stays for
      the native fallback when Lenis is off.
      HOW TO TEST THIS PROPERLY: dispatchEvent's return value is useless here
      because Lenis prevents everything. Attach a spy listener on `window` and
      count whether the event reaches it.
        outside carousel -> reached window: 1
        mid-list         -> reached window: 0, slide advanced
        at last slide    -> reached window: 1  (page released)
    NEVER_TRAP_THE_PAGE: >
      At the first slide scrolling up, or the last scrolling down, the handler
      returns before either call, so the gesture falls through to Lenis and the
      reader scrolls out of the section. Without that release a reader who
      scrolls in can never scroll out — the standard failure of scroll-jacked
      carousels. Verified by the spy above.
    WHEEL_MUST_BE_A_NATIVE_NON_PASSIVE_LISTENER: >
      React attaches wheel at the root as PASSIVE, so preventDefault inside an
      `onWheel` prop is ignored outright. Registered by hand with
      { passive: false }.
    the_500ms_lock_lives_in_a_REF: >
      A local `let` inside the effect resets every time the effect re-runs, and
      the effect re-runs whenever the deps change — the throttle would evaporate.
      Verified: 8 rapid wheel events produced exactly ONE advance.
    AUTO_REVEAL_TIMER_BELONGS_IN_THE_CARD: >
      The carousel only says which slide is centred (`spotlight`); the card owns
      `opened`, so it owns the timer. All three clearing rules then fall out of
      the dependency list instead of being written by hand:
        navigate away -> spotlight false -> cleanup clears it
        opened first  -> effect bails before arming
        unmount       -> cleanup
      `handleReveal` is read through a REF: depending on the function itself
      would restart the five seconds on every parent render and it might never
      fire.
      Proven with the iframe probe: decorations appeared 5702ms after the
      carousel was ready (5s + hydration lag), confetti canvas appeared on
      <body> at the same moment and was torn down by 8854ms.
      Clear-on-navigate proven separately: advanced at 2557ms, Weddings NEVER
      revealed, Engagements revealed 5104ms after the advance.
    AMBIENT_LAYER_CANNOT_BE_z_MINUS_1: >
      The brief asked for z-[-1]. That would put it behind the ANCESTOR
      SECTION's background, which is opaque white with its own stacking context
      — the wash would be painted over and invisible. It sits at z-0 with the
      track at z-10 above it instead.
      `sizes="320px"` on purpose: it is blurred to 100px and never read, so a
      full-width fetch would be pure waste.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 15.4 -> 15.8 kB.
      1280: ambient layer z-0 overflow-hidden, image blur(100px) scale 1.5,
      track z-10, slides z 100/90, pe auto/none, inert false/true.
      Wheel: one advance from eight events; full walk 1->7 and back.
      375: scrollWidth 375, active card inside the stage, 7 dots, 12 tap
      targets >= 40px.
    NOT_verified: >
      Every animation — hover lift, the ambient crossfade, the slide spring.
      rAF is frozen here, so framer never applies `animate` or `whileHover`.
      Symptom to expect in this pane only: all seven ambient images accumulate
      at once, because AnimatePresence exits never complete. In a browser each
      unmounts 0.8s after its crossfade.

  - id: feature-carousel-2026-08-11
    date: 2026-08-11
    what: >
      The Celebrations services grid became a centre-focused carousel.
      New: components/ui/FeatureCarousel.tsx. ServiceCardGrid now branches on a
      `layout` prop; ServiceCard is UNCHANGED apart from an opt-in `standalone`
      flag for its own radius and shadow.
    OPT_IN_BECAUSE_THE_GRID_IS_SHARED: >
      ServiceCardGrid renders for SIX verticals, not just Celebrations. Swapping
      it wholesale would have turned corporate, fitness, adventure,
      entertainment and community into carousels too. `grid.layout?:
      'grid' | 'carousel'` on the vertical data, set only on celebrations.
      Regression-checked: /services/corporate still renders ul.grid, 3 columns,
      6 cards, no carousel.
    THE_THREE_STACKING_RULES_AND_WHAT_THEY_ACTUALLY_NEEDED:
      - "Rule 1 (no clipping): the track is overflow-visible and stated as such.
        But the premise needs correcting — the confetti CANNOT be clipped by any
        ancestor, because canvas-confetti draws to a fixed full-viewport canvas
        appended to <body>. And the cards' own overflow-hidden must STAY: the
        corner art is anchored to the card's edges and is meant to be trimmed by
        them. The only thing that ever needed protecting was the neighbours'
        ability to spill, and the active card's shadow."
      - "Rule 2 (z-index): active slide z-100, neighbours z-90 — a gap of 10 per
        step, not 1, so a card's own internal bands (decor z-20, copy z-30,
        artwork z-50) can never reach a neighbouring slide's range."
      - "Rule 3 (pointer events): inactive slides get pointer-events-none AND
        `inert`. pointer-events alone leaves them in the tab order, so a
        keyboard user could still open an off-centre card."
    OVERFLOW_X_CLIP_ON_THE_STAGE_IS_NOT_A_VIOLATION: >
      Without it the peeking neighbour ran past the viewport on a phone —
      measured documentElement.scrollWidth 511 against a 375px section. On a
      375px screen a 323px active card plus a real peek cannot both fit; the
      neighbour is the right thing to trim.
      `clip`, NOT `hidden`: hidden forces the other axis to `auto`, making the
      stage a scroll container that would trap page scroll and cut the card
      vertically. `clip` leaves the vertical axis alone. Verified overflowY
      stays `visible` and scrollWidth returns to 375.
    MEASURED_STEP_AND_HEIGHT_NOT_ASSUMED: >
      Slides are absolutely positioned so they can overlap, which means the
      track has no intrinsic height and the step cannot be constant — the slide
      width is responsive and the seven cards are different heights, and they
      grow again when opened. A ResizeObserver on the ACTIVE slide drives both.
      Verified the track height tracks the card exactly: 616/616 at 1280,
      426/426 at 375.
    mx_auto_not_translate_x_1_2: >
      Slides centre with `mx-auto` and offset with framer's `x`. Tailwind's
      `-translate-x-1/2` compiles to the discrete `translate:` property, which
      COMPOSES with framer's transform rather than replacing it — every slide
      would have sat half a width off. Same trap as the hero deck.
    first_positioning_must_not_animate: >
      `step` is 0 until measured, so the first render stacks every slide at
      x: 0 and the measured render moves them out — framer would treat that as a
      transition and the carousel would deal itself open on every page load. A
      ref flipped in an effect AFTER the render that reads it makes that one
      change instant and lets every later change spring.
    drag_must_not_fire_the_card: >
      The cards open on click and the burst is not undoable, so a swipe starting
      on one would open it. A capture-phase click handler on the track swallows
      the click when a drag just ended.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 14 -> 15.4 kB.
      1280, active = Haldi (index 3): 3 slides mounted, active 416px centred,
      neighbours 349px, symmetric 208px peeks, ALL INSIDE the white section,
      z 90/100/90, inert true/false/true, track 616 = card 616.
      Reveal still works inside a slide: 3 decorations mounted at z-20, all
      inside the card, panel present, confetti chunk requested, no canvas left
      on <body>.
      375: scrollWidth 375 (was 511), active card fully visible, 7 dots,
      12 tap targets >= 40px.
      /services/corporate unchanged.
    NOT_verified: >
      The motion itself — rAF is frozen in this pane, so framer's `animate`
      never applies and every slide reads x: 0. All geometry above was proven by
      applying the same transform by hand and measuring. The drag gesture and
      the spring cannot be exercised here.

  - id: seemantha-reveal-2026-08-11
    date: 2026-08-11
    what: >
      Seventh and final reveal card. ALL SEVEN service cards in the Celebrations
      grid now carry the treatment; no static card is left.
      One new capability: a per-card `ground`.
    GROUND_IS_NOW_PER_CARD: >
      The opened card's background was hardcoded `bg-[#f8f5ee]`, sampled from
      the Weddings artwork back when it was the only reveal card. Seemantha's
      three decorations are drawn on PURE WHITE (#fefefe-#ffffff sampled on all
      three), so on cream every one of them would have shown a hard rectangle
      edge. `ground?: string` on the config, defaulting to the old cream, and
      the colour moved from a Tailwind class to the inline style that already
      carries the reserves.
      Verified both branches: Seemantha settles to rgb(255,255,255), Weddings
      still rgb(248,245,238).
    THE_BRIEFS_30_45_WAS_BACKWARDS_HERE: >
      Used 38 / 28 — the inverse — because the two crops are opposite shapes:
      the sweets are WIDE (1.502), the blessing is TALL (0.958). Width does not
      describe size when the aspects differ that much.
      At the brief's 30/45 the blessing would have rendered 2.35x the sweets'
      HEIGHT (183px vs 78px at 1280) and its 47% height would have forced a 58%
      bottom reserve. 38/28 gives 25.3% and 29.2% of card width in height, so
      the blessing still leads by ~15% as the focal point.
      Measured 148x98 and 109x114 at 1280.
      THIRD DISTINCT VARIANT OF THIS TRAP NOW: Weddings/Sangeeth/Haldi needed
      proportional widths to EQUALISE, Baby Showers had equal aspects so the
      split created an imbalance, and here the aspects invert the brief outright.
      Always derive the height (widthPct / aspect) before accepting a width.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 14 kB / 185 kB
      (unchanged — the ground change is a style, not new code).
      No icon, no "07". Overlap with decoration AND corner art 0 everywhere.
      1280 (389x567): ground white, decor 148px @2.619 full-width, panel 53%
      centred, corners 148x98 + 109x114, gaps 20/26, 7 lines.
      1024 (304x601): decor 116px, panel 52% centred, corners 115x77 + 85x89,
      gaps 18/17, 11 lines.
      375 (335x545): decor 128px, panel 54% centred, corners 127x85 + 94x98,
      gaps 19/20, 8 lines, no horizontal scroll.
    grid_complete: >
      Rows at 1280: 626 / 620 / 567. Seemantha sits alone in row 3 and is the
      shortest, so nothing stretches it — the only card whose height is its own.

  - id: baby-showers-reveal-2026-08-11
    date: 2026-08-11
    what: >
      Sixth reveal card, and the second in a row that needed ZERO component
      changes — pure data plus a pastel confetti palette. Six of seven service
      cards now carry the treatment.
    asset_paths_correct_this_time: >
      Brief named public/ root AND the .jpg extension accurately. First time in
      six. Already small (428 KB), so this was cropping, not rescuing.
    THE_DECOR_WAS_A_FULL_BLEED_PATTERN_NOT_A_BAND: >
      baby-shower-bg-decor-revealed is a damask WALLPAPER covering the whole
      1.79 frame, not a top-edge decoration. Rendered `full` at its native ratio
      it would have reserved 58% of the card width in height before a word of
      copy. Cropped to the top 45% — the bunting and the crowns of the damask
      arches — giving 3.984 and a 27% reserve.
      Same trap as the Engagements arch: check whether a "top decor" asset is
      actually a band before trusting its native aspect.
    THE_BRIEFS_30_45_SPLIT_DOES_NOT_EQUALISE_HERE: >
      Both crops came out the SAME shape — 1.119 and 1.128 — so unlike every
      previous card, proportional widths were not needed and 30/45 is not a
      parity ratio: it renders the blessing scene 49% TALLER than the cradle.
      Measured 117x104 against 175x155 at 1280; the 1.49 ratio holds at every
      width.
      Kept as specified — a single prop against a three-figure scene is a
      defensible hierarchy — but flagged, because on the other five cards the
      corners deliberately match. 34/38 would render them equal.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 14 kB / 185 kB
      (unchanged from Birthdays — no new component code).
      No icon, no "06". Overlap with decoration AND corner art is 0 everywhere.
      1280 (389x620): decor 98px @3.985 full-width, panel 53% centred,
      cradle 117x104, blessing 175x155, gaps 32/39, 7 lines.
      1024 (304x630): decor 76px, panel 52% centred, cradle 91x81,
      blessing 137x121, gaps 43/43, 9 lines.
      375 (335x596): decor 84px, panel 54% centred, cradle 101x90,
      blessing 151x134, gaps 19/21, 9 lines, no horizontal scroll.
    grid_state: >
      Row 1 626, row 2 620, row 3 (Baby Showers + 2) 620 at 1280. Only
      "Seemantha" remains static in the last row alongside it — the odd card
      out is now a single one.

  - id: birthdays-reveal-2026-08-11
    date: 2026-08-11
    what: >
      Fifth reveal card. Two genuinely new capabilities: an ordinal-only header
      and per-card confetti (palette + emoji particle shapes).
    THE_BRIEF_WAS_WRONG_TWICE_ABOUT_THE_ASSETS: >
      Specified /assets/birthday-*.PNG. They are in public/ ROOT and they are
      .JPG. Either mistake alone 404s every render. Glob for the basename AND
      check the extension — five briefs running, the stated path has been wrong
      in four of them.
      Already small (319 KB), so this was cropping rather than rescuing:
        cake-initial   1024x572 (1.7902) 79 KB, no crop needed
        decor-top      1024x383 (2.6736) 60 KB, trimmed empty cream top+bottom
        celebration    799x572  (1.3969) 76 KB, subject sat in the right 78%
    ORDINAL_ONLY_HEADER_MUST_BE_ABSOLUTE: >
      `header` went boolean -> 'full' | 'ordinal'. Safe to repurpose because no
      card was still setting it.
      The ordinal CANNOT live in the flex column on a card whose decoration is
      `full` — the card's top padding is reserving the whole garland, so an
      in-flow number lands under the drapes. Pinned absolute top-right at z-40
      it sits above the decoration (z-20) and the copy (z-30), which is where
      the brief wants it. Frosted pill is not decoration: 11px charcoal over
      balloons is unreadable whichever element is in front.
      `flies` is now `header === 'full'`, so 'ordinal' still fades the art out.
    per_card_confetti: >
      celebrationBurst(origin, theme?) with { colors?, emoji? }. Emoji become
      particles via confetti.shapeFromText, rasterised ONCE each rather than per
      particle. Guarded on the function existing — it arrived in
      canvas-confetti 1.6 and a card configured with emoji on an older copy
      should fall back to the plain burst rather than throw mid-click.
      Verified installed version 1.9.4, shapeFromText present.
      Birthdays fires a chocolate/cake/cupcake stage alongside the plain ones;
      the emoji carry their own colour, which is why the plain stages remain.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 13.8 -> 14 kB.
      Cake chip gone; "05" present at z-40, absolute, above the decor, inside
      the card at every width. Overlap with decoration AND corner art is 0
      everywhere.
      1280 (389x620): decor 145px @2.674 full-width uncropped, panel 53%
      centred, corner 175x125 (45%), gaps 42/48, 7 lines.
      1024 (304x630): decor 114px, panel 52% centred, corner 137x98,
      gaps 42/41, 10 lines.
      375 (335x553): decor 125px, panel 54% centred, corner 151x108,
      gaps 19/20, 8 lines, no horizontal scroll.
    INCONSISTENCY_FLAGGED_NOT_SILENTLY_RESOLVED: >
      This is the ONLY reveal card with a visible ordinal. The owner had the
      chip and number removed from Weddings, Engagements, Sangeeth and Haldi,
      then explicitly asked to keep "05" here. Built as asked and raised — one
      numbered card among five unnumbered ones reads as an oversight rather
      than a choice.

  - id: haldi-mehendi-reveal-2026-08-11
    date: 2026-08-11
    what: >
      Fourth reveal card. ZERO component changes — pure data. The config now
