  - id: copy-self-centres-in-the-band
    date: 2026-08-11
    reported: Weddings text box too high, overlapping the garland's hanging detail.
    CONFIRMED_BY_MEASUREMENT_AND_IT_WAS_THE_WASH: >
      At 1280 the Weddings radial wash started at y=80 against a garland ending
      at y=96 — a 16px overlap — and the heading cleared the strip by 4px.
      Below it sat 103px of dead space. The brief described a "frosted glass box
      at max-w-[55%]", which is the ENGAGEMENTS treatment, so both cards were
      measured before touching anything; Engagements was already clean at 0.
    THE_CAUSE_WAS_ROW_STRETCH_NOT_A_MISSING_MARGIN: >
      These are grid items under items-stretch, so the shorter card is stretched
      to the tallest in its row while its content stays pinned to the top. The
      slack is not a constant to be absorbed by a bigger `mt-` — it is however
      much the row happened to stretch THIS card, and it moves with viewport and
      with whatever the tallest sibling is doing. A hardcoded margin would have
      been right at exactly one width.
      Fix: wrap the copy in `flex flex-1 items-center`. flex-1 claims the slack,
      items-center splits it evenly, and the copy self-centres in the band
      between the decoration and the reserved corner strip at any card height.
      1280: wash top 80 -> 136, overlap 16 -> 0, gaps 52 above / 70 below.
    CENTRING_ATE_THE_CLEARANCE_IT_HAD_BEEN_GIVEN: >
      Both reserves had been tuned with the copy pinned to the TOP. Once it
      centred, half the slack moved into the bottom gap and the grounds' own
      bleed went with it — Engagements collapsed from 17px to 1px of clearance
      at 1024. Reserves are now sized for the centred case:
      Engagements 41 -> 46, Weddings 46 -> 49.
      ANY CHANGE TO VERTICAL ALIGNMENT INVALIDATES BOTH RESERVES. Re-measure
      gapGroundToCorners at 1280, 1024 AND 375 after touching either.
    a_phone_has_no_slack_to_centre_into: >
      Single column means each card is its own row, so flex-1 finds nothing to
      claim and the copy sits where the margins put it. That is where the wash's
      20px vertical bleed was overspending: 4px into the garland, 11px into the
      families. Cut to 12px (-inset-y-3); horizontal bleed untouched, nothing
      competes with it there.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 13.8 kB / 185 kB.
      Overlap with decoration AND with corner art is 0 for BOTH cards at all
      three widths.
      1280 (389x561): W gaps 52/70, E gaps 20/26.
      1024 (304x565): W gaps 62/76, E gaps 18/16.
      375: W 335x432 gaps 4/7, E 335x537 gaps 19/20. No horizontal scroll.
      Widths preserved as instructed: W ground 94% of card, E 65%.
    row_cost: 542 -> 561 at 1280, from the two larger reserves.

  - id: engagements-simplified-2026-08-11
    date: 2026-08-11
    THE_NEW_ASSET_WAS_NOT_WHERE_THE_BRIEF_SAID: >
      Brief specified /assets/engagement-silhouette_2.png. No such file, and no
      public/assets/ directory. The owner had REPLACED
      public/engagement-silhouette.png in place — same filename, new picture,
      9.39 MB, and portrait 0.886 swapped for landscape 1.79.
      Pointing the code at the named path would have 404ed every render.
      Check the mtime and size of the EXISTING file before trusting a new
      filename in a brief.
      Optimised 9.39 MB -> 347 KB (1100x614).
    changes:
      - header removed (chip thumbnail + "02" gone). With no chip there is
        nothing to fly into, so the artwork fades out under AnimatePresence —
        which is exactly what the brief asked for, and it falls out of the
        existing `header` flag rather than needing new code.
      - artFit flipped 'height' -> 'width'; the replacement is landscape.
      - panelPct 55 -> 70. Copy dropped 8 lines -> 6 at 1280.
      - cornerRight 36% -> 45% (the brief's number, now affordable).
      - reservePct 32 -> 41.
    DECOR_TOP_IS_NOW_AN_OBJECT_WITH_A_full_FLAG: >
      The engagement decoration is a floral ARCH whose drapes run down both
      sides; cropping it into the fixed ~96px strip cut them off, which is what
      the owner saw. `full: true` renders it at its own aspect across the card's
      full width so nothing is cropped, and the card reserves that height with
      paddingTop DERIVED from the same ratio (100 / aspect + 2) so the two can
      never drift apart. Weddings' garland keeps the cropped strip — it is
      horizontal and looked right that way, and changing it was not asked for.
    PANEL_70_AND_CORNER_45_ONLY_WORK_BECAUSE_THEY_ARE_VERTICALLY_SEPARATED: >
      70 + 45 = 115% of the card, which would be nonsense as a horizontal
      budget. It is fine because the bottom reserve puts the copy entirely
      ABOVE the family's band — the only thing that had to clear horizontally
      was the panel's 16px bottom bleed, which is why reservePct went to 41.
      Measured overlap 0 at every width.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 13.7 kB / 185 kB.
      Served silhouette decodes 1.7944 (expected 1.7915) — the new landscape
      file, 58 KB at 384w.
      1280: both cards 389x542. Engagements — 0 chips, 0 ordinals, decor 145px
      at its native 2.685 (uncropped), padTop 152.6, heading clears it, panel
      65% of card, corner 45%, overlap 0, gap to corner 22, 6 lines.
      1024: 304x550, decor 113px, panel 64%, overlap 0, gap 17, 9 lines.
      375: Engagements 335x520, decor 125px, panel 66%, overlap 0, gap 19,
      7 lines. No horizontal scroll anywhere.
    row_cost: >
      497 -> 542 at 1280. The full-height decoration costs ~52px more than the
      cropped strip plus its margin did. That is the price of showing the drapes
      uncropped, which was the explicit request.
    still_known_cosmetic: >
      Weddings' radial wash still overlaps its corner art by ~1065px^2 at 375.
      Transparent at that edge by construction; only a rectangle intersection.

  - id: reveal-cards-are-now-config-driven
    date: 2026-08-11
    what: >
      Engagements got the tap-to-reveal treatment. Rather than a second card
      component, `revealImage?: string` became `reveal?: ServiceReveal` — a
      per-card config object — and ServiceCardGrid reads it. Any of the seven
      can now opt in with its own assets and layout.
    NEVER_PUT_A_TAILWIND_CLASS_IN_DATA: >
      Tailwind's scanner is static: it reads source files for literal class
      strings. `w-[${n}%]` assembled from a data value is NEVER emitted and the
      rule silently does not exist. So every per-card value in ServiceReveal is
      a NUMBER and the component turns it into an inline style — widths,
      aspect ratios, the bottom reserve. All classes stay literal.
      Bonus: the inline paddingBottom also beats `sm:p-9` outright, which the
      arbitrary `pb-[46%]` did not (media-query rules are emitted after base
      utilities at equal specificity).
    TWO_EXITS_AND_THEY_CANNOT_COEXIST: >
      `header: true` -> the artwork FLIES into the icon chip via a shared
      layoutId. `header` unset -> nothing to fly to, so it fades and shrinks
      under <AnimatePresence>. These are mutually exclusive: an exiting element
      holds its layoutId until the exit finishes, so both copies would claim the
      same id mid-flight. Hence <ConditionalPresence enabled={!flies}> — a
      component, not inline JSX, so toggling does not remount the subtree.
    ART_FIT_AXIS_IS_PER_CARD: >
      The centred artwork takes its own ratio, but which dimension drives it
      depends on the piece. Weddings' is landscape (1.83) -> bound by card
      WIDTH. Engagements' is portrait (0.886) -> fitted to width it would be far
      taller than the card and spill out, so it is bound by HEIGHT. `artFit`
      picks; the unused axis carries a max- so neither can overflow.
    THE_BRIEFS_45_PERCENT_DID_NOT_FIT: >
      Spec said family art max-w-[45%] and panel max-w-[55%]. That is exactly
      100% of the card with nothing left for the panel's own frosted bleed —
      measured, it clipped the family by 12px. 40% fixed 1280 but left 3px at
      1024, because the bleed is a FIXED 32px while both neighbours are
      percentages, so it eats a larger share the narrower the card gets.
      Settled at 36%: clears by 23px at 1280, 15px at 1024, 17px at 375.
      Trimmed the ART, not the copy — the card is already the tallest in its row
      and a narrower panel only adds lines.
    assets: >
      All three arrived in public/ ROOT again, not public/assets/ as the brief
      assumed, and totalled 21.9 MB. Optimised to 1.00 MB:
        engagement-silhouette 1952x2204 -> 900x1016  (0.886)  416 KB
        engagement-decor-top  1952x2204 -> 1200x447  (2.685)  307 KB
        engagement-family-br  2814x1536 ->  816x640  (1.275)  276 KB
      decor-top is a full PORTRAIT arch, not a strip: cropped off the white
      margin and the empty cream above the florals, leaving the drape band.
      family-br is a full-width scene, not a corner vignette: cropped to the
      couple plus the cheering right-hand group.
    header_margin_is_conditional: >
      mt-16 on a headerless card (the copy must clear the 96px decoration
      itself); mt-7 with a header (the 56px chip row already cleared it). Worth
      36px of card height.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 13.7 kB / 185 kB.
      1280: both cards 389x497. Weddings — no header, radial wash, two corners,
      overlap 0, 4 lines. Engagements — chip carries the art, ordinal present,
      both z-40; decor 0-96 z-20; frosted panel blur(12px) z-30 with 23px
      clearance to the family; overlap 0; 8 lines.
      1024: 304x521, panel-to-corner 15px, overlap 0, 10 lines.
      375: Weddings 335x422, Engagements 335x523, panel-to-corner 17px,
      no horizontal scroll.
    THE_ROW_IS_NOW_497: >
      Up from 430. Engagements drives it: 8 lines at 1280 and 10 at 1024,
      because a 55% column is narrow for 155 characters. The levers are widening
      panelPct or shortening the Engagements longDesc — flagged to the owner
      rather than chosen unilaterally.
    known_cosmetic: >
      At 375 the Weddings radial wash overlaps its corner art by ~1065px^2. The
      wash is transparent at that edge by construction so nothing shows; it only
      registers as a rectangle intersection. Not chased.

  - id: card-washes-were-painting-over-the-artwork
    date: 2026-08-11
    THE_REAL_OVERLAP_BUG_FOUND: >
      Owner reported the card overlapping the garland and both families. Last
      turn I measured decoration-vs-TEXT overlap, found 0px, and said it did not
      reproduce. I measured the wrong pair. The card paints three full-bleed
      layers of its own AFTER the decorations in DOM order — the
      charcoal/[0.02] dimension gradient, the hover wash, and the top hairline —
      and all three are z-auto. `z-0` on the decorations put them at the SAME
      stacking level, so DOM order decided, and the card's own washes sat on top
      of the artwork.
      Fix: decorations z-0 -> z-20. Still under the copy at z-30.
      WHEN SOMEONE SAYS "X IS COVERING Y", CHECK X AGAINST EVERY SIBLING, NOT
      JUST THE ELEMENT YOU EXPECT. z-0 and z-auto are the same paint level.
    GARLAND_NOW_DISSOLVES_INSTEAD_OF_BEING_CUT: >
      At 80px the strip sliced the garland mid-motif, through the hanging
      strands and bells — a hard rule across the card, and the thing the owner
      circled. No strip height ends on a natural gap because the strands are
      continuous, so the bottom is masked to transparent instead:
      [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_bottom,#000_62%,transparent_100%)] plus the
      -webkit- twin. Strip also grew h-16/sm:h-20 -> h-20/sm:h-24.
      Text `mt-14` -> `mt-16` so the heading clears the taller strip.
    PB_MUST_TRACK_THE_TALLEST_FIGURE: >
      pb-[42%] was exactly right until the groom went 29% -> 31%, at which point
      his box became 43.2% of card width and he rode up into the paragraph.
      Now pb-[46%]. Re-derive this every time a figure width changes:
      pb% >= (widest figure %) / (its aspect) + ~3.
    GROOM_BUMP_IS_AN_OVERRIDE_NOT_A_CORRECTION: >
      Owner asked twice to enlarge the groom "to match" the bride. Measured with
      a background-difference mask (not the saturation mask — that one misses
      dark hair and the cream-clad figure, which is what made the first
      measurement suspect), the figures fill 96.6% of the groom frame and 97.0%
      of the bride's. At 29% they were already equal to within 0.4px.
      Bumping to 31% makes his group 7.2% TALLER than hers — verified. Done at
      the owner's explicit request and flagged in the code comment; w-[29%]
      restores exact parity.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 13.4 kB / 184 kB.
      1280: card 389x430, decorations z-20 above the card washes (z-auto),
      garland 96px masked, heading top 100 clears it, groom 121x168 vs bride
      128x156 (groom +7.2%), both feet flush at 0, gap to copy 11, inside card.
      1024: card 304x416, garland 96, groom 94x131, bride 100x122, gap 8.
      375: card 335x422, garland 80, groom 104x145, bride 111x135, gap 9,
      no horizontal scroll.
      Row 1: 406 -> 430 at 1280.

  - id: groom-was-genuinely-smaller
    date: 2026-08-11
    the_report_was_right_the_stated_cause_was_not: >
      Owner said the groom's family looked smaller than the bride's and asked
      for a max-w bump. Two things were true and one was not:
        - `max-w-[130px]` was NOT binding (groom box measured 105px), so the
          suggested change would have been a literal no-op.
        - The BOXES were already equal height (155 vs 156).
        - And yet the groom really was smaller.
      MEASURING THE BOX IS NOT MEASURING THE PEOPLE. A saturation mask over
      each asset showed the figures filled 90.3% of the groom frame against
      96.9% of the bride's — so at equal box heights his group rendered ~7%
      shorter and floated ~10px off the floor while hers stood on it. The dead
      space was baked into the crop.
    fix: >
      Re-cropped the groom asset (trimmed the dead 5.7% below the feet):
      433x640 -> 459x640, people 90.3% -> 96.3% of frame, ending at 99.7% like
      the bride's 99.8%. Component: aspect-[433/640] w-[27%] ->
      aspect-[459/640] w-[29%].
      Result: people 151px vs 151px, DELTA 0.1px, both feetGap 0.
    THE_TECHNIQUE_TO_REUSE: >
      To compare two illustrations' apparent scale, do not compare their boxes.
      Project a saturation mask (sat > 0.30, value > 40) onto a row histogram
      and take the first/last row above threshold — that gives the subject's
      extent as a fraction of the frame. Then size boxes so
      fraction x boxHeight matches.
    z_stacking_claim_did_not_reproduce: >
      Owner reported the text being covered by the decorative assets. Measured
      overlap of garland + both families against the text box: 0px at 1280,
      1024 and 375. They occupy disjoint horizontal bands (garland 0-80, copy
      92-243, families 250-406). The only element ever above the copy is the
      exiting silhouette, which unmounts when its 0.45s exit finishes.
      Made the stack explicit anyway since it costs nothing:
      decorations z-0 < copy z-30 < silhouette z-40.
      In THIS PANE the silhouette lingers forever because AnimatePresence's
      exit needs rAF — that is why the card can look permanently covered here.
    STALE_BRIEF_MARKERS: >
      The brief asked to restore three things that had been deliberately
      removed the turn before: the frosted-glass panel, the icon chip and the
      "01". It also asked to re-narrow the copy to w-[55%]. All four were
      undone by the owner's own previous instruction, so none were re-added —
      flagged instead. When a brief describes UI that does not exist, check
      whether it predates the last change before implementing it.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 13.3 kB / 184 kB.
      1280: groom 113x157 / bride 128x156, people 151/151, feet 0/0, deco
      overlap 0, gap to copy 6, chip 0, ordinal 0.
      1024: groom 88x123 / bride 100x122, people 118/118, delta 0.1.
      375: groom 97x136 / bride 111x135, people 131/130, no horizontal scroll.
    IMAGE_CACHE_BIT_AGAIN: >
      Replacing groom-family-bl.png in place served the OLD crop at aspect
      0.6772 while the box expected 0.7172 — the figures would have rendered
      letterboxed. Second time this session. After ANY same-path asset swap:
      stop the server, delete .next/cache/images, restart, and re-check the
      DECODED aspect of a /_next/image variant.

    STACKED_LAYOUT_2026_08_11: >
      Owner: bigger families, text on the card itself and highlighted, heading
      in a different font, and drop BOTH the top-left icon chip and the "01".
      The centre-column + frosted-panel arrangement is gone; the card is now
      three horizontal bands — garland, copy, families.
    REMOVING_THE_CHIP_DELETED_THE_FLIGHT_TARGET: >
      The chip was the landing box for the silhouette's shared-layout flight.
      With it gone there is nothing to fly to, so layoutId is removed entirely
      and the artwork now exits on its own — scale 0.86 + fade over 0.45s while
      the confetti is still in the air. That also REVERSES the earlier rule in
      this file: <AnimatePresence> is now correct, because the thing it used to
      break (two elements claiming one layoutId) no longer exists.
    DROPPING_THE_TOP_ROW_PAID_FOR_THE_BIGGER_FIGURES: >
      Chip 56px + its 28px margin = ~84px reclaimed, and full-width copy fell
      from 7 lines back to 4. Both cards went back to 300px before the reserve
      band was added — the centre-column version had pushed row 1 to 369.
    figures_27_and_33_percent: >
      Up from 18/22. Same equal-height trick: 0.677 and 0.822 sit in nearly the
      ratio 27:33, so both resolve to 39.9% of card width in height (within
      0.6%). 105x155 and 128x156 at 1280 — roughly half again the old size.
    pb_42_PERCENT_RESERVES_THE_BAND: >
      The copy no longer dodges the figures horizontally; a percentage
      bottom-padding reserves their whole band instead. 42% against a 39.9%
      figure height leaves a constant ~2% gap at any card width.
    SM_P_9_BEATS_A_BARE_PB_ARBITRARY: >
      First attempt used `pb-[44%]` alone and the computed padding stayed 36px
      above 640px — `sm:p-9` is a media-query rule emitted after the base
      utilities at equal specificity, so it wins. The families rode up over the
      paragraph by 99px. Must be declared at BOTH breakpoints:
      `pb-[42%] sm:pb-[42%]`. Same trap applies to any arbitrary longhand
      overriding a responsive shorthand in this codebase.
    heading_and_copy_treatment: >
      Heading: font-display (Playfair Display, already loaded as --font-playfair
      and tokenised as --font-display), 28/32px, italic, plum, with a gold/40
      marker bar behind the glyphs — behind, because a wash on top mutes the ink
      it is meant to lift. Sized in `em` so it tracks both breakpoints.
      Copy: /85 and font-normal rather than /70 light, which read washed out on
      cream. Backed by a radial white wash that fades to nothing at its edges —
      a lift, not a container.
      mt-14 on the text block clears the garland; the default mt-7 put the
      heading at 60px, inside the 80px strip.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 13.3 kB / 184 kB.
      1280: card 389x406, Playfair 32px italic, 4 lines, chip 0, ordinal 0,
      groom 105x155, bride 128x156, equal heights, ZERO overlap with the copy
      (7px gap), all inside the card, heading clears the garland.
      1024: card 304x396, 5 lines, groom 82x121, bride 100x122, gap 6.
      375: card 335x401, 28px heading, 5 lines, groom 91x134, bride 111x135,
      gap 6, no horizontal scroll.
      Rows: 1280 row-1 300 -> 406; 1024 326 -> 396; mobile 315 -> 401.
    FILE_HAD_DRIFTED_BEFORE_THIS_TURN: >
      Found contradictory comment blocks describing a 27/33 sizing and a
      `pb-[42%]` that were not in the code, alongside a frosted-panel note for a
      panel that no longer existed. Reconciled. If comments and classes disagree
      here again, trust the measured DOM and rewrite the comment.

    CENTRE_COLUMN_LAYOUT_2026_08_11: >
      Owner: frosted panel was full width and completely covering both family
      groups. Constrained to a centre column so the corners are exposed.
      Text wrapper: mx-auto w-full max-w-[56%], mt-7 -> mt-5, glass bleed
      -inset-x-4 -> -inset-x-3. Figures: w-[18%] / w-[22%] with max-w-[130px]
      and object-contain. Ordinal got a frosted pill.
    THE_CARD_HAD_TO_GROW_AND_THAT_IS_ARITHMETIC_NOT_A_BUG: >
      There is no arrangement of this 155-character paragraph, a 56px chip row,
      an 80px garland and ~100px figures inside the old 300px card. Narrowing
      the column to clear the corners makes the copy taller, and the grid
      stretches every card in the row to match.
      Row 1 (Weddings / Engagements / Sangeeth): 300 -> 369 at 1280.
      At 1024 it is worse: card 304 wide, copy runs to TEN lines in a 154px
      column, row 326 -> 445. 1024 is the worst case because that is where the
      3-column card is narrowest — narrower than the single-column mobile card.
      It grows from FIRST PAINT, not on click, because the copy is always
      mounted (opacity 0) to keep it crawlable.
    SIZE_THE_FIGURES_IN_PERCENT_OR_THEY_DRIFT: >
      First pass used fixed heights (h-24/sm:h-28), which gives fixed widths
      while the text column stays a percentage. Measured: cleared fine at 389px
      but overlapped the panel by 13px on a ~330px lg card. Percentage widths
      keep figures and column in a fixed relationship at every card width.
    18_AND_22_PERCENT_GIVE_EQUAL_HEIGHTS: >
      The two aspects (0.677 and 0.822) sit in nearly the same ratio as 18:22,
      so both resolve to ~26.7% of card width in height — within 0.6%. That is
      what keeps the two families at one scale. Equal WIDTHS would have made the
      groom's three figures visibly larger than the bride's four.
      44% total (20/24) left 0px clearance on the bride side at 1280: the glass
      is centred on the card while the figures are asymmetric, so the wider side
      loses. 40% total clears at both ends at every width.
    z_index_was_already_correct_legibility_was_not: >
      The chip row and ordinal were already z-20 over the garland's z-0 —
      stacking order was never the problem. 11px charcoal on marigolds is
      unreadable whichever element is in front, so the ordinal now gets its own
      bg-white/70 backdrop-blur-sm pill when the card is open.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 11.2 kB / 182 kB.
      1280: card 389x369, glass 201 wide, groom 70x103, bride 86x104, equal
      heights, ZERO overlap with the glass, gaps 24 left / 8 right, 7 lines.
      1024: card 304x445, groom 55x81, bride 67x81, zero overlap, gaps 20/8,
      10 lines.
      375: card 335x384, groom 60x89, bride 74x90, zero overlap, gaps 19/6,
      8 lines, glass inside the card, no horizontal scroll.
    TWO_MORE_FROZEN_TRANSITION_FALSE_ALARMS: >
      The ordinal pill read background transparent and the chip still read
      gold/12 after reveal. Both were `transition-colors duration-500` frozen at
      the start value. With the transition suppressed they settle to white/0.7
      and transparent respectively. That is now three separate times this pane
      has faked a styling bug — suppress the transition before believing any
      computed colour on an element that just changed class.

    REAL_ASSETS_LANDED_2026_08_10: >
      Owner supplied the three decorations. They went to public/ ROOT, not
      public/assets/ where the component pointed — so the card was still showing
      my placeholders. Component paths moved to /garland-top.png etc. and
      public/assets/ was deleted. Canonical location is now public/ root,
      matching where the owner drops files.
      Combined 18.2 MB -> 637 KB.
        garland-top     2814x1536 -> 1200x537 (2.2346)  323 KB
        groom-family-bl 2332x1824 ->  433x640 (0.6766)  154 KB
        bride-family-br 2332x1824 ->  526x640 (0.8219)  198 KB
      Originals in the scratchpad as *.original.png.
    ALL_THREE_ARE_OPAQUE_SO_THE_CARD_GROUND_MOVED_TO_MEET_THEM: >
      alpha min=max=255 on all three — no cut-outs, just illustrations on a flat
      #f8f5ee. On the white card they read as three pasted rectangles with
      visible edges. Fix: the OPENED card's background becomes #f8f5ee, sampled
      from the assets, so the seams vanish and the figures appear to stand on
      the card. The value is deliberately not a brand token — it is copied from
      the artwork and must change if the artwork does.
      Verified rgb(248,245,238) with the 500ms transition-colors suppressed.
      Body copy over the frosted panel on that ground measures 8.41:1.
    FINDING_THE_FIGURES: >
      A plain background-difference bbox was useless — it locked onto the faint
      decorative border frame and returned ~the whole image. What worked was a
      SATURATION mask (sat > 0.30, value > 40) projected onto column and row
      histograms: the clothing is strongly saturated, the cream ground and the
      tan border are not. That located groom figures at x 5-50% and bride at
      x 55-100%, both y ~33-100%, which is what the crop windows came from.
    corner_pieces_are_matched_on_HEIGHT_not_width: >
      Three figures vs four means different natural widths (433:640 vs
      526:640). Equal widths would have scaled the two sets of people
      differently; equal heights is what makes them read as one scene. Each box
      carries its own aspect-[] and a shared h-28/sm:h-32.
    a_stray_element_had_to_be_cropped_past: >
      The groom source has a detached green saree panel at its far left edge.
      The first crop kept it and it read as a floating shape. Crop now starts at
      x 15% of the source, immediately left of the first figure.
    garland_anchors_object_top: >
      Source is 2.23:1 into a ~4.9:1 strip, so cover shows only the upper 46%.
      object-top puts that window on the garland rows, medallion and bells;
      anything lower surfaces the pillar bases and bare floor.
    verified_with_real_assets: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 11.1 kB / 182 kB.
      1280: card 389x300, all three inside the card, garland 389x80 100% clear
      of the glass, groom 87x128 at 38% unfrosted, bride 105x128 at 34%.
      Served variants decode at 2.226 / 0.677 / 0.823 — matching the sources.
      375: card bg rgb(248,245,238), garland 335x64, groom 76x112, bride 92x112,
      glass covers the copy and stays inside the card, no horizontal scroll,
      all cards still 315.

    PLACEHOLDERS_ARE_REAL_FILES_NOT_MISSING_PATHS: >
      Owner asked for placeholder src paths to swap later. next/image pointing
      at a nonexistent public file does NOT break the build, but it 400s per
      render and logs an error every time — the card would look broken until
      assets landed. So public/assets/{garland-top,groom-family-bl,
      bride-family-br}.png exist as flat translucent colour blocks (~1.3 KB
      each): nothing 404s, geometry is measurable, and dropping real art at the
      same paths just works. NOT artwork and NOT depictions of people — the
      owner's standing rule against AI-generated imagery still holds.
    decorations_are_conditionally_MOUNTED: >
      `isRevealCard && revealed`, not opacity-0-always. A visitor who never
      opens the card never downloads three extra images.
    THE_GLASS_IS_AN_ABSOLUTE_SIBLING_NOT_A_BACKGROUND: >
      An absolute panel at -inset-x-4/-inset-y-3 behind the text, rather than a
      bg on the text container. It bleeds past the copy on all four sides so the
      frosting has a margin instead of cropping tight to the glyphs, and it
      still auto-resizes with the copy. Measured: covers the text with 12px
      vertical and 16px horizontal padding, pointer-events none.
    flex_grow_had_to_come_OFF_the_paragraph: >
      On the reveal card only. Letting the paragraph absorb the column's slack
      stretched the frosted panel to the bottom edge and buried both corner
      decorations behind it. Without it the slack collects at the bottom, which
      is the 24px clear band the figures now show in.
    HOW_MUCH_ACTUALLY_READS: >
      389x300 card: garland 389x80 and 100% clear of the glass. Corner figures
      128x128 with only 31% fully unfrosted each — the rest shows through 60%
      white + blur(12px), softened rather than hidden. Clear bands: 24px below
      the panel, 20px each side. 375px: same picture, 30%.
      The card is nearly full at 300px (chip 56 + heading + a 4-line paragraph),
      so this is the honest ceiling without shortening the copy, letting the row
      grow, or shrinking the corner boxes. Flagged to the owner rather than
      silently changing any of the three.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. overflow-hidden on the reveal card
      only, nothing clipped by the card edge, no horizontal overflow, all seven
      cards still 300px (315 mobile). Chip still rests at 56x56 after the
      overflow-hidden change, so the FLIP is unaffected. Focus still lands on
      the heading. Zero decoration images requested before the click.
    MEASURE_TEXT_WITH_THE_ENTRANCE_TRANSFORM_ZEROED: >
      First coverage read said the glass did NOT cover the paragraph. False —
      h3 and p were frozen at translateY(20px) from the REVEAL variant because
      rAF does not advance here. Zero the inline transform before judging any
      geometry that involves these two.

    ENLARGED_2026_08_10: >
      Owner: "I want that image to cover a bigger space of that card."
      It was capped at max-w-[17rem] = 272px inside ~341px of available space,
      so the card read as mostly empty.
      Changes: overlay p-6 -> p-4; button w-[86%] max-w-[17rem] -> flex h-full
      w-full items-center justify-center (the cap removed entirely, and flex
      centring added because h-full would otherwise pin the frame to the top);
      span gained max-h-full; sizes -> "(min-width:1024px) 400px,
      (min-width:640px) 340px, 90vw".
      Result at 1280: 357x195, up from 272x148 — +73% area, 92% of the card
      width, 60% of its area, still vertically centred and aspect-exact.
      1600: 362x198, 92% width, gaps 51/51 symmetric.
      375: 303x166, 90% width, gaps 75/75, no horizontal scroll.
      FLIP still exact: scale(6.373, 3.478) vs 357/56 = 6.375 and
      195/56 = 3.482.
    MAX_H_FULL_IS_THE_SAFETY_CATCH: >
      With the width cap gone, a wide-and-short card would let the fixed aspect
      push the frame past the bottom edge. `max-h-full` clamps it; because the
      img is object-cover the picture crops rather than the frame overflowing.
      Not currently reachable (cards are ~394x300 at the widest) but it is what
      stops a future column-count change from breaking the card.

    THE_ASPECT_IS_HARDCODED: >
      `aspect-[960/524]` is pinned to this specific file. Any future asset of a
      different shape letterboxes or crops until that class and the chip's
      object-position are updated. Flagged in a comment at the call site.
    NEXT_CACHES_OPTIMISED_IMAGES_ACROSS_A_FILE_SWAP: >
      After replacing public/image_3.png, /_next/image kept serving the OLD
      picture — decoded 384x278 at aspect 1.381 with byte-identical size to the
      previous variant, while the raw /image_3.png was already the new file.
      Next's image cache did not invalidate on the swap.
      Fix: stop the dev server, delete .next/cache/images, restart. 48 stale
      files. ALWAYS check the DECODED ASPECT of a /_next/image variant after
      swapping an asset — the page layout can be perfectly correct while the
      pixels served are stale, which looks exactly like "you didn't change it".

    THE_ASSET_WAS_5_MB: >
      image_3.png shipped at 2816x1536, 5.19 MB, for something rendered at
      240px. Trimmed its padding and downscaled to 900x651 palette PNG:
      187 KB, a 96.6% cut. Original backed up to the session scratchpad as
      image_3.original.png. Same treatment as bg-pattern.jpg earlier.
      Delivered via next/image it is 15 KB at 256w, 28 KB at 384w.
    IT_IS_NOT_A_TRANSPARENT_SILHOUETTE: >
      Sampled corners read #f5f4f0-#f7f6f4, fully opaque — an illustration on a
      cream ground, not a cut-out. So it is framed deliberately
      (rounded-xl + ring-1) instead of floating, and the chip drops its gold
      tint when the artwork lands, or the tint would show as a rim.
      Chip uses `object-cover object-left`: the couple occupies the left ~62%
      of a 1.38:1 frame and a centre crop cuts the bride out.
    accessibility: >
      Real <button> with aria-label and aria-expanded. It unmounts on reveal,
      which would drop focus to <body>, so focus moves to the heading
      (tabIndex={-1}). Title and copy stay MOUNTED at opacity 0 rather than
      unmounted — the words stay in the served HTML for crawlers and the card
      keeps full height from first paint, which is what stops the grid
      resnapping. aria-hidden mirrors the visual state.
    confetti: >
      lib/confetti.ts, dynamic import so it stays off the critical path —
      confirmed as its own chunk, and /services/[slug] First Load JS is
      182 kB without it. Two-stage burst plus sparkler tails in plum/gold.
      canvas-confetti appends a permanent fixed <canvas> to <body>; reset()
      runs at 2600ms so it does not linger — this project has already mistaken
      a stray full-bleed canvas for a broken background once.
      Timers held in an ARRAY: the first draft kept one id and the second
      assignment orphaned the first.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 10.9 kB / 182 kB.
      Clicked it for real: button and overlay unmount, both aria-hidden flip to
      false, chip receives the image, focus lands on the H3, card height stays
      300px (no snap), confetti chunk requested, zero canvases left on <body>.
      Mobile 375: card 335, artwork 213x154 at aspect 1.383, inside the card,
      tap target 213x154, no horizontal scroll.
    not_verified: >
      The motion itself — rAF is frozen in this pane, so the spring, the fade
      and the particles never advance. The frozen FLIP transform above is the
      evidence the transition is correctly computed, not that it looks right.

  - id: wrong-component-for-two-turns
    date: 2026-08-10
    THE_LESSON: >
      The owner reported a "giant radial gradient / black hole hiding the text"
      in "StatsFooter" THREE times. I cleaned StatsFooter twice and measured it
      clean twice, then asserted it was clean — which was true and useless.
      There are TWO stats rows on the home page:
        - StatsFooter  — inside the hero, 4 icons + numbers (what I kept fixing)
        - StatsBand    — a separate charcoal section further down, imported by
                         app/page.tsx AND components/about/AboutContent.tsx
      StatsBand was the one with the effect. When a report keeps not matching
      the measurement, the component identity is the thing to doubt, not the
      owner.
    HOW_TO_FIND_IT_NEXT_TIME: >
      Do not search by component name. Query the DOM for every element whose
      text matches the reported content and report which <main> child each one
      lives in. Two hits for "10+" in different sections is the whole answer.
      Second query: every element >300x300 carrying a blur filter, a radial
      gradient, a blend mode, or a canvas, grouped by section.
    what_was_actually_there: >
      StatsBand carried three stacked layers behind the figures:
        - BlackHoleScene (WebGL/three.js) intensity 0.85 at opacity-70
        - ParticleField <canvas>, full-bleed
        - a radial-gradient ellipse scrim whose only job was to claw back
          legibility from the other two
      All three deleted. Ground is now a flat #0d0216 with border-t white/10,
      and the grid is the section's ONLY child.
    props_removed: >
      `particles` and `blackHole` went with the layers they gated. Neither call
      site ever passed them — checked both before deleting.
    also_removed: >
      drop-shadow-[0_2px_18px_rgba(28,27,31,0.9)] on the numbers. It existed
      solely to lift them off the accretion disc; on a flat near-black ground a
      charcoal shadow renders nothing.
    left_alone: >
      components/three/BlackHoleScene and components/ui/ParticleField still
      exist — only StatsBand's usage was removed. HeroIntro's BlackHoleTransition
      is a different component and is untouched.
    verified: >
      tsc clean. StatsBand: bg rgb(13,2,22), border-t 1px white/0.1, relative
      z-10, ZERO canvases, ZERO absolutely-positioned children, ZERO elements
      with filter/backdrop-filter/gradient/blend inside, exactly one direct
      child (the grid), 4 cols at lg and 2 at 385px, numbers #D4AF37.
      Canvases on the whole page: 1 -> 0. No horizontal scroll.
      Page-wide check: effect layers behind every StatsBand figure = 0.
    the_hero_orbs_are_a_red_herring: >
      A proximity query still lists 2-4 blurred layers "behind" the StatsFooter
      numbers. Those are the hero orbs and key light at z-0/z-1, and the bar's
      opaque z-10 ground sits between them and the text. Geometric overlap is
      not visual bleed — check for alpha and backdrop-filter on the ground
      before concluding anything from an overlap count.

  - id: stats-bar-kinetic-rebuild
    date: 2026-08-10
    reported: "massive, unstyled radial blur/gradient obscuring the text"
    THE_BLUR_WAS_NEVER_IN_THIS_COMPONENT: >
      StatsFooter's markup had no canvas, no gradient, no blur. The bar was
      bg-[#150524]/90 + backdrop-blur-md, and sitting directly behind it are
      hero-orb-b (653px radial gradient under blur(64px)) and the 1512px key
      light (also blur(64px)). 10% transparency let both through and the
      backdrop-blur smeared them across the numbers. Fix: a fully opaque
      #0d0216 — the hero gradient's own end colour.
      DO NOT reintroduce alpha or backdrop-blur on this element. Measured after:
      zero elements in the bar carry backdrop-filter or a blur filter.
      The only <canvas> on the page is in the charcoal StatementBand section,
      nowhere near the stats row — left alone.
    rebuilt_to_spec: >
      Client component. motion.dl with whileInView + staggerChildren 0.15.
      Per card: perspective wrapper -> preserve-3d tilt card. useMotionValue ->
      useSpring -> useTransform, max 10deg. Number is a motion.dd with a second,
      heavily damped spring for magnetic pull (max 10px) and z:20 so it floats
      off the tilting plane.
    TWO_ELEMENTS_PER_CARD_IS_MANDATORY: >
      `perspective` applies to an element's CHILDREN, never to its own
      transform. Collapsing the wrapper and the tilt card into one element
      yields a flat 2D skew that looks subtly wrong rather than obviously
      broken. The wrapper also has to be a motion.div: framer propagates
      variants through motion components only, so a plain div there would
      silently kill the stagger.
    pointer_position_is_normalised: >
      0..1 fraction of the card, not raw px — so the same transform ranges give
      exactly MAX_TILT at the edge whether the card is 375px wide on a phone or
      350px in a four-up row. 0.5/0.5 is rest.
    kept_deliberately: >
      The MetallicRing bezel and the double gold bottom border, both from the
      owner's previous spec one turn earlier. Neither appears in this spec's
      cleanup list (canvas / radial blur / black hole), and deleting
      just-requested work unasked is worse than carrying it. Flagged to the
      owner; one word removes either.
    reduced_motion: tilt and magnet handlers detach entirely, reveal becomes opacity-only.
    verified: >
      tsc clean. 1440: ground rgb(13,2,22) with backdrop none, border-t
      white/0.05, 4 cols, perspective 1000px on the wrapper, preserve-3d on the
      card, transition-colors 0.5s, number 36px/700/#D4AF37, label 12px with
      2.4px tracking (= 0.2em) uppercase gray-400. Bar 153px.
      385px: 1 col, all labels 1 line, no horizontal scroll.
      1440x560: stats overflow 0, nothing clipped.
    NOT_verified_and_why: >
      The reveal, the tilt and the magnet. 57 of 68 framer-driven elements
      across the WHOLE page read opacity 0 here — including the hero's
      pre-existing stats wrapper, which the owner's own screenshots show
      rendering fine. Framer never ticks in this pane. The falsification test
      worth repeating: count inline-opacity elements at 0 vs 1 page-wide; if
      almost all are 0, it is the pane, not the component. The h1 reads 1
      because it is CSS-keyframe driven, which is precisely why it was built
      that way.
    THE_HEADROOM_IS_NOW_1PX: >
      Bar went 141 -> 153. At 1440x560 the grid needs 406 and has 407. One
      pixel. The clip threshold is back up to ~560px of viewport height from
      ~530. Anything else added to this bar MUST be re-measured at 560 first.
    label_wraps_at_four_up: >
      "Years Creating Memories" now takes 2 lines in a 4-column row — the spec
      moved labels from 10px to 12px. Harmless, but it is why the first cell is
      taller than the others.
    double_entrance: >
      Hero.tsx still wraps StatsFooter in its own enter() fade, so the band
      fades in and THEN the items stagger. Sequenced, not broken. If the
      stagger should be the only entrance, drop the wrapper animation in
      Hero.tsx.

  - id: metallic-bezel-stats-bar
    date: 2026-08-10
    decision: >
      StatsFooter rebuilt to an owner-supplied spec: 3-layer nested-div metallic
      bezel around each icon, richer container, double bottom border, larger
      type. No image assets — the whole bezel is CSS.
    HOW_THE_BEZEL_WORKS: >
      Three nested rings, none collapsible:
        1. rim   h-16 w-16, bg-gradient-to-b #E0B95B -> #8C6D23, p-[2px].
           The gradient IS the illusion — lit edge above, shadowed below, so it
           reads as turned metal rather than a flat stroke.
        2. groove w-full h-full, bg-[#11041C], p-[3px]. The dark gap is what
           sells the depth; without it the two golds touch and it flattens.
        3. inner  w-full h-full, border-[#D4AF37]/70, centres the icon.
      Sizing comes from padding on the parents, not fixed sizes on the children,
      so the whole bezel scales from the single h-16 w-16 on the rim.
    GRID_COLS_2_DOES_NOT_FIT_A_PHONE: >
      Spec said `grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4`. Built it, measured at 385px: cells
      are 188px, and p-6 (48) + 64px ring + gap-5 (20) leave 60px for the text.
      Every number and label overflowed its cell by 11-20px and "Years Creating
      Memories" wrapped to THREE lines. Changed to
      `grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4` — the column count gives, not
      the 64px bezel, because the bezel is the point of the component. The
      specified two-column layout is untouched from sm up.
    THE_BAR_GREW_95_TO_141: >
      +46px, straight out of the hero's vertical budget (see
      stats-bar-was-clipped-on-short-viewports). The clip threshold moved from
      ~630px of viewport height to ~530px — still far better than before the
      fluid-padding fix, but the margin is thinner than it was. Any future
      growth in this bar has to be re-checked at 560.
      Below ~530 the failure mode is now correct-by-design: min-h-0 + shrink-0
      mean the hero COPY tucks behind the bar rather than the numbers being
      sliced. At 500 the grid spills 46px; stats overflow still 0.
    verified: >
      tsc clean. 1280: 4 cols, border-top gold/30, bg #150524/90, backdrop
      blur(12px); rim 64x64 with the gradient and shadow, groove 60x60 #11041C,
      inner 54x54 border gold/70, icon 24x24 #E0B95B; number 30px #E0B95B,
      label 10px tracking 2.5px (= 0.25em); bars 1px gold/30 then 3px gold/70
      at mt 2px. 1440x900: padding 32/40, gap 20, no horizontal scroll.
      707px: 2 cols at 350, all labels 1 line, 37-117px headroom.
      385px: 1 col at 375, all labels 1 line, 63-142px headroom, no h-scroll.
      1440x560: grid needs 406 of 419 available, stats overflow 0, nothing clipped.

  - id: void-under-the-stats-bar
    date: 2026-08-10
    symptom: Owner circled a large empty ivory band between the stats bar and the services eyebrow.
    measured_cause: >
      252px from the section's top edge to the eyebrow at 1280, and almost all
      of it was nothing: 112px of section padding (py-28, symmetric) + a 74px
      DescenderRule + 36px of margin under it. A hairline gold rule hanging in
      the middle of an empty band reads AS the void rather than as a mark
      announcing the section.
    fix: >
      ServicesGrid: py-20/24/28 split into pb-20/24/28 + pt-10/12/14. Only the
      TOP gives — it is already cushioned by the dense dark stats band above it;
      the bottom keeps the full section rhythm against the next section.
      DescenderRule: line h-12/sm:h-16 -> h-8/sm:h-10, wrapper mb-7/sm:mb-9 ->
      mb-4/sm:mb-6.
    safe_to_retune: ServicesGrid is imported only by app/page.tsx — this top-padding change cannot leak onto /services.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, server 200. 1280 (lg): padTop 112 -> 56, rule 74 -> 50, ruleMb
      36 -> 24, gap section-top-to-eyebrow 252 -> 160 (-37%). padBottom still
      112. Mobile 385: padTop 40, padBottom 80, rule 42, gap 128, no horizontal
      scroll.
    whats_left: >
      Of the remaining 160px, ~74 is the DescenderRule (50 + 24 margin). If the
      owner wants the gap gone rather than reduced, deleting <DescenderRule />
      from ServicesSectionHeader is the one-line follow-up. Not done unasked —
      it is a deliberate element with a documented rationale.
    I_MADE_THE_SAME_JSX_MISTAKE_TWICE_IN_ONE_SESSION: >
      Put {/* comment */} directly after `return (` as a sibling of the root
      element — AGAIN, in ServicesGrid, minutes after doing it in
      ScrollRevealCards. Two adjacent expressions, SWC syntax error, every route
      500s. A comment describing the root element goes ABOVE the `return` as a
      plain block comment. The stale 500s stay in preview_logs afterwards and
      look live — check the log TAIL, not a search across history.

  - id: stats-bar-was-clipped-on-short-viewports
    date: 2026-08-10
    symptom: Owner screenshot — stats band sliced through, numbers visible, labels gone.
    NOT_WHAT_IT_LOOKED_LIKE: >
      It looks exactly like the next section overlapping the hero, which is a
      bug this project has had before (see the -mt-20 / pb-16 fix on the
      vertical heroes). It was not. Measured every child of <main>: no negative
      margins, sections stack edge to edge, 2160 -> 2160. CHECK THE STACK BEFORE
      ASSUMING OVERLAP.
    real_cause: >
      The hero column had ZERO vertical slack and the sticky viewport is
      overflow:hidden. Fixed demand was grid 384 + pt-28 112 + pb-10 40 +
      stats 94 = 630px MINIMUM. At 720 it fit with 90px spare; at 620 it
      overflowed and the wrapper sliced the stats bar. Reproduced at 1440x620:
      24px clipped.
    fix:
      - min-h-0 on the flex-1 grid wrapper. A flex item defaults to
        min-height:auto and REFUSES to shrink below content, so the wrapper held
        its size and pushed the stats bar out of the box instead of giving way.
      - shrink-0 on the stats wrapper. Together these set the order of
        sacrifice — the grid gives, the numbers never do.
      - svh-fluid padding, pt-[clamp(5rem,14svh,7rem)] and
        pb-[clamp(0.75rem,3svh,2.5rem)], replacing 152px of immovable space.
        5rem floor clears the fixed header; 14svh reproduces the old 112px at
        800px and above.
      - deck max-w-lg -> max-w-[min(32rem,69.3svh)] in ScrollRevealCards.
        Capping WIDTH by viewport height is what preserves aspect-[4/3]:
        69.3svh wide is 52svh tall. min() means 32rem still wins above ~740px.
    MIN_H_0_ALONE_IS_NOT_ENOUGH: >
      It only changes WHAT gets clipped — the grid is items-center, so the
      headline would have been sliced instead of the stats. The fluid padding
      and the deck cap are what make it actually fit, so nothing is sacrificed.
    verified: >
      tsc clean. Measured with the frozen entrance transform neutralised
      (translateY(14px) is a pane artifact and accounted for every phantom
      overflow reading — ALWAYS zero it before judging this element).
      560: pad 80/17, deck 388x291, overflow 0, clipped leaves 0
      620: pad 87/19, deck 430x322, overflow 0, clipped leaves 0
      720: pad 101/22, deck 499x374, overflow 0, clipped leaves 0
      900: pad 112/27, deck 512x384 — byte-identical to the old flat values
      mobile 385x834: position relative, overflow 0, zero overlap with the next
      section, no horizontal scroll.
      All 8 leaves (4 numbers + 4 labels) inside the frame at every height.
    cosmetic_deltas: >
      Bottom padding is 27px at 900 rather than 40 (3svh does not reach 40 until
      1333px), and the deck is 499 rather than 512 at exactly 720. Both are
      below the threshold of notice; flagged so they are not later mistaken for
      regressions.
    i_broke_the_build_getting_here: >
      Put a {/* JSX comment */} directly after `return (` as a sibling of the
      root element — two adjacent expressions, SWC syntax error, every route
      500ed. A comment above the root element must be a plain /* */ ABOVE the
      `return`, not a JSX comment inside it.

  - id: diagonal-deal-exit
    date: 2026-08-10
    decision: >
      Cards 03 and 02 now exit diagonally — up AND right, tilting 15deg — like a
      card being dealt off a deck, instead of sliding straight up. Card 01 is
      unchanged; it only settles into primary and stays.
      Top: x 0->400, y 0->-400, rotate 0->15, scale 1->0.9 over [0, 0.33].
      Mid: x [0,0,400], y [20,0,-400], rotate [3,0,15] over [0, 0.33, 0.66].
      Opacity ramps unchanged. Rest state unchanged.
    REST_STATE_STILL_DECODES_EXACT: >
      x 0/0/0, y 40/20/0, scale 0.9/0.95/1, rotate -4/+3/0. The new x and rotate
      channels start at 0 so the fanned stack at progress 0 is byte-identical to
      before. Verified from the computed matrix.
    the_exit_is_clipped_not_offscreen: >
      The card does not fly past the browser edge — the hero's sticky wrapper is
      overflow:hidden (0..1265 x 0..720 at 1280x720), so the card slides under
      the section edge. At the end of the arc the top card's box is
      1061..1596 x -278..175, i.e. still overlapping the frame by a ~204x175
      corner — but opacity is already 0 there, so nothing shows. Desirable, and
      it is why 400px of travel causes no horizontal scroll:
      documentElement.scrollWidth 1271 < viewport 1280.
    SCALE_ASYMMETRY_FLAGGED_NOT_FIXED: >
      Card 03 shrinks on exit (scale -> 0.9, specified) while card 02 still
      GROWS (scale -> 1.05, carried over — the spec listed x/y/rotate/opacity
      for card 02 and omitted scale). So the two exits read differently: 03
      recedes, 02 looms. Left as specified rather than silently harmonised.
      One-line fix if wanted: midScale [0.95, 1, 0.9].
    verified: tsc clean. Rest state exact. No horizontal overflow. Mobile untouched (column is hidden lg:block).
    not_verified: the flight itself — rAF does not advance in this pane. End-state geometry above was measured by applying the terminal transform by hand, not by scrubbing.

  - id: nav-hides-behind-the-curtain
    date: 2026-08-10
    decision: >
      The header is hidden for the duration of the opening sequence. HeroIntro
      writes data-intro="playing" onto <html>; globals.css hides
      [data-site-header] while it is there. Navbar's <header> carries the
      data-site-header attribute.
    why_not_z_index: >
      The intro overlay is z-[60] and the header is z-50, yet the header still
      painted OVER the closed doors — the overlay sits inside the hero section
      and the header is fixed at document level, so they are not in a shared
      stacking context and the numbers never compete. Raising z-[60] would not
      have fixed it. State on the root element sidesteps the whole question.
    why_not_react_context: >
      Header is in the layout, intro is in the page. No shared provider, and
      adding one would make every route carry a sequence only Home plays.
    TRANSITION_NONE_IS_LOAD_BEARING: >
      A transition is read off the state being animated TO. `transition: none`
      in the hidden rule makes the HIDE instant; the header's own
      `transition-all duration-500` still governs the RETURN. Getting this
      backwards (a 280ms fade out, which is what I wrote first) reintroduces the
      exact bug — 280ms of nav sitting on the closed curtain.
    useLayoutEffect_not_useEffect: >
      With a plain useEffect the browser can paint the frame where the doors
      mount but the attribute has not landed. Guarded via a typeof window
      check because React warns on useLayoutEffect during SSR.
    fails_open: >
      The attribute is only ever ADDED by JS and the cleanup removes it, so no
      JS / no mount / crashed component all leave the header visible. The
      existing 4.2s watchdog flips `finished`, which tears the attribute down
      even if GSAP never loads.
    visibility_not_just_opacity: an opacity-0 header is still tabbable.
    verified: >
      tsc clean. Live CSS both directions: with the attribute the header reads
      opacity 0 / visibility hidden / pointer-events none; suppressing the
      element's own transition snaps it back to opacity 1 / visible (the
      residual 0 on the way back is this pane freezing the 500ms return
      transition, not the rule).
      Real load probed through a same-origin iframe, 280 samples at 25ms:
      433-1420ms hydrating, no curtain, header visible; 1560-5759ms curtain up
      AND intro=playing AND header hidden/none across 169 consecutive samples;
      5790ms attribute gone. curtainWithoutHide = 0 — not one sample where the
      overlay was on screen and the header was not already hidden.
    probe_gotcha: >
      First probe used img[src*="smaya-monogram-light"] to detect the curtain
      and reported a 1.5s window of curtain-without-hide. False positive — the
      NAVBAR LOGO is the same file. Detect the intro by circle[r="74"] (the
      drawn ring), which nothing else on the site has.
    iframe_probe_is_the_technique: >
      Reloading and then querying cannot catch a sequence shorter than the tool
      round-trip. Loading the site into a same-origin iframe from the already-
      open page lets a setInterval sample the whole thing without navigating
      the outer document. Reusable for anything time-boxed at load.
    not_verified: >
      Curtain ran 1560-5759ms here = the 4.2s watchdog, because rAF does not
      advance in this pane so GSAP never finishes the doors. On a real browser
      the sequence is ~2.6s. The hide/show wiring is unaffected by that.

  - id: card-peel-mappings
    date: 2026-08-07
    decision: >
      Track lengthened to lg:h-[300vh] and ScrollRevealCards rewritten to a
      supplied set of useTransform mappings. Deck gained [perspective:1000px].
    status: current
    ALL_TRANSFORMS_ARE_FRAMERS_NOW: >
      There are deliberately NO Tailwind rotate-*/translate-* utilities left on
      these cards. Tailwind v4 compiles those to the discrete `rotate:` /
      `translate:` CSS properties, which COMPOSE with framer's `transform:`
      rather than replacing it — so a static offset silently adds to the
      animated one and the resting fan drifts as it animates. One source of
      truth for card position: the motion style.
      Verified: rotate/translate computed properties read `none` on all three.
    REST_STATE_IS_THE_PROOF: >
      At progress 0 the deck decodes to EXACTLY the specified values —
      y 40/20/0, scale 0.900/0.950/1, rotate -4.0/+3.0/0. That is meaningful
      evidence the whole useTransform chain is wired correctly even though the
      progress input cannot be advanced here; only the input is stuck, not the
      mapping. Decode scale as hypot(m[0],m[1]) and rotation as
      atan2(m[1],m[0]) from the computed matrix.
    the_pin_is_now_two_viewports: >
      300vh track minus a 100vh sticky viewport = 200vh of pinned scroll before
      the hero releases. Section measures 2460px at 1440x820. That is a long
      pinned stretch and it is the user's specified number — but it is the first
      thing to shorten if the hero feels like it traps the reader.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 16.2 kB / 195 kB. No server errors.
      1440x820: track 2460 (exactly 3x viewport), sticky h 820, deck 512x384
      with perspective 1000px, cards z 10/20/30, gold /50 borders, all three
      images loaded, captions 01 Fitness / 02 Corporate / 03 Celebrations.
      375x812: section 855, position relative, no track, no dead scroll, stats
      present, zero overlap with the next section, no horizontal overflow.
    NOT_verified: >
      THE PEEL. Three separate attempts now: framer's useScroll reads on rAF,
      rAF does not advance in this pane, and every sampled scroll position
      returns the rest state. If this ever needs proving without a human, it has
      to be rewritten on GSAP ScrollTrigger, which IS exercisable here because
      lib/lenis.ts wires lenis.on('scroll', ScrollTrigger.update) synchronously.

  - id: hero-is-a-scroll-track
    date: 2026-08-07
    decision: >
      Hero.tsx rewritten to the track architecture: the <section> IS the scroll
      track (lg:h-[200vh], and the element useScroll measures), with a single
      sticky h-svh wrapper holding every visible layer. ScrollRevealCards became
      presentational and takes `progress` as a prop.
    status: current
    THE_BACKGROUND_MUST_LIVE_INSIDE_THE_STICKY_WRAPPER: >
      This is the actual fix for the reported "seam", and it is not obvious.
      With the image layer on the SECTION, `bg-cover` sized itself against a
      200vh box — 1440x1975 — so a 2.09-aspect image was scaled to cover a
      0.73-aspect area and the visible crop bore no relation to the artwork.
      Inside the h-svh sticky wrapper it covers exactly one viewport, which is
      what bg-cover was written for. Measured: bg layer 820px tall, not 1975.
      Any future background layer goes inside the sticky wrapper too.
    WHY_THE_CARDS_WERE_SQUISHED: >
      The cards are absolutely positioned, so they fill their parent. The parent
      must carry explicit size — `max-w-lg aspect-[4/3]` on the deck. Previously
      the deck sat inside a track that owned the height, and the deck itself had
      none. Deck now measures 512x384 (was 448x336).
    TAILWIND_OFFSETS_AND_FRAMER_Y_COMPOSE: >
      `rotate-[3deg] translate-x-4` compile to the DISCRETE CSS `rotate:` and
      `translate:` properties in Tailwind v4, while framer writes `transform:`.
      CSS applies translate -> rotate -> scale -> transform, so the resting fan
      and the animated y coexist instead of overwriting each other. This would
      NOT work if the offsets were transform utilities. Verified: card 1 reads
      rotate -4deg / translate -16px 16px while framer owns its transform.
      Corollary: reading getComputedStyle(el).transform tells you nothing about
      these offsets — read `rotate` and `translate`.
    track_is_lg_only: >
      lg:h-[200vh] and lg:sticky. Below lg the card column is not rendered, so a
      200vh track would be a second viewport of empty scroll on a phone.
      Verified: mobile section 855px, position relative, no dead scroll.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 16.1 kB / 195 kB.
      1440x820: section 1640 (200vh), sticky pinned at top 0 with height 820 and
      overflow hidden, background layer 820px / cover / no-repeat, deck 512x384,
      3 cards with correct rotations and gold /30 and /50 borders, all images
      loaded, headline 64.8px unwrapped, stats present.
      Pin holds through scroll 820 (= track minus one viewport) then releases,
      with ZERO overlap against the next section at any sampled position.
      375x812: relative, no track, headline 34.5px unwrapped, no overflow.
    NOT_verified: >
      THE PEEL ITSELF. framer's useScroll is rAF-driven and rAF does not advance
      in this pane — every sampled scroll position returned y 0 / opacity 1.
      Architecture, pin, rest state and degradation are verified; the animation
      is not. GSAP's ScrollTrigger CAN be exercised here because Lenis updates
      it synchronously; framer cannot.

  - id: sticky-scroll-linked-cards
    date: 2026-08-07
    decision: >
      components/home/ScrollRevealCards.tsx — a 200vh scroll track with a
      sticky deck of three cards that unstack on scrollYProgress, via framer
      useScroll + useTransform. Replaces EventShowcaseStack.tsx (deleted).
    status: current
    THE_HERO_IS_NOW_TWO_VIEWPORTS_TALL: >
      Widest consequence. The section went from 820px to 1975px at 1440x820,
      because a sticky scroll-linked reveal needs the scroll distance to exist.
      The stats bar now sits ~2 viewports down. This is inherent to the effect,
      not a bug — but the "hero fits exactly one viewport" property that held
      for most of this project is gone on desktop. Told to the user.
    TWO_LAYOUT_CHANGES_THE_TRACK_FORCED: >
      Both in Hero.tsx, both required or the layout breaks:
        · grid is `lg:items-start`, not items-center. A centred row parks the
          short left column in the middle of a 200vh cell, putting the headline
          a full viewport below the fold.
        · the left column is `lg:sticky lg:top-32`, so the copy stays with the
          cards instead of scrolling away and leaving them stranded.
    REST_STATE_IS_THE_DESIGN: >
      At scrollYProgress 0 the deck reads as a correct static stack —
      y 0/24/48, scale 1/0.92/0.84, all opaque. Deliberate: if the scroll never
      progresses (reduced motion, short viewport, collapsed track, framer
      failing) what remains is the intended composition rather than an empty or
      half-peeled deck. That is why no media-query gating is needed.
    mobile: >
      Track height is `lg:h-[200vh]` only, and the column is hidden below lg —
      measured trackH 0 on a phone, so no dead scroll. Verified.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 16.1 kB / 195 kB.
      1440x820: track 1640px (200vh), sticky at top 128px, left column sticky,
      grid align flex-start, 3 cards with captions 01 Fitness / 02 Corporate /
      03 Celebrations bottom-to-top, all three images loaded, section 1975px,
      no horizontal overflow.
      375x812: column display:none, track 0px, no section overlap, no overflow.
    NOT_verified: >
      THE SCROLL LINKAGE ITSELF. framer's useScroll is rAF-driven and rAF does
      not advance in this pane, so all five sampled scroll positions returned
      the identical rest state. Structure, rest state and degradation are
      verified; the peel is not. GSAP's ScrollTrigger COULD be exercised here
      (lenis.on('scroll', ScrollTrigger.update) makes it synchronous) — framer
      cannot. If this needs proving without a human, it would have to be
      rewritten on ScrollTrigger.

  - id: reveal-hook-and-stats-footer
    date: 2026-08-07
    decision: >
      components/ui/Reveal.tsx — IntersectionObserver scroll reveal, no
      animation library — wrapping the hero card stack. And
      components/home/StatsFooter.tsx, extracted from Hero.tsx.
    status: current
    REUSED_THE_EXISTING_OBSERVER: >
      Reveal uses lib/hooks useInViewOnce rather than a new
      useIntersectionObserver. It is already the project's useRef + useEffect IO
      wrapper, used by the section dividers and icon draws; a second one would
      just be a second place for the rootMargin to drift.
    A_REVEAL_MUST_FAIL_VISIBLE: >
      Two guards, both load-bearing:
        · `armed` is false during SSR and the first client render, so the markup
          leaving the server is the VISIBLE state. Rendering opacity-0 directly
          means content invisible forever without JS, or if hydration fails.
        · a 2.5s timeout reveals regardless. useInViewOnce covers a MISSING
          IntersectionObserver but not one that exists and never fires.
      This is the third time this session that an animation could have stranded
      content invisible. Treat "what does this look like if the animation never
      runs" as a required question, not a nicety.
    INLINE_STYLE_NOT_UTILITY_CLASSES: >
      opacity/transform are set inline, not via `opacity-0 translate-y-10`.
      The class version was tried and the element carried the classes while its
      computed opacity stayed 1 — an identical probe div elsewhere in the same
      document resolved correctly, and the cause was never established. Inline
      wins unconditionally, so the reveal cannot be defeated by it.
    DIAGNOSTIC_TRAP_THAT_COST_THE_MOST: >
      This pane does not advance CSS transitions. A transitioning element
      therefore reports its computed value FROZEN AT THE START, which reads
      exactly like a broken stylesheet: inline style says opacity 0, computed
      says 1, and no rule appears to match. I chased that as "the dev server is
      not recompiling CSS" and was wrong — a production build had every utility.
      When inline style and computed style disagree here, suspect a frozen
      transition before suspecting the CSS.
    stats_footer: >
      Own component, server-rendered, no state. bg-[#1a0b2e] with
      border-y border-[#d4af37]/20; 1 col -> sm:2 -> lg:4 with divide-y swapping
      to divide-x. 44px gold rings, Lucide icons positional and aria-hidden.
      Contrast on the solid bar: labels #a8a0b5 on #1a0b2e measure 7.40:1,
      gold numbers 8.85:1 — both clear AA, and the solid ground means the
      background artwork no longer bleeds under the smallest text on the page.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 16 kB / 195 kB.
      Built CSS contains .opacity-0, .translate-y-10, .duration-1000, divide-x,
      #1a0b2e and #a8a0b5 — checked in the real 83 kB chunk, not the first file
      found (an arbitrary 17 kB chunk reported them all missing).
      Reveal: transition 1s cubic-bezier(0,0,0.2,1); failsafe observed flipping
      the inline style to `opacity: 1; transform: none`.
      Stats: bg rgb(26,11,46), 4 equal 356px columns, 44x44 rings, 4 icons,
      numbers #d4af37, labels #a8a0b5 at 10px. Section exactly 820 at 1440x820,
      no horizontal overflow.

  - id: static-card-stack-and-stats-bar
    date: 2026-08-07
    decision: >
      EventShowcaseStack rewritten as a STATIC 3-layer stack per a written spec
      (two bare cards behind one photo card). The stats bar became a 4-col grid
      with gold ring icons and a double gold rule. All GSAP in the hero is gone.
    status: current
    THE_PIN_IS_GONE: >
      Widest consequence, easy to miss. EventShowcaseStack owned the only
      ScrollTrigger left in the hero, and it pinned the section for +110% of
      viewport. The spec defines the stack as fixed Tailwind transforms
      (rotate-[-4deg], translate-x-[15px] ...), and GSAP writing `transform` on
      those same elements overwrites every one on its first frame — the two
      cannot both own that property. So the scrub went, and with it the pin,
      which only ever existed to give the scrub room. The hero now scrolls
      normally, the page is ~900px shorter, and there is no .pin-spacer.
      contracts.pin_needs_a_block_parent still applies if a pin ever returns.
      The cursor tilt went with it.
    TAILWIND_V4_USES_DISCRETE_TRANSFORMS: >
      getComputedStyle(el).transform reads "none" on these cards even though the
      rotate/translate ARE applied — Tailwind v4 emits the separate `rotate:`
      and `translate:` CSS properties. Read those, or measure the rect. Reading
      `transform` will convince you the classes failed when they did not.
    stats_bar: >
      `divide-*` draws rules only BETWEEN children, so the 2-col mobile grid
      gets its vertical rule free and the horizontal one is suppressed at lg.
      Icons are positional (CalendarDays / PartyPopper / Smile / Globe), decorative
      and aria-hidden — they carry nothing the label does not already say.
      Labels are text-gray-300 at 9px, the smallest text on the page: measured
      9.54:1 over the bar's worst-case ground, gold numbers 6.69:1.
    CARDS_ARE_DESKTOP_ONLY_STILL: >
      The spec's `mt-12 lg:mt-0` implies the stack should also show on mobile,
      but the column wrapper is `hidden lg:block` and showing it would add
      ~384px to a hero that currently measures exactly 812 on a 375x812 phone.
      Left desktop-only and flagged to the user with that number; it is a
      one-line change to `block` if they want it.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 15.9 kB / 194 kB (from 16.3).
      1440: stack 448x336, three layers reading rotate -4deg/translate -15px
      15px, rotate 3deg/translate 15px 5px, and none — exactly as specified.
      Stack clears the headline by 178px. Stats 4 equal 356px columns, 4 rings,
      double rule present, no pin-spacer, section exactly 820.
      1024: stack 425x318 inside a 425px column, 19px clearance to the headline,
      labels wrap to two lines as expected, section exactly 768.
      375: stats fall to 2 cols at 187.5px, rings 56px, section exactly 812,
      no section overlap, no horizontal overflow at any width.

  - id: seam-was-a-divider-monogram-removed
    date: 2026-08-07
    decision: >
      Removed the 1px column divider from the hero's right cell, and removed the
      SA monogram entirely (components/home/HeroMonogram.tsx deleted). The
      background image layer was NOT changed.
    status: current
    THE_SEAM_WAS_NOT_THE_IMAGE: >
      Reported as "a harsh vertical seam where the background image is
      repeating". It was not repeating — the layer was already bg-no-repeat,
      and sampling the artwork column-by-column found no discontinuity (largest
      column-to-column jump 9 against a mean luminance of 47.7, i.e. ornament,
      not an edge).
      The seam was a leftover STRUCTURAL RULE: a 1px x 542px white gradient
      span at -left-4 in the right grid cell, sitting exactly on the column
      boundary at x=713. Subtle against a plain gradient, a hard seam against
      an ornate background.
      HOW IT WAS FOUND: query the section for every element with width <= 4px
      and height >= 120px. That returns thin vertical elements directly and is
      far faster than reasoning about background-repeat.
    monogram_gone: >
      HeroMonogram.tsx deleted. The owner's artwork already carries the gold
      linework the monogram was providing, so the two clashed. Its scroll-scrub
      logic and the "only one trigger may pin" note now apply only to
      EventShowcaseStack.
    background_untouched: >
      bg-[url('/bg-pattern.jpg')] bg-cover bg-center bg-no-repeat z-0
      opacity-25 mix-blend-lighten — verified unchanged and already exactly the
      requested class set.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 16.3 kB / 195 kB (down from 17.2).
      ZERO thin vertical elements remain in the section. Monogram absent.
      Background repeat no-repeat, size cover, position 50% 50%, z-0,
      opacity 0.25, blend lighten. Content wrapper relative z-10. Headline
      unwrapped, 3 cards visible, section exactly 820 at 1440x820, no
      horizontal overflow.

  - id: owner-supplied-bg-pattern
    date: 2026-08-07
    decision: >
      The hero background is the OWNER'S OWN ARTWORK at public/bg-pattern.jpg —
      z-0, bg-cover, bg-center, opacity-25, mix-blend-lighten. All generated
      ornament is gone: components/home/HeroDamask.tsx deleted, and with it the
      last of five SVG background attempts.
    status: current
    THE_JPG_IS_BUILD_CRITICAL: >
      Tailwind compiles the arbitrary background-image class into a real CSS
      url(). If public/bg-pattern.jpg is missing the STYLESHEET fails to resolve
      ("Module not found ... ./app/globals.css") and EVERY route 500s — a dead
      site, not a missing background. Never delete it without changing the class
      in the same commit.
    IT_IS_A_JPEG_ON_PURPOSE: >
      The supplied file was bg-pattern.png at 8.5 MB / 2948x1408 — an absurd
      payload for a decorative layer, and almost certainly why the owner asked
      for ".jpg". Converted with sharp: resize width 2400, jpeg quality 82,
      mozjpeg -> 534 kB, a 94% reduction. Re-run those exact settings if the
      artwork is ever replaced. The 8.5 MB PNG was deleted.
    OPACITY_IS_25_NOT_30: >
      Measured against the BRIGHTEST pixel in the artwork (211,167,143)
      composited through mix-blend-lighten onto the #2a0f4c gradient:
        30 -> body 4.48, gold 4.36  FAILS AA
        25 -> body 4.85, gold 4.88  passes
        20 -> body 5.23, gold 5.45  passes with more margin
      25 is the brightest value that stays legible. The owner pre-authorised
      dropping to 20. If the ARTWORK is ever changed, re-run this measurement —
      the safe opacity depends entirely on the image's brightest region.
    how_to_measure: >
      Draw the image to a canvas, find the max-luminance pixel, apply
      max(pixel, gradient) for lighten, composite at the layer opacity, then
      ratio the text colours against that. Guessing an opacity here is how the
      first attempt shipped under AA.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 17.2 kB / 196 kB.
      Layer resolves url(bg-pattern.jpg), bg-cover, 50% 50%, no-repeat, z-0,
      opacity 0.25, mix-blend lighten, pointer-events none. Content wrapper
      relative z-10. 3 cards visible. Section gradient intact as fallback.
      No generated SVG remains in the hero background.

  - id: damask-restored-from-screenshot
    date: 2026-08-07
    decision: >
      components/home/HeroDamask.tsx RESTORED and remounted at z-[2],
      opacity 0.16, mix-blend-soft-light. The bg-pattern.png image layer stays
      at z-0 underneath it.
    status: current
    THE_DAMASK_IS_THE_ONE_THEY_WANT: >
      This exact layer was built, then DELETED when the user described it as
      "the current faint, repeating cross-pattern", then asked for again by
      screenshot ("i want this for the hero section") — the screenshot was of
      the state this component produced.
      Four background treatments were tried in between (central rangoli,
      bilateral ogee, image-only) and all were rejected. Do not remove this a
      third time without an instruction that names the component.
    why_this_kept_happening: >
      Every judgement about these backgrounds was made from the user's
      screenshots, because this pane composites nothing and no rendered frame
      was ever visible on my side. That is the root cause of the churn, not the
      artwork. For anything purely visual, ask for a screenshot before
      iterating rather than after.
    both_layers_coexist: >
      Image layer z-0 (still a transparent stub until the owner supplies
      bg-pattern.png), damask z-[2] above it. Dropping the real PNG in ADDS to
      the damask rather than replacing it — delete the single <HeroDamask/> line
      if the supplied artwork should stand alone.
    contrast_regression_caught: >
      Restoring the damask pushed the 10px stat labels to 4.39:1 worst-case,
      under AA. Raised white/50 -> white/60 (5.61). Re-measure those labels
      whenever a background layer is added; they are the tightest text on the
      hero.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 17.7 kB / 196 kB. No server errors.
      Damask 54 shapes, z-2, soft-light, opacity 0.16. Image layer present at
      z-0. Grid 632.5 + 632.5, 3 cards visible, headline unwrapped, 9 dust
      motes, no horizontal overflow.
    NOT_verified: >
      Appearance. Still no rendered frame seen on my side.

  - id: background-is-an-image-now
    date: 2026-08-07
    decision: >
      All generated SVG ornament for the hero background is DELETED —
      public/mandala.svg and scripts/make-mandala.mjs are gone. The background
      is a CSS image layer pointing at /public/bg-pattern.png, supplied by the
      site owner. Grid moved from 12-col (8/4) to lg:grid-cols-2.
    status: current
    DO_NOT_GENERATE_ORNAMENT_AGAIN: >
      Three attempts at generating a floral damask/mandala in raw SVG all
      failed — radial rings read as "rigid, symmetrical, geometric", and the
      bilateral rewrite read as "random stringy lines". The user's verdict:
      "AI cannot generate complex floral mandala SVGs via raw code." They are
      right, and the deeper reason is that this pane composites nothing, so
      every iteration was made blind against a screenshot.
      If ornament is wanted, it comes in as an IMAGE FILE. Do not rebuild it.
    THE_PLACEHOLDER_PNG_MUST_NOT_BE_DELETED: >
      public/bg-pattern.png is a 70-byte 1x1 transparent PNG stub. It exists
      because Tailwind compiles the arbitrary background-image class into a real
      CSS url(): if the file is missing the STYLESHEET fails to resolve
      ("Module not found ... ./app/globals.css") and every route 500s — not a
      missing background, a dead site. That failure already happened twice.
      The owner overwrites this file with the real artwork; the path stays.
    grid_change_forced_a_headline_retune: >
      50/50 columns dropped the headline track from ~757px to ~640px at 1440,
      and ~587px to ~432px at 1024. "Creating Moments," runs 8.94x the font
      size, so the lg clamp went 5.9vw -> 4.5vw.
      The clamp FLOOR mattered as much as the slope: at 3rem the min won at
      1024 (4.5vw = 46px there) and forced 48px, which wrapped inside the mask.
      2.6rem keeps the vw term in control across the whole lg range.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 17.2 kB / 196 kB. No server errors.
      1440: grid 632.5px + 632.5px, headline 64.8px unwrapped (both line boxes
      equal), 3 cards visible with the 24/12/0 stagger intact, cards clear the
      headline by 130px, section exactly 820.
      1024: headline 46.08px, unwrapped, 13px of clearance, 3 cards, section 768.
      375: single column, headline 34.5px inside the gutters, pattern layer
      present, section exactly 812, no section overlap.
      Pattern div resolves url(bg-pattern.png), z-0, mix-blend-overlay,
      opacity 0.1. No horizontal overflow at any width.
    NOT_verified: >
      How the real artwork will look — the current file is a transparent stub,
      so the layer renders as nothing until the owner supplies the PNG.

  - id: damask-watermark-final
    date: 2026-08-07
    decision: >
      public/mandala.svg regenerated as a BILATERAL damask, and the hero <img>
      scaled to 2.8 with a 10%/-5% offset at opacity 0.08.
    status: current
    BILATERAL_NOT_RADIAL: >
      This is the third attempt at this artwork and the distinction is the whole
      point. Radial construction — concentric bands repeated around a centre —
      reads as "rigid, symmetrical, geometric" no matter how curved the
      individual forms are. Damask is BILATERAL: one half authored, mirrored
      across the vertical axis with translate(1000 0) scale(-1 1).
      24 sweeping Bézier paths: an ogee cartouche frame, spade leaves, volute
      swirls, filigree tendrils, axis finials. No circles, no radial repeat.
      Do not "improve" this by adding rings.
    scale_and_offset: >
      150vh base then scale-[2.8] = ~3444px at 1440x820, so only sweeps of the
      ogee cross the viewport — sprawling watermark, not a stamp. The 10%/-5%
      offset breaks dead-centre symmetry so it does not read as a target behind
      the headline.
      NOTE: getComputedStyle(img).transform reports "none" — Tailwind v4 emits
      the discrete `scale`/`translate` CSS properties, not `transform`. Verify
      by measuring the rect, not by reading that property.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 17.2 kB / 196 kB.
      Asset 2.3 kB / 24 paths, parses (img naturalWidth > 0).
      In page: renders 3444px, opacity 0.08, mix-blend soft-light, offset +123
      / -61 from section centre, bleeds all four edges, no horizontal overflow.
    NOT_verified: >
      How it actually looks. The pane composites nothing, so no frame of this
      artwork has ever been seen — three iterations have all been judged from
      the user's screenshots, not from my own view.

  - id: central-mandala-asset
    date: 2026-08-07
    decision: >
      components/home/HeroDamask.tsx DELETED. The tiled damask repeat was read
      as "a repeating cross-pattern"; it is replaced by ONE central medallion,
      a real file at public/mandala.svg rendered through an <img> in a
      z-0 flex-centred container inside Hero.tsx.
    status: current
    THE_ASSET_IS_GENERATED_BY_A_SCRIPT: >
      scripts/make-mandala.mjs writes public/mandala.svg — 199 shapes, 25 kB.
      Re-run `node scripts/make-mandala.mjs` to change band radii or petal
      counts. DO NOT hand-edit the SVG; it is generated output.
      Gold is baked into the file, so it cannot be recoloured from CSS. Change
      GOLD in the script and re-run.
      All cubic Bézier — petals, veins, volutes. A previous version of this
      artwork was radial spokes and star polygons and was rejected as too rigid.
    SIZED_IN_VH_NOT_VW: >
      The brief said w-[150vw]. At 1440x820 that is 2160px, which crops the
      medallion to its inner rosette and throws the entire outer crown
      off-screen sideways — zoomed, not massive. 150vh is 1230px there: the full
      ornament reads across the width while still bleeding off top and bottom,
      which is the stated goal. `max-w-none` is required or the base reset caps
      the img at 100%.
    HOW_TO_MEASURE_AN_SVG_YOU_CANNOT_SEE: >
      getBBox() IGNORES ancestor transforms. On a radially-built mandala every
      rotated petal reports a box around the origin, so the artwork looks
      broken and off-centre when it is fine. Use getBoundingClientRect(), and
      when the SVG is standalone remember it letterboxes: at 1280x720 a 1000-unit
      viewBox scales by 720/1000, not 1280/1000. Getting that wrong made a
      correct radius-460 mandala measure as 259.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 17.2 kB / 196 kB. No server errors.
      Asset: 199 shapes, zero zero-length, ink perfectly circular, radius 460 of
      a 500 half-viewBox.
      In page at 1280x720: img loads, renders 1080x1080, opacity 0.09,
      mix-blend soft-light, wrapper z-0 flex overflow-hidden, centred on both
      axes, bleeds top AND bottom, content grid z-10, tiled pattern gone.
      Contrast over the worst-case ground (57,29,74) — treating 9% gold as a
      NORMAL blend, which over-states soft-light: headline 14.44, body 6.11,
      stat labels 4.70, gold text 6.87. All pass AA.
      375x812: 1218px, bleeds all four edges, section exactly 812, no section
      overlap, no horizontal overflow.
    NOT_verified: >
      Whether it reads as "luxurious" at 9% under soft-light. The pane
      composites nothing, so no rendered frame has been seen — opacity is a
      single number to tune if it is too faint or too present.

  - id: damask-background-stack
    date: 2026-08-07
    decision: >
      components/home/HeroMandala.tsx DELETED — the radial starburst was
      rejected as "sharp, geometric ... too rigid". Replaced by
      components/home/HeroDamask.tsx, a seamless ornate damask, inside a five
      layer ground: gradient / orbs z-0, key light z-[1], damask z-[2], bokeh
      dust z-[3], monogram z-[4], content z-10.
      Base gradient is now #2a0f4c via #1a052e to #0d0216.
    status: current
    NO_DAMASK_IMAGE_EXISTS: >
      The brief specified a background-image utility pointing at
      /path-to-your-damask-image.png. No such asset was supplied and one cannot
      be invented (see the no-AI-images rule), so the ornament is a generated
      SVG <pattern>. Told to the user, with the swap path.
      Seamless BY CONSTRUCTION: one motif at the tile centre, one at the tile
      ORIGIN. The origin motif is quartered by the tile edge and the three
      neighbouring tiles supply the rest, so no half-drop offset is needed.
      All cubic Bézier — acanthus leaves, volute scrollwork, a six-petal
      rosette. That is the point: curves, not the spokes and star polygons that
      were rejected.
    blend_choice: >
      mix-blend-soft-light, not overlay. Overlay pushes the gold toward white
      over the lighter end of the gradient and the ornament starts reading as
      grey haze; soft-light keeps it warm.
    key_light_is_left_anchored: >
      The z-[1] glow sits at left-[8%], not centre. Its job is to lift the
      headline off the ground — centred, most of it falls behind the card stack
      instead.
    dust_is_authored: >
      Nine motes at fixed positions. NOT random: a re-roll on every render
      flickers on any state change, and module-scope randomness differs between
      server and client and trips hydration. Also no will-change — they never
      animate, and promoting nine blurred layers for nothing costs memory.
    TWO_FAILURES_WORTH_KEEPING: >
      Both cost a 500 and both are now contracts:
        · never_put_a_class_in_a_comment — the "how to swap in a PNG" comment
          contained a real background-image utility and broke globals.css.
        · stop_the_dev_server_before_deleting_next — deleting .next live
          corrupted the webpack cache, after which the server reported an error
          from a file that no longer contained the code.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 17.7 kB / 196 kB. No server errors.
      Gradient computes rgb(42,15,76) -> rgb(26,5,46) -> rgb(13,2,22).
      Damask: pattern present, 220x220 tile, 54 shapes, z-2, soft-light,
      opacity 0.16. Key light z-1. Dust z-3, 9 motes. Starburst gone. 3 cards
      still present. No horizontal overflow.
    NOT_verified: >
      Frame rate, reduced motion, and whether the damask reads as "luxurious"
      at 16% under soft-light — the pane composites nothing, so no rendered
      frame of it has been seen.

  - id: rangoli-and-staggered-stack
    date: 2026-08-07
    decision: >
      components/home/HeroMandala.tsx — a generated gold rangoli at z-0, 120vw,
      opacity 0.08. And EventShowcaseStack's resting layout changed from
      rotations to a tight 24/12/0 down-and-right stagger with z-10/20/30,
      landscape 16:10 cards, gold/40 border.
    status: current
    MANDALA_IS_GENERATED_NOT_DRAWN: >
      Every ring, petal, spoke and dot comes out of a helper in an 800x800 box,
      so the symmetry is exact and counts are tuned in one place. 77 shapes, all
      valid geometry. If it needs to be denser or sparser, change the counts —
      do not hand-edit path data back into it.
      Its stroke SCALES with the artwork, deliberately the opposite of the
      monogram and the icons: at 2.16x its viewBox, a non-scaling 1px hairline
      would all but vanish at 8% opacity.
    TAILWIND_TRANSLATE_CANNOT_BE_USED_ON_THE_CARDS: >
      The brief specified `translate-y-6 translate-x-6` etc. Those classes would
      be silently overwritten — GSAP owns `transform` on the cards for the
      scroll fan and rewrites it every frame. The offsets are therefore GSAP
      values in the rest state, not utilities. Same trap as the CSS-keyframe /
      Tailwind-rotate collision noted elsewhere in this file.
    TWO_ELEMENTS_PER_CARD: >
      A shell and a card, and the split is load-bearing. The SHELL centres with
      grid and carries the z-index and must NEVER receive a transform — the
      moment it does, the card's offsets stop being measured from centre. The
      CARD is the only thing GSAP writes to, so its transform is purely stagger
      plus fan. Centring the card itself with -translate-1/2 would have been
      erased on GSAP's first frame.
    stagger_is_derived: >
      step = clamp(6, round(cardWidth * 0.034), 12), giving the specified
      24/12/0 at the design width and scaling itself down automatically —
      measured 16/8 at 1024 where the card is 234px. That is what makes the
      stack responsive rather than just smaller.
    TWO_GOLD_WATERMARKS_NOW_OVERLAP: >
      The rangoli (z-0, centred, 8%) and the SA monogram (z-[1], right 54%,
      30% stroke) both sit behind the same content and cover overlapping area.
      Flagged to the user; neither was removed because both were explicitly
      asked for. If the background ever reads as muddy, the monogram is the one
      to drop — it is the later, smaller and more incidental of the two.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 17.5 kB / 196 kB.
      Mandala: 77 shapes, zero zero-length, z-0, opacity 0.08, 1728px square at
      1440, bleeds off BOTH edges, no horizontal overflow.
      Stack at 1440: labels 01/Production, 02/Corporate, 03/Celebrations with
      Celebrations on the face; z 10/20/30; all three 353x221, aspect 1.60,
      landscape and identical; offsets exactly 24/24, 12/12, 0/0; border
      gold at 0.4; all three shells transform:none. Scroll fan still resolves
      (tz -190 / 0 / 60 at scroll end).
      Contrast over the mandala ground (43,23,48): headline 16.56, body 6.70,
      stat labels 5.07, gold text 7.88 — all pass AA.
      1024: stagger 16/8, cards clear the headline, section exactly 768.
      375: mandala still bleeds both edges, card column display:none, section
      exactly 812, no section overlap, no horizontal overflow.
    NOT_verified: >
      Frame rate, and the reduced-motion branch. This pane advances neither rAF
      nor CSS animations, so nothing has been seen moving.

  - id: hero-atmosphere-and-tilt
    date: 2026-08-05
    decision: >
      Four additions to the hero: two drifting ambient orbs at z-0, a GSAP
      parallax layer on the monogram vectors, a cursor-driven 3D tilt on the
      card stack, and a line-by-line clip-path headline reveal. MagneticButton
      removed from the hero CTA. Background switched to #1c0a2f -> #0d0417.
    status: current
    REVEAL_IS_CSS_ON_PURPOSE: >
      The brief said "Framer Motion or GSAP". It is neither, and the reason
      matters more than the letter.
      A JS-driven reveal means the hidden state is CSS and the REVEAL is JS, so
      the <h1> is invisible until an animation library loads and runs. That is a
      catastrophic failure mode for the one element that must be readable — and
      it happened during development: GSAP loaded, the watchdog cancelled
      itself, the tween never rendered, and the headline sat clipped at t=35s.
      As a CSS keyframe with `fill-mode: both`, the hidden state and the reveal
      ship in the SAME stylesheet. There is no partial-failure window: sheet
      loads, both apply; sheet fails, neither does and the text is visible.
      PROVEN both ways — forced to its end the clip resolves to the full
      rectangle; with data-motion removed the clip is `none`.
      Do not "modernise" this back onto GSAP.
    stagger_comes_from_a_css_var: >
      --line-delay per line, because the offset has to clear the opening
      sequence, which is 1.6s on a fresh load and 0.1s on a client-side return.
      Verified as 1.90s / 2.02s — the specified 0.12 stagger.
    TILT_GOES_ON_A_WRAPPER: >
      Never on the cards. Their transforms are owned by the scroll timeline, and
      writing rotateX/rotateY onto the same elements has the two animations
      overwrite each other frame by frame. The wrapper tilts, the cards fan
      inside it — and the wrapper needs `transform-style: preserve-3d` or it
      flattens the cards into its own plane and their translateZ collapses.
      Touch gating is a `(pointer: fine)` matchMedia CONDITION, so on a phone
      the handler is never attached rather than attached and ignored. Verified:
      pointer-fine false on mobile, listener does not fire.
    parallax_inside_a_pin: >
      The section is pinned for the whole range, so nothing drifts with the page
      on its own — depth has to be produced as differential motion inside the
      pin. The vector layer travels -44 -> 44 across the scrub while the cards
      move a few hundred px. It sits on its own element because the root's
      opacity is animated, and stacking a transform onto the element GSAP is
      fading invites the two tweens to fight over the style attribute.
    orbs: >
      Coprime 26s/37s. Positioned with plain offsets and NO Tailwind transform
      utility — the keyframes set `transform` outright and would drop one.
    background: >
      #1c0a2f -> #0d0417 as specified. I had flagged that these sit outside the
      plum/plum-deep token family the footer and vertical heroes key to; asked
      for twice, so they are the values. Re-check the seam if the footer is
      ever revisited.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 16.8 kB / 195 kB.
      Headline: 2 lines, animation smaya-line-reveal, delays 1.90s/2.02s,
      fill-mode both; end state resolves to the full rect; no-data-motion state
      is unclipped.
      Tilt: perspective(1000px) written into the transform, preserve-3d on the
      wrapper, will-change transform, listener fires on desktop.
      Parallax layer present with will-change transform.
      Orbs: 2, correct animation names and durations.
      No MagneticButton anywhere in the hero; CTA intact.
      Mobile 375x812: pointer-fine false, tilt listener silent, headline still
      animates, no pin-spacer, hero exactly 812, no overflow.
    NOT_verified: >
      Frame rate. Note this pane advances NEITHER rAF nor CSS animations, so no
      reveal, tilt or drift has been seen moving — only proven correct by
      forcing end states and reading computed style.

  - id: hero-monogram-watermark
    date: 2026-08-05
    decision: >
      components/home/HeroMonogram.tsx — six traced monogram strokes plus three
      registration dots that scatter and assemble on the same scroll range as
      the card stack. Mounted in Hero.tsx at z-[1], right 54%, lg only.
    status: current
    THE_TRACE_IS_AN_APPROXIMATION: >
      The paths are a HAND-TRACE of public/logo/smaya-monogram.png — the
      interlocking S and A, authored in a 0..512 box so coordinates map 1:1 onto
      that 512px source and can be re-checked against it.
      There is NO vector original in the repo; the supplied logo is raster only
      (PNG + JPEG). At 15-30% opacity behind a card stack it reads as the mark,
      but it is not the official outline. If an .svg/.ai/.eps ever arrives,
      replace these paths outright rather than nudging them by eye — and say so
      to the user, who asked for something "matching the logo mark".
    ONLY_ONE_TRIGGER_MAY_PIN: >
      EventShowcaseStack already pins this section. HeroMonogram deliberately
      does NOT pin — it shares the same trigger/start/end with scrub:true and no
      pin. Two ScrollTriggers pinning one element fight: the second computes its
      pin-spacer against a document the first has already resized.
      Because both are pure functions of the same scroll range they stay in
      lockstep by construction. VERIFIED in lockstep at 0/300/600/902 down and
      up. If a third layer is ever added here, it must not pin either.
    VECTOR_EFFECT_DOES_NOT_INHERIT: >
      Second time this exact bug appeared (see lucide-practice-lattice).
      `vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"` on the parent <g> silently does
      nothing — it must be on each SHAPE. On the group, the 512 viewBox scaled
      the stroke to 2.25px against the 1.5px asked for. Always verify by
      computing viewBox scale x specified width, never by reading
      getComputedStyle().strokeWidth, which reports the SPECIFIED value.
    legibility: >
      A left-edge mask fades the layer out below 38% of its width, reaching full
      opacity at x=948 while the headline ends at 918 — 30px clear. The faintest
      ink does still cross the headline band at ~9% effective opacity; measured
      there, white text is 13.2:1. It is a watermark, not a legibility cost.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 16.5 kB / 195 kB.
      9 animated parts, 9 geometry elements, none zero-length.
      Rendered stroke 1.5px (was 2.25px before the vector-effect fix).
      Scrub proven by scrollTo + a synchronous 'scroll' event: part 0 goes
      tx -221 / rot -26 / opacity 0.5 -> tx 0 / rot 0 / opacity 1, and the cards
      report identical values at the same positions. Scrolling up restores both.
      Mobile 375x812: layer display:none, no pin-spacer, hero exactly 812, no
      section overlap, no horizontal overflow.
    NOT_verified: >
      Frame rate, and the reduced-motion branch — this pane cannot emulate the
      media query. Also NOT verified: that the assembled trace actually looks
      like the logo. The geometry lands at its authored coordinates, but no
      frame of the rendered mark has been seen.

  - id: event-showcase-stack
    date: 2026-08-05
    decision: >
      components/home/EventBlueprintAnimation.tsx is DELETED. The hero's right
      column now holds components/home/EventShowcaseStack.tsx — three
      photography cards, tightly stacked at rest, fanned apart on the same
      scroll-scrubbed pinned trigger. `.icon-hairline` removed from globals.css
      with it.
    status: current
    why: >
      "The current SVG icon node grid looks like a generic tech flowchart and
      completely fails as a luxury event brand visual." Seven line-art glyphs
      joined by connectors read as a systems diagram — wrong register for an
      events brand. Photography does what a diagram cannot.
    UNSPLASH_IDS_ARE_REUSED_ON_PURPOSE: >
      The three photo IDs already appear elsewhere on this site (celebrations,
      corporate and entertainment vertical heroes), so they are known to
      resolve and are covered by the existing images.unsplash.com entry in
      next.config.mjs. Do NOT invent plausible-looking Unsplash IDs — that ships
      404s that only surface in production. Optimizer verified: 200, 8.2 kB.
    NOTHING_MAY_TRAVEL_LEFT: >
      There are only ~32px of gutter between the headline's right edge (918 at
      1440) and this column (starts 950). The top card was authored drifting
      left at x:-34 and its rotated, scaled box reached x=905 — measured 13x174px
      of overlap WITH THE HEADLINE. It now drifts right (x:30). Any future card
      motion here must be rightward, and must be re-swept against the copy.
    image_loading: >
      `loading="eager"`, not next/image's default lazy: these sit in the hero
      viewport on desktop and lazy gates them behind an IntersectionObserver, so
      the stack can fade in before its photography arrives. NOT `priority` — the
      headline is LCP and should not share preload bandwidth.
      `sizes="(min-width:1024px) 340px, 1px"`. The 340px matches the card's real
      325px render (30vw asked for 432px, a third too big). The `1px` fallback
      is what stops phones paying for a display:none column — verified, mobile
      fetches the w=16 candidate, a few hundred bytes.
    perspective_is_per_card: >
      `transformPerspective` is set on each card, NOT `perspective` on the
      container. A container perspective shares one vanishing point across all
      three, so the outer cards skew toward the middle instead of each turning
      about its own centre.
      Cards are also rendered bottom-first so DOM order IS paint order — no
      z-index, because a z-index on a 3D-transformed element creates a stacking
      context that breaks the translateZ depth sort.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 15.7 kB / 194 kB.
      3 cards, correct labels, empty alts, all three images load (384w candidate
      for a 325px card).
      Scrub proven by scrollTo + a synchronous 'scroll' event: at rest all three
      share one transform (tight stack); at scroll-end card 03 sits at
      translateZ -190 scale 0.85, card 02 at scale 1.06, card 01 lifted 108px
      with translateZ 60. matrix3d confirms real 3D. Scrolling up restored the
      exact pristine stack.
      Collision swept every 50px across the whole 902px scrub: ZERO overlap with
      the headline, CTA or stats bar. Cards clear the headline by 59px.
      Mobile 375x812: column display:none, no pin-spacer, hero exactly 812, no
      section overlap, no horizontal overflow.
    NOT_verified: >
      Frame rate, and the reduced-motion branch — this pane cannot emulate the
      media query.

  - id: lucide-practice-lattice
    date: 2026-08-05
    decision: >
      The hand-authored SVG icons in components/home/EventBlueprintAnimation.tsx
      are replaced by seven lucide-react components, and the circular mandala by
      a 2-3-2 lattice with twelve self-drawing connectors.
      Briefcase / Music / Users / Gem (centre) / Mountain / HeartPulse /
      BookOpen. globals.css gained `.icon-hairline`.
    status: current
    why: >
      "The current SVG animation looks cheap and generic because the raw SVG
      paths generated are too basic ... the previous circular astrology chart
      layout was ineffective." lucide-react 0.544 was already a dependency; no
      install was needed.
    HAIRLINE_STROKE_IS_NOT_JUST_A_PROP: >
      The single thing that made this look cheap, and the easiest to get wrong.
      Lucide draws into a 24-unit viewBox. Render one at 56px and its stroke is
      scaled 2.33x; the outer SVG's viewBox scales it AGAIN (0.79 here). A
      strokeWidth of 1.25 therefore measured 2.31px on screen — chunky.
      Lucide's `absoluteStrokeWidth` does NOT fix it: that prop only divides
      through by its own `size` prop, and is bypassed entirely if you pass
      `width`/`height` instead of `size`, which nesting inside an SVG invites.
      The fix is `.icon-hairline * { vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke }`, which
      lifts stroke width out of the transform chain: 1.25 means 1.25 CSS px at
      every viewport and at every point of the scatter's scale tween.
      VERIFY BY COMPUTING, not by reading getComputedStyle().strokeWidth —
      that returns the SPECIFIED value and will happily report "1.25px" while
      the screen shows 2.31.
    COLOUR_TWEENS_VIA_currentColor: >
      Lucide's root svg carries `stroke="currentColor"`, so the timeline tweens
      `color` on the animated group and the whole glyph follows. Tweening
      `stroke` there does nothing — it never reaches the paths.
    layout: >
      2-3-2 lattice, symmetric about both axes, which suits a portrait column
      far better than the ring did (the ring pushed everything to the edges and
      left a hole in the middle). Twelve connectors: rails, centre spokes and
      side-to-corner braces.
      Connectors STOP SHORT of both icons — `link()` insets each end by that
      node's own half-size plus 12, so the larger centre icon pushes its
      connectors back further. Without the inset the middle rail runs straight
      through the Gem.
    scatter: >
      Icons start LARGER (scale 1.18-1.44) and at opacity 0.2-0.5, per the
      brief, then settle to scale 1 / opacity 1. Authored, not randomised, so
      no two start on top of each other and their paths cross on the way in.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 16.4 kB / 195 kB.
      Icons are genuinely the library — class="lucide lucide-briefcase" etc,
      stroke="currentColor", 7 nested svgs, 12 connectors, none zero-length.
      Rendered stroke measured 1.25px (was 2.31px before the fix), with
      vector-effect confirmed as non-scaling-stroke.
      Scrub proven by scrollTo + a synchronous 'scroll' event: rotations
      -24/31/-37/18/27/-30/22 -> all 0, scales 1.18-1.44 -> all 1, opacities
      0.2-0.5 -> all 1, connector dash 96 -> 0, gold 0 -> 1 -> 7. Scrolling up
      returned IDENTICAL values at the same scroll positions.
      Assembled: worst pairwise icon overlap NULL, and zero overlap with the
      headline, CTA or stats bar at 1440x820. No horizontal overflow.
    NOT_verified: >
      Frame rate, and the reduced-motion branch — this pane cannot emulate the
      media query.

  - id: practice-mandala
    date: 2026-08-04
    decision: >
      The floor-plan geometry in components/home/EventBlueprintAnimation.tsx is
      GONE. It now draws Smaya's seven practices as line-art icons that scatter
      and assemble into a mandala — six orbiting a seventh. The hero background
      reverted from flat obsidian to the house plum ramp.
    status: current
    why: >
      "The current scroll-scrubbed animation uses basic circle and rect elements
      that look like mechanical gears and boxes. It does not look like event
      management." Plus: the obsidian background was called a regression.
    BACKGROUND_IS_THE_HOUSE_RAMP: >
      bg-[linear-gradient(150deg,#141318,#231F36,#2E1A47,#3D1F5C)] — the ORIGINAL
      hero stops, ending on smaya-plum. The user offered a two-stop
      `from-[#1c0a2f] to-[#0d0417]`; that reads purple but sits outside the token
      family and would seam against the footer and the vertical heroes, which are
      keyed to plum / plum-deep. Told to them.
      The obsidian never leaked site-wide — it was one line in Hero.tsx.
      HeroBackdrop.tsx remains ORPHANED (blooms, grid, grain, particles). The
      colour came back; the noise did not, per the earlier "no background noise".
      Restore that file's component if the full atmosphere is ever wanted.
    icons: >
      Seven, one per practice, all authored in the SAME local 0..100 box and
      centred in it, so one `translate(-50 -50)` places any of them on any node
      with no per-icon nudging. Celebrations = interlocking bands under a cut
      solitaire (centre node), then Corporate = mic on a cradle, Entertainment =
      hung mirrorball with latitude chords, Adventure = ridge line above a guyed
      tent, Fitness = kettlebell with a hollow handle, Community = three figures,
      Workshops = open book.
    THREE_NESTED_GROUPS_ARE_LOAD_BEARING: >
      placement (translate) > data-shape (the ONLY group GSAP writes) > sizing
      (scale + recentre). Collapsing these breaks two things at once: GSAP would
      overwrite the placement transform, and `scatter` x/y would silently be
      scaled by the icon's own scale instead of being plain viewBox units.
    composition: >
      Orbit r=175 about (260,300). Guide rings at r=118 and r=232 FRAME the
      icons rather than crossing them — 118 clears the centre icon (half-height
      33) and sits inside the orbit's inner edge (141); 232 sits outside its
      outer edge (209). Both clear by 23. If icon scale changes, re-check.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean 21/21. Home 16.4 kB / 195 kB.
      38 paths, ZERO zero-length — every icon's path data is valid geometry.
      Stroke 1.6px, non-scaling, so it stays crisp through the scatter scale.
      Scrub proven by driving scrollTo + a synchronous 'scroll' event:
      rotations -18/-28/34/-22/30/-35/26 -> all 0, scales 0.5..0.72 -> all 1,
      armature dash 742 -> 0, gold count 0 -> 1 -> 7. Scrolling up returned
      IDENTICAL values at the same scroll positions.
      All seven ARE gold at scroll-end — the earlier "three gold, three not"
      timing bug did not recur.
      Assembled mandala: worst pairwise icon overlap is NULL (none overlap),
      extents x 77..444 and y 99..502 inside the 520x600 canvas.
      Zero overlap between the mandala and the headline, the CTA or the stats
      bar at 1440x820.
      Mobile 375x812: column is display:none, no pin-spacer, hero still exactly
      812, no horizontal overflow.
    NOT_verified: >
      Frame rate, and the reduced-motion branch — this pane cannot emulate the
      media query.

  - id: event-blueprint-layer
    date: 2026-08-04
    status_note: SUPERSEDED by blueprint-replaces-practice-list — the blueprint
      is no longer a full-bleed background layer and its legibility mask is gone
      with it (it now lives in its own column and overlaps no text).
    decision: >
      components/home/EventBlueprintAnimation.tsx — a scroll-scrubbed top-down
      floor plan that assembles behind the hero copy. Added to Hero.tsx as ONE
      line plus an import; no layout, headline, practice list or stats markup
      was touched.
    status: current
    THE_HERO_IS_NOW_PINNED: >
      This is the change with the widest blast radius, and it is easy to miss.
      On >=1024px the hero is pinned for +110% of viewport height, so the page
      is ~790px taller and ServicesGrid does not begin until the pin releases.
      A .pin-spacer element now wraps the hero <section>. Anything that measures
      the hero's document position must account for it.
      Requested explicitly ("Pin the hero section for the duration"). Not pinned
      below 1024px — pinning a touch scroller against Lenis with
      syncTouch:false fights native momentum.
    why_it_works_with_lenis: >
      lib/lenis.ts already does the required wiring — `lenis.on('scroll',
      ScrollTrigger.update)` plus a gsap.ticker-driven raf. Without that a pin
      jitters badly. Do not remove it.
      PageTransition also drops its resting clip-path, so ScrollTrigger's
      default pinType:"fixed" is safe here; a resting clip-path would have made
      <main> a containing block and broken the pin.
    three_behaviours: >
      Chosen by gsap.matchMedia, which also handles teardown on unmount:
        · prefers-reduced-motion — renders assembled and static, NO ScrollTrigger
        · <1024px — scrubs over the section's own range, no pin
        · >=1024px — scrubs and pins
    LEGIBILITY_MASK: >
      The layer carries a vertical mask fading it out above 14% and below 72%.
      Measured reason: a 0.22 gold stroke over #0D0D0E lifts the ground to
      L=0.031, which takes the 10px `text-white/50` stat labels from 5.33:1 to
      4.51:1 — still AA but with no margin. The mask clears the stats bar and
      the header outright instead of relying on it. Do not remove it without
      re-measuring those labels.
    layering: >
      z-[1] — above HeroBackdrop (unlayered), below the z-10 content grid which
      contains the headline, CTAs, practice index AND the stats bar. Verified by
      hit-test: headline, stat value and practice link all receive the pointer,
      never the SVG.
    geometry_is_real: >
      22 drawable elements, total path length 8176 user units, zero
      zero-length paths. Seat ticks and the truss web are generated by helpers
      that emit real path data. The SVG renders a readable plan with JS off; the
      animation only reveals what is already there.
    resting_state_is_invisible: >
      The layer is opacity-0 in markup and faded in by the timeline, so with JS
      off or GSAP unavailable it never appears at all — it cannot strand a
      half-drawn plan behind the copy.
    perf: >
      will-change:transform is written only while the trigger is active
      (onToggle), not permanently — otherwise a dozen SVG groups keep their own
      compositor layers for the life of the page. All getTotalLength() reads
      happen in one pass before any write, so the line-draw does not thrash.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean, 21/21. Home 16.9 kB / 195 kB (+1.5 kB).
      SCRUB PROVEN, not inferred: driving window.scrollTo + a synchronous
      'scroll' event exercises ScrollTrigger without needing rAF.
      Desktop 1440x820 scrolling down, dashoffset 1060 -> 798 -> 535 -> 273 ->
      10 -> 0 and the truss group -9deg/-55.4px -> 0/0; pin engaged 0..750,
      released by 792. Scrolling back up returned IDENTICAL values at the same
      scroll positions (y=600 -> 10, y=300 -> 535), i.e. a pure function of
      scroll position, and y=0 restored the full scattered state.
      Shape matrix at rest decodes exactly to rotate(-9deg) about the group
      centre (600,60) plus translateY(-150) — the authored scatter.
      Mobile 375x812: no pin-spacer, section still exactly 812, scrub still runs
      (1060 -> 719 -> 377 -> 0), SVG right edge 375, no horizontal overflow.
      Mask: blueprint fully transparent by y=705, stats bar starts at 734.
      All 8 home sections still lay out in order after the pin.
    NOT_verified: >
      Frame-rate. rAF does not fire in this browser, so "60fps" is unproven —
      only that the work per frame is small and compositor-friendly.
      The reduced-motion branch was reasoned, not exercised; this pane offers no
      way to emulate the media query.
    revert_how: >
      Delete components/home/EventBlueprintAnimation.tsx and remove its import
      plus the single <EventBlueprintAnimation /> line from Hero.tsx. Nothing
      else changed; the pin disappears with it.

  - id: ai-wing-audit
    date: 2026-08-04
    decision: >
      Audited this file against the working tree rather than against memory, on
      the user's instruction to record everything so far. Added hero_history,
      design.hero_ground, smaya-gold-deep to the palette, the snapshot gap
      warning, three conventions and two verification notes. Removed one dead
      import (`useRef` in components/home/Hero.tsx, left over from when the hero
      had a scroll ref).
    status: current
    what_the_audit_found: >
      The decisions log was accurate — every change from 2026-08-04 was already
      recorded. What was MISSING was navigability and drift:
        · six hero versions in a newest-first list with no index, so a future
          session could act on a dead entry (fixed: hero_history)
        · --color-smaya-gold-deep absent from design.palette despite being a
          contrast contract (fixed)
        · #0D0D0E hero ground undocumented as a non-token exception (fixed)
        · open_items still chasing hero-loop.mp4, an asset the user killed
          (marked moot)
        · verification claiming only 375-1024 coverage (updated to 320-1920)
        · no warning that the newest snapshot predates ~two days of work
    honest_state: >
      Deleted files are recoverable ONLY from the session transcript, not from
      disk. ai.wing records decisions; it has never been able to restore code.
    decision: >
      components/services/VerticalTemplate.tsx hero: headline scale cut about
      35% (xl 6.5rem -> 4.25rem), copy column widened max-w-4xl -> max-w-5xl,
      and section padding pb-16 -> pb-24 sm:pb-28. One edit, all seven verticals.
    status: current
    why: >
      "reduce these wordings font size, since for the 90% screen size also, the
      last line is not shown. similarly correct for the other service sections."
    TWO_SEPARATE_CAUSES: >
      The font size was only half of it, and the other half is the one that
      would have come back.
      1. SIZE. At 6.5rem the accent "Most Beautiful Moments" measured ~1070px
         inside an 896px column, so it broke to a second line and every hero ran
         to THREE lines (306px of type). At 4.25rem the longest accent is ~700px
         and all seven sit on two lines (133px).
      2. PADDING. `pb-16` (64px) was SMALLER than the negative margin the next
         section uses to overlap this one (-mt-14 / sm:-mt-20 = 56 / 80px). The
         foot of the hero copy was underneath the white panel BY CONSTRUCTION.
         At 1280x720 the tagline cleared it by 5px; any shorter viewport buried
         it regardless of type size.
      Do not "tidy" pb back down. The rule is pb > the next section's overlap.
    column_width: >
      max-w-5xl because the longest lead, "Building Stronger Communities",
      measures 963px — it was the one vertical still wrapping to three lines at
      the reduced size inside a 896px column. Widening the column fixed the odd
      one out instead of shrinking type for the other six. The tagline keeps its
      own max-w-xl, so measure length is unchanged.
    clearance_floor: >
      Content is centred inside (heroH - 240) of padding, so
      clearance = (heroH - 359) / 2, and heroH is at least the 423px the content
      needs. The floor is therefore 32px at ANY viewport height — verified at
      420px tall, which is well past anything real.
    verified: >
      tsc clean, next build clean, /services/[slug] 9.96 kB / 181 kB.
      All seven verticals at 1280x650: 68px, exactly 2 visual lines each, widest
      line 963px inside a 1024px column, clearance 87px.
      1536x730 (the reported case): "Most Beautiful Moments" now one line, hero
      599px inside a 730px viewport, clearance 120px.
      1280x420: clearance 32px, the predicted floor.
      375x812: 34px, 3 lines all inside the 335px gutter, clearance 164px, no
      horizontal overflow.

