  - id: hero-loop-tooling
    date: 2026-08-03
    decision: >
      scripts/make-hero-loop.mjs is the ONLY sanctioned way to build a hero
      loop. Do not hand-write the ffmpeg command.
    status: current
    usage: node scripts/make-hero-loop.mjs <src.mp4> <slug> <tmpdir>
    what_it_does: >
      Probes duration/fps, DETECTS SCENE CUTS, picks a splice point X clear of
      every cut by 3 frames, encodes desktop + mobile + poster, then measures
      the resulting seam and prints a verdict.
    why_it_exists: >
      Every Gemini clip on this project is a multi-shot montage. If X lands on a
      shot boundary the output's first and last frames sit on opposite sides of
      a cut, and the "seamless" loop becomes the harshest cut on the page. This
      shipped once on adventure-hero (seam 59.16) and was caught only because
      every encode is measured.
    BUG_ALREADY_FIXED_ONCE: >
      detectCuts MUST use spawnSync, not execFileSync. showinfo writes to
      stderr and `ffmpeg -f null -` exits 0, so execFileSync never surfaces it —
      the first version silently reported "no cuts" for every clip and spliced
      straight onto entertainment's t=2.00 boundary (seam 56.57). If cut
      detection ever reports "none" for a Gemini clip, distrust it: none of them
      are single-shot.
    companion: >
      scripts/measure-loop-seam.mjs re-measures existing files without
      re-encoding.

  - id: adventure-video-hero
    date: 2026-08-03
    decision: >
      /services/adventure has a looping video hero. FOUR of seven verticals now
      carry footage (celebrations, corporate, fitness, adventure);
      entertainment, community and workshops still use their still image.
    status: current
    revert_how: >
      Delete the `heroVideo` block from the `adventure` entry in
      data/verticals.ts. Optionally delete public/videos/adventure-hero*.
    encoded_assets:
      - public/videos/adventure-hero.mp4 — 802 KB, 1280x720, 8.500s
      - public/videos/adventure-hero-mobile.mp4 — 484 KB, 854x480
      - public/videos/adventure-hero-poster.jpg — 64 KB
      Note the 8.5s length: the crossfade point had to move (see below), and
      output length is always D - X.

    SPLICE_POINT_MUST_AVOID_SCENE_CUTS:
      severity: >
        This is the one real trap in the seamless-loop recipe. Read it before
        encoding any new clip.
      what_happened: >
        The adventure source is a FIVE-SHOT MONTAGE with hard cuts at exactly
        t=2, 4, 6 and 8 — and the standard recipe splices at X=2. Output then
        began on source t=2.000 (first frame of shot 2) and ended on source
        t=1.958 (last frame of shot 1): adjacent timestamps on OPPOSITE SIDES
        of a cut. Seam measured 59.16 against a 2.99 baseline — a hard cut,
        the worst on the site, produced BY the loop recipe rather than fixed
        by it.
      fix: >
        Re-spliced at X=1.5, comfortably inside shot 1. Seam 59.16 -> 14.8
        against a 9.55 baseline. Output is 8.5s and the closing dissolve is
        1.5s.
      how_to_check_first: >
        ffmpeg -i <src> -filter:v "select='gt(scene,0.3)',showinfo" -f null -
        then grep pts_time. Choose X so that no cut lands in the ~42ms window
        [X - 1/fps, X]. Anywhere mid-shot is fine.
      audit_of_shipped_clips: >
        ALL ten Gemini sources are montages. Every other clip was checked and
        none has a cut inside its risk window — entertainment's 1.875 is the
        closest at 3 frames clear, which is why its 13.85 seam was normal
        montage variance rather than a bad splice. NO OTHER CLIP NEEDS
        RE-ENCODING.

    verified: >
      Desktop cut playing, readyState 4, faded in, and observed to LOOP
      (7.90 -> 8.50 -> 0.66 -> continues) at 8.5s duration. Poster-path audit
      confirms four verticals on video, three on stills. The Smaya Tribe band
      still renders. 375px: mobile cut, 375x617 object-cover, h1 33.6px fits,
      56px CTA, zero overflow. Build clean, 21/21.
    playback_note: >
      Playback advanced here despite document.visibilityState reporting
      "hidden", which means the fitness-hero freeze recorded earlier was
      TRANSIENT rather than a systematic block on hidden tabs. Do not treat
      "hidden" as proof that playback cannot be observed — retry instead.

