    the AI session never displays, so the page does not composite frames and
    requestAnimationFrame never fires — framer, GSAP, IntersectionObserver and
    count-ups all sit frozen. Structure, layout boxes, CSS contracts and image
    bytes were verified instead. The split-door reveal, black hole, scroll
    scrubs and card stacks are unwatched. Treat any claim that they "work" as
    unproven until a human confirms.
  lighthouse: Never measured. Do not claim the 85+ mobile target is met.

restore:
  warning: >
    THIS PROJECT HAS NO VERSION CONTROL. This file records decisions; it cannot
    restore code. Only the snapshots below can, and they are plain directory
    copies — nothing prunes or updates them automatically.
  SNAPSHOT_GAP: >
    THE NEWEST SNAPSHOT IS 2026-08-02. Everything from 2026-08-03 onward — all
    14 hero videos, the six home-hero rebuilds, the services header, the
    editorial motion pass — exists in the working directory ONLY. A mistaken
    overwrite of components/home/Hero.tsx or VerticalTemplate.tsx cannot be
    undone from disk; it would have to be reconstructed from the session
    transcript, which is how the 2026-08-04 revert was performed.
    Take a snapshot (command below) before any large refactor.
  snapshots:
    # Newest first. Each is a plain directory copy — restore by copying files back.
    - id: 2026-08-02-pre-lottie
      path: C:\Users\pavit\Downloads\smaya-events-snapshot-2026-08-02-pre-lottie
      taken: 2026-08-02
      contains: >
        The state immediately BEFORE the Lottie services grid (84 files). Holds
        components/home/VerticalsGrid.tsx — the icon-based 7-card grid with
        TiltCard + gradient badges, no Lottie dependency.
      use_when: >
        The Lottie grid is unwanted, or its animations/placeholders are not
        working out. See decisions.lottie-services-grid.revert_how.

    - id: 2026-08-02-pre-revert
      path: C:\Users\pavit\Downloads\smaya-events-snapshot-current
      taken: 2026-08-02
      contains: >
        Full pre-revert state (95 files, excludes node_modules/.next): the seven
        per-vertical layouts and the six uniquely rebuilt pages, plus the
        primitives they needed (HorizontalScroller, SpotlightMask, HoverPreview).
      use_when: >
        The user asks for any of that distinct-layout work back. Lift from here
        rather than rewriting from memory.
  how_to_snapshot: >
    robocopy "<root>" "<dest>" /E /XD node_modules .next .git
  recommendation: >
    `git init` + a commit would make rollback instant and exact. Reverting
    without it costs a full session and yields reconstructions, not
    restorations. Offered and declined once — re-offer if rollback comes up again.

conventions:
  - Use next/image for every image, next/link for every internal link.
  - Mobile-first: base classes target the smallest viewport, md:/lg:/xl: layer up.
  - Grids: grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 — never a cramped 2-col on mobile.
  - Stagger grids with framer variants on the parent, not per-item delay: i * 0.1.
  - Load GSAP through lib/gsap.ts (lazy, per section). Never import it at module top level.
  - ParticleField / CustomCursor / BlackHoleTransition are next/dynamic + ssr:false.
  - Headline pattern: serif, with the accent word in italic gold. The HOME hero
    is the one exception — its spec called for sans `font-bold`, so only the
    italic-gold accent carries over there. Every other headline follows the rule.
  - Word-splitting a headline? Separate words with a real space TEXT NODE, never
    a right margin. Margins look identical and leave the accessible name, the
    copied text and the indexable content reading "CreatingMoments,".
  - Oversized headlines get a `clamp()` whose ceiling equals the intended
    Tailwind step, not the bare step. A flat `text-4xl` is a fixed 36px and
    overflows narrow phones; a flat `lg:text-8xl` overruns a 12-col column at
    1024px. Both shipped as bugs.

## 2026-08-12 — Corporate: stacking-card deck with six bespoke interactions

**Brief premise was wrong twice, corrected rather than followed literally.**
1. There is no `app/services/corporate/page.tsx`. Corporate renders through the
   dynamic `app/services/[slug]/page.tsx` → `VerticalTemplate`. Creating the
   named file would have produced a second, unreachable route (or shadowed the
   dynamic one and dropped the hero/benefits/CTA). Implemented as a new
   `layout: 'stack'` branch in the shared `ServiceCardGrid` instead.
2. `public/corporate-award-nights-realistic..jpg` had TWO dots. The brief's path
   (single dot) would have 404'd. Renamed the file.

**Why the branch lives in ServiceCardGrid, not VerticalTemplate:** every vertical
gets identical section chrome (eyebrow, rule, title, spacing) from the template.
Branching upstream would have meant duplicating that chrome for one vertical.
`stack` is the first layout that also swaps the CARD — the six interactions can't
reuse `ServiceCard` the way `carousel` does.

**THEMES is keyed by service title, not index.** An index would silently pair the
wrong animation with the wrong card the moment anyone reorders the array in
data/verticals.ts.

**Per-card `useScroll`, offset `['start start','end start']`.** Progress is how far
THAT card has left the viewport, driving its own recede. Measuring against the
container instead would shrink all six on the same schedule regardless of
position. Hooks-in-a-loop is why each card is its own component.

**Split/curtain halves use a 200%-wide inner box.** Two `w-1/2 overflow-hidden`
boxes each holding a full-card-width image, anchored left and right. Verified:
both inner boxes measure 512x448 @L632 — they overlay exactly, so the halves
reconstruct one uncropped photograph. Two independent `object-cover` crops would
have shown a visible discontinuity at the seam.

**Award Nights' detail panel is translucent; the other four are opaque.** Caught by
inspection, not by the brief: elsewhere the covering layer slides fully away so an
opaque panel is fine, but curtains part onto a LIT STAGE. An opaque panel would
have hidden the photograph, the spotlight and the bokeh — i.e. the entire reveal.
Now a bottom-anchored scrim.

**Verified:** tsc clean; `next build` clean, /services/[slug] 19.2 kB / 190 kB.
Runtime: 6 cards, staggered sticky tops 96/114/132/150/168/186px, uniform
1024x448, six distinct grounds and six distinct labels. Sticky PROVEN under
scroll (cards pin at 96/114/132/150 while 5 and 6 still climb) despite
`body { overflow-y: auto }`. All six toggles flip `aria-expanded` and mount the
right payload: 4 agenda tracks / 4 AV specs / 5 node pills / 4 ledger counters /
4 dual-track items / 4 trophy items + 5 bokeh.

**NOT verified — pane limitation, unchanged from previous sessions.** Measured
directly this time: **0 rAF ticks in 600ms**, and screenshots fail with "the
Browser pane is not displayed, so the page is not compositing frames". Framer
writes motion values on rAF, so NOTHING that tweens could be observed: the
scroll-linked scale/dim read `none`/`0` even after a card had fully passed, and
the Annual Day counters read `0+` (the `+` suffix proves the real `Counter`
mounted — it is not the literal `0` fallback). State machines and geometry are
verified; easing, timing and the depth effect are not. Needs a real browser.

## 2026-08-12 — Corporate deck: 1.5s auto-reveal on pin

**"Active" is a stack-level question, and this is the whole design.** A card cannot
answer it alone. Once it pins it STAYS pinned — sticky holds it at its offset
while the rest of the deck gathers on top — so "am I at my pin line?" remains
true long after the card is buried, and is useless as an activity test. An
IntersectionObserver has the identical blind spot: a fully covered card is still
fully intersecting. What actually distinguishes the front of the deck is
ORDERING: every card at or above its pin line is pinned, and the LAST such card
is the one nothing has covered. One `scroll` listener in CorporateStack walks the
deck, takes the highest arrived index, and passes `active` down. It flips to i+1
at the exact moment card i+1 pins over card i, and flips back on the way up.
`bottom > 0` drops cards scrolled off the top, so leaving the section upward
deactivates the deck rather than stranding the last card as permanently active.

**Timer lifecycle falls out of effect cleanup**, keyed on `active`. Scroll away
before 1500ms and the pending timeout is cancelled before it fires; the `!active`
branch then resets `open` so the card replays fresh on the way back.

**`manual` flag:** once the reader works the button, the card stops playing itself.
An auto-reveal firing 1.5s after a deliberate close would fight them.

**Verified — and this time the pane could observe it.** Auto-reveal is React state,
not a rAF tween, so `data-active` / `data-open` are readable here:
 - `A-` at +0.4s (timer pending) → `AO` at +1.7s. Fires at 1.5s, not before.
 - Handoff: card1 `AO` → card2 pins → card1 resets to `--`, card2 `A-` then `AO`.
 - All six dwelt in turn: exactly one `AO` at a time, correct payload each
   (4 agenda / 4 AV specs / 5 node pills / 4 counters / 4 dual-track / 4 trophy
   + 5 bokeh).
 - CANCELLATION: card3 held 0.3s went `A-`, reset to `--` on flying past, and was
   still `--` 1.7s later — no late fire.
 - Reset + replay: back to page top → all `--`; return to card1 → `AO` again.
`tsc` clean; `next build` clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 19.5 kB / 190 kB.

**PANE LIMITATION IS WIDER THAN RECORDED.** Previously logged as "rAF never ticks".
Measured this session: the pane ALSO NEVER DISPATCHES SCROLL EVENTS — a listener
attached fresh to `window` heard 0 events while `scrollY` moved 735→765. Scroll
events are emitted during the rendering steps the non-compositing pane skips,
same root cause. To verify, substitute both: shim `window.requestAnimationFrame`
onto setTimeout (the handler reads it at dispatch time, so the real code runs
unmodified) and `window.dispatchEvent(new Event('scroll'))` after setting
scrollTop.

**TWO PROBE BUGS worth remembering — both made the component look broken when it
was not.** Getting the absolute layout position of a STICKY element:
 - `getBoundingClientRect().top + scrollY` reports the PINNED position for any
   currently-stuck element, not its layout position.
 - `offsetTop` is no better — Chrome reflects the sticky shift there too. Five
   cards all returned an identical 2624.
Correct method: measure at `scrollTop = 0`, where the deck is below the fold and
nothing is stuck. Real absolute tops 826/1302/1778/2254/2730/3206 (476px pitch).

**PROCESS FIX, my error last turn:** running `npx next build` against a live
`next dev` deletes the chunks dev is serving — the site rendered as unstyled HTML
with `Cannot find module './331.js'` / `'./vendor-chunks/motion-dom.js'`. Both use
`.next`. Stop dev → build → delete `.next` → restart dev. (Also seen: a transient
`next/font/google` webpack failure on the first build after wiping `.next`; a
plain re-run succeeded.)

## 2026-08-12 — Corporate deck: 1s timer, ambient motion, typography

**Timer 1500 → 1000ms.** Verified by polling: fired at 1132ms (1000 + 25ms poll
granularity + rAF-shim + React render); card still closed at 800ms, open at
1300ms.

**Ambient motion is CSS keyframes, NOT framer-motion.** Six cards sit in the deck
at once, so six JS infinite loops would be six permanent rAF subscriptions doing
purely decorative work. Keyframes in globals.css run off the main thread, and the
blanket `prefers-reduced-motion` rule already there neutralises all of them with
no per-layer guard. Secondary benefit: computed `animation-name` is READABLE in
this pane, so the ambient layer could actually be verified — a framer loop would
have shown nothing.

Per-card parameters are inline styles, not classes — colours and durations are
data, and an assembled class string never survives Tailwind's static scanner.
Verified layer counts: 3 orbs / grid+2 orbs+sweep / 2 wave blobs+orb / 3 shimmer
leaks+orb / 3 orbs / orb+7 bokeh.

**`Layer` had to lose its `theme.base` fill.** Harmless while the ground was flat,
but an opaque fill over the new ambient layer would have cancelled exactly the
motion this change adds. Now transparent, so orbs drift behind the visual half
and the glass panels have something to frost.

**Detail is an inset glass card, not a full-bleed opaque fill** —
`backdrop-blur-md bg-black/40 border-white/10`, verified blur(12px) /
rgba(0,0,0,0.4) / 16px radius on all six. The inset margin lets ambient show
around it as well as through it.

**Copy column uses a directional scrim, not a glass box.** The brief asked for
glassmorphism on all text containers; boxing a 44px/900 heading that is already
the largest element on the card adds a border for its own sake. A 100deg
black-to-transparent wash buys the same contrast and stays invisible.

**Heading gradient fill** verified live: `background-clip: text`,
`-webkit-text-fill-color: rgba(0,0,0,0)`, 44px, weight 900.

`tsc` clean; `next build` clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 20.6 kB / 192 kB.

**PROBE BUG, third of this kind — always re-derive selectors after changing DOM
structure.** Inserting `<CardAmbient>` as the grid's first child shifted the
visual column from `:scope > div > div`[1] to [2]. The old index silently
inspected the COPY column, reporting `glassFound: false` on all six cards and
making the glass panels look unimplemented. Tell: micro-label tracking read
4.62px (= 0.42em x 11px, the index counter in the copy column) instead of the
3.4px (0.34em x 10px) MicroLabel actually uses. Select the LAST column, or by a
stable marker, not a positional index.

## 2026-08-12 — Corporate deck: single-fire auto-reveal

**The reported diagnosis was half right; recording both halves.**

WRONG: "scroll progress dependencies are causing timers to reset constantly."
The effect's deps were already `[active, manual]` — two booleans. `active` only
changes at a handoff boundary, and `setActive` already short-circuits on
`prev === front`, so scroll movement never re-ran the effect.

RIGHT, and the actual defect, one line below: `if (!active) setOpen(false)` plus
effect-cleanup clearing the pending timeout. Cards pin 476px apart, so at any
ordinary scroll speed a card holds the front for ~300-400ms — far under the
1000ms it needed. Every pending reveal was cancelled on the way past, and any
card already open slammed shut the moment the next one pinned. Symptom matches
the report exactly; cause does not.

**Fix: `armed` as a REF, not state.** Read and written inside the effect without
appearing in the dep array, so a re-run cannot re-arm a card that already
claimed its turn. Two rules that look wrong alone but are the fix together:
 1. The timeout is NOT cleared when the card goes inactive (unmount only). A
    card scrolled past is already open when the reader returns.
 2. There is no `setOpen(false)` branch at all. Once revealed, stays revealed.
This DELIBERATELY REPLACES the reset-and-replay behaviour verified in the
1.5s-timer entry above. The two are mutually exclusive — a card cannot both
re-arm on return and fire exactly once — and the cancellation guarantee proved
in that entry is now intentionally gone. Reliability was judged worth more.

**"Already visible at page load" premise was false on this page, at every
viewport.** Measured: at 720px tall the first card sits 106px BELOW the fold
(top 826, 0% visible). Went taller to 1400px expecting it to come into view — it
was still only 4% visible, because the hero is viewport-height, so a taller
window pushes the whole deck down proportionally. There is no viewport where
card one is substantially visible at scrollY 0.
Implemented the general rule anyway, since it is right for a restored mid-page
scroll: front-of-deck = last PINNED card, or, when nothing has pinned, card one
once it is >=60% on screen. The floor stops it firing while the card is still a
sliver at the bottom edge. Verified in the window where it actually applies:
card top 1064 (pin line 98, NOT pinned), 75% visible -> armed at 400ms, open at
1300ms.

**ResizeObserver on the wrappers.** A mount-time measurement goes stale by itself:
photographs and webfonts land after the effect runs and reflow everything below.
If the reader has not scrolled — precisely the already-in-view case — no scroll
event ever arrives to correct it.

**Verified:** fired at 1101ms; 12/12 scroll-jitter samples stayed open (no
restart); still open after scrolling away and after returning to page top.
THE DECIDING TEST — a brisk pass at 350ms per card, well under the timer: 3 cards
already open by the end of the pass, all 6 open a second later. Under the old
code every one would have been closed.
`tsc` clean; `next build` clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 20.8 kB / 192 kB.

## 2026-08-12 — Corporate deck: per-card arming (the real "subsequent cards" bug)

**REPRODUCED THE REPORTED FAILURE, and my previous verification was the reason I
had missed it.** Arming was keyed on a card being EXACTLY the active one:
`phase === 'active'`. Any scroll covering more than one 476px card-pitch between
two animation frames — a trackpad fling, a Lenis ease, a jump — moves the front
of the deck from card 1 straight to card 5. Cards 2/3/4 go `before` -> `past`
without ever passing through `active`, so they never armed and stayed shut
permanently. Measured before the fix: `PO P- P- P- AO B-`.

Why the earlier "brisk pass at 350ms/card" test proved nothing here: it stepped
scrollTop to EVERY pin position in turn, so no card was ever skipped. A test that
walks every state cannot catch a bug about skipping states. Fling-style jumps are
now part of the verification.

**Fix: arm on REACHED, not on ACTIVE.** `for (i = 0; i <= active; i++)` arms every
index the reader has passed, each with its own 1000ms timeout in a
`Map<index, timeoutId>`, guarded by an `armed` Set so ordinary scrolling never
restarts a countdown. Reveal state is a `Set<number>` replaced immutably, so card
i revealing cannot disturb any other card's entry. Rewind (`i > active`) clears
that index's timer, unarms and un-reveals it.

**Second bug, mine, found during verification:** the manual-override reset was
keyed on `revealed` — `useEffect(() => { if (!revealed) setOverride(null) },
[revealed])`. For a card opened by hand that the scroll never auto-revealed,
`revealed` is false the whole time, so the effect never re-runs and the card
stays open forever, including back at the top of the page. Keyed on `phase`
instead. Tell: `finalTopState` read `B- B- B- BO B- B-` — one card open while its
phase was `before`.

**Also corrected a premature reading of my own:** an intermediate run showed cards
1 and 2 open at the top (`BO BO ...`) and I nearly chased it as a third bug. With
`data-revealed` / `data-override` instrumentation added, the same sequence came
back clean — the earlier sample was read before React had re-rendered. Added
those two data attributes permanently; the Set/override state is otherwise
invisible from the DOM and that is what made this hard to see.

**Verified (clean reload, no leftover state):** per-card fire 1181/1160/1156/
1136/1136/1140ms — six independent countdowns. Single jump past the WHOLE deck
from page top -> all six open. Partial rewind to card 3 -> `PO PO AO B- B- B-`
(cards 4-6 reset, 1-3 retained). Re-descent replays all six. Back at top, all
closed. `tsc` clean; `next build` clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 21.7 kB / 193 kB.

## 2026-08-12 — Adventure: topographic node explorer (Expeditions)

**Brief premise corrected, third time in this arc.** There is no
`app/services/expeditions/page.tsx`, and no "expeditions" vertical. Adventure
renders through the dynamic `[slug]` route; "Expeditions" is its grid EYEBROW,
not a slug. Implemented as `layout: 'map'` on the `adventure` vertical, a third
branch in ServiceCardGrid beside `stack` and `carousel`.
Assets were the one thing right first time: all six exist, correctly named, and
are already light (0.14-0.19 MB) — no optimisation pass needed, unlike every
earlier asset drop.

**Reused the corporate deck's hard-won arming model rather than re-deriving it.**
Per-node `reached` Set, per-index timers in a `Map`, `armed` Set for idempotence,
and arming on REACHED not on "currently active". That last point is the whole
lesson from the corporate bug: a fast flick carries several nodes past the
trigger line between two animation frames, and anything keyed to a single active
slot loses every node it skipped. Verified directly here — a single fling past
all five still opens all five.

**Trigger is geometry, not IntersectionObserver.** Each node asks whether its own
top has crossed `innerHeight * 0.72`. IO would report a node still intersecting
long after it scrolled past, and the threshold is a viewport fraction rather
than an element ratio, which is what "entering the active zone" actually means.
0.72 puts the line below centre so a node is comfortably in frame when its
countdown starts.

**Radar sweep occupies the waiting second.** Rendered only while
`reached && !open`, so the sweep IS the 1000ms countdown made visible, then
yields to the HUD. Conic-gradient rotation in CSS (`smaya-exp-radar`, 1s linear)
— linear because any easing reads as a wobble rather than a mechanism turning.

**Nodes alternate sides** via a 3-column grid with an empty spacer, so the route
zig-zags like a survey line while DOM order stays reading order.

**Verified:** 5 nodes, codes BC-01..05, all six assets resolving. Per-node fire
1132/1157/1158/1188/1192ms — five independent countdowns. Single fling past all
five -> all open. Partial rewind to node 2 -> `RO RO -- -- --` (3-5 reset, 1-2
retained). Back to top -> all clear. HUD computed style on all five: bg
rgba(0,0,0,0.7), border teal 0.3 alpha, blur(12px) — the brief's exact spec.
Per-node telemetry distinct and correct: difficulty bars 3/2/4/5/1, 12-point
elevation profiles, matching weather triples, 3 itinerary tiers each. Radar
sweep absent before reach, `smaya-exp-radar 1s` during the window, gone once
open. `tsc` clean; `next build` clean 21/21, /services/[slug] 26.1 kB / 197 kB.

**PROBE NOTE, same class of error as the corporate glass panel.** First HUD audit
reported `bg-black/50` + white border and looked like the spec had been missed —
the selector matched the OUTER card, which also contains the polyline I was
keying on. Selecting by the brief's own classes gave the right element. When
auditing a nested panel, key on something only that panel has.

