## 2026-08-15 — Workshops: true fanning 3D folder

Rebuilt to the reference mechanics: cards now live INSIDE a pocket and fan up
and out of it, rather than sitting as a flat list within a folder-shaped box.

**REFERENCE URL NOT FETCHED.** The brief cites a 21st.dev component; built from
its written description of the mechanics (flap tilts on rotateX, cards fan
upward/outward with weighted springs) rather than guessing at unseen code.

**THE FLAP IS HINGED AT ITS BOTTOM EDGE — that is the whole illusion.**
`transformOrigin: 'bottom center'` makes it tip away from the viewer like a real
pocket opening; at the default centre origin it would pivot straight through the
folder body. Verified computed origin `288.167px 132px`, where 132px is the
flap's own height, i.e. its base. The flap also sits at z-index 40, ABOVE the
cards, so they emerge from behind it — which is what makes them read as having
been inside.

**POCKET IS `overflow: visible` AND preserve-3d.** The fan rises well above the
folder's own box, so any clipping would cut it off. Note this is only safe
because nothing here needs `overflow: hidden` on the 3D parent — that
combination is what flattens preserve-3d in Chrome (see the diorama entry).

**Fan geometry is per-step, not per-card.** angle/spread/lift/droop are defined
once and multiplied by `i - centre`, so the same constants describe the
four-card folder and the three-card one, always centred on the stack.

**ZERO LAYOUT SHIFT BY CONSTRUCTION.** Cards are absolutely positioned inside a
fixed-height pocket, so fanning, expanding and collapsing cannot move a pixel of
the page. Verified pocket height constant at 300px through open, close and every
card selection.

**OPEN ON HOVER *OR* FOCUS, permanently on touch.** A hover-only fan is
unreachable two ways: a keyboard user tabbing in would expand a card still
tucked in the pocket, and touch has no hover at all. `onFocusCapture` catches
focus arriving anywhere in the stack. Verified: focusin on card 0-2 opened
folder 0 and made 0-2 active.

**DEDUPED THE COARSE-POINTER HOOK.** Added `useCoarsePointer` to lib/hooks
beside `usePointerFine`, documenting why they are NOT interchangeable: both
start `false`, so `!usePointerFine()` means "assume cannot hover" while
`useCoarsePointer()` means "assume can" — and only the latter renders the safe
first frame for anything hidden until hover. CommunityBurst's local copy
removed; re-verified afterwards (5 coins, ONLY_PHOTO_VISIBLE true, drop initial
intact).

**Verified:** 2 folders, 7 cards, perspective 1000px, all 7 thumbnails correct,
every card drives its own backdrop, exactly one active throughout, hover opens
only its own folder and closes cleanly. `tsc` clean.

**Brief's `/images/wellness-retreatment.jpg` NOT adopted** — "retreatment" means
treating again; the asset is a wellness retreat and is named accordingly. Third
time this path has appeared in a brief, so it is noted in the component itself
rather than only in this log.

## 2026-08-15 — Workshops: collapsed cards lose their images

Card model inverted. Collapsed cards now carry ONLY the palette gradient, a
badge and the title; the photograph belongs to the expanded state, alongside
the description. The ambient cross-fade backdrop is unchanged.

**THE PREVIEW IMAGE IS MOUNTED, NOT FADED.** Rendering it at `opacity: 0` when
collapsed would satisfy the brief to the eye while still downloading all seven
files on load — absent visually, fully present on the network. Mounting it with
the active state means a card costs nothing until opened, and by then the
ambient backdrop has already requested the identical URL, so both are served
from one fetch. Verified: 1 image in the DOM on a fresh load, 0 in collapsed
cards.

**DEVIATED FROM THE BRIEF'S `layout` PROP — deliberately.** Framer performs
layout animations by writing `transform`, and its own docs rule out combining
that with `rotate`. Every card sits inside a rotated ancestor (the fan angle)
and the active one rotates back to 0 as it expands, so a `layout` card would be
measured through a rotating matrix for the whole expansion. Animated `width`
plus `height: 'auto'` gives the same spring growth from the brief's exact
physics (350/30) with no projection tree to distort.

**THE LI IS NOW A ZERO-SIZED ANCHOR.** It carries only the fan transform; the
card hangs off it and centres itself with `-translate-x-1/2`. Centring by a
negative margin (the previous `marginLeft: -98`) would need that margin
re-animated in lockstep with the width, and any disagreement shows as a
sideways lurch mid-expand.

**POCKET RAISED 300 -> 340, FAN_LIFT 128 -> 118.** An expanded card grows
upward from its hinge and the lift stacks on top; the pocket does not clip, so
nothing would stop a card climbing over the heading. Measured every card at
full expansion: max height 139px, worst headroom 73px. Fits.

**CONTRAST IS GUARANTEED BY CONSTRUCTION, not by the photograph.** Copy sits on
a scrim, never on bare artwork, since a picture's local brightness is
unknowable. Measured against the worst case any photo can present — pure WHITE
under the scrim's most permissive stop (0.80): titles 8.32-8.54, descriptions
8.56, badges 5.40-5.65. Min 5.40, AA throughout. No photograph can be brighter
than white, so this is a floor rather than a sample.

**PROBE TRAPS HIT THIS TURN — all three looked like product bugs:**
1. `mouseenter` dispatched with `bubbles: false` did nothing. React synthesises
   `onMouseEnter` from a BUBBLING `mouseover` at the root; the non-bubbling
   native event never reaches it. Read as "hover is broken".
2. Selecting the title by `font-family: serif` grabbed the art wrapper instead
   (inherited charcoal, 16px) and reported 1.44:1. The container-before-child
   trap again — keyed on the `font-serif` CLASS and got the real 8.32.
3. `.focus()` moved `activeElement` correctly but fired ZERO focus events,
   because `document.hasFocus()` is false in this pane. A dispatched `focusin`
   opens the fan and activates the card, so the keyboard path is fine.

**ANIMATION TWEENS REMAIN UNVERIFIED — stated plainly.** 0 rAF ticks in 600ms
with `visibilityState: "hidden"`, so every framer animation is frozen. Proven
rather than assumed: React's intended width (`data-w`) is correct on all seven
cards while the applied width stays at its mount value, and stale art layers
sit at opacity 1 with their exit never run while new ones sit at 0 with their
entrance never run — frozen mid-transition, exactly inverted from a live
browser. State logic verified via data attributes; the motion itself needs a
real tab.

## 2026-08-15 — Workshops: two card-stack decks replace the folders

Section fully re-specified. The folder/pocket metaphor is gone; each column is
now a horizontal row pairing an editorial header with a physical card deck.
Cards carry image-on-top, badge, heading and description, per the reference.

**A MONOTONIC CURSOR, NOT A ROTATED ARRAY.** Rank is derived as
`(cursor + rank) % total`, so the deck cycles for ever with no state that can
drift. Rotating an array in place would also shift every card's key position,
which is how a stack ends up re-mounting its images mid-animation.

**RENDER EXACTLY `min(3, total - 1)` CARDS — the cap is load-bearing.** The
peeling card leaves the rendered window the instant the cursor moves, which is
what gives AnimatePresence something to animate out. Its NEW rank is always
`total - 1`, so rendering any more would show the card reappearing at the back
of its own deck mid-flight. Four-card deck shows three; three-card deck shows
two.

**THE KEY CARRIES A CYCLE SUFFIX.** A title alone repeats every `total`
advances and an exiting card holds its key until its exit finishes, so a fast
deck could mount one title twice under one key — the classic way an
AnimatePresence stack drops or duplicates children. Verified across 9 advances
(two full cycles): zero collisions, 12 distinct keys, mounted count rising by
exactly one per advance.

**HOVER LIFTS, IT DOES NOT PEEL — deliberate deviation.** The brief lists
hovering beside dragging and clicking, but a hover that commits cannot work:
the next card arrives under the same stationary cursor and peels in turn, so
the deck riffles itself to a blur until the pointer leaves. Hover lifts the top
card to show it is live; commit stays with the two inputs that carry intent.

**ZERO LAYOUT SHIFT IS STRUCTURAL.** Deck reserves fixed height, every card
absolute. Measured across a full cycle: deck 444px, page 2972px, deck top 702px
— all constant. Mobile 375px: card 327x380 in a 404px deck, no horizontal
overflow, `touch-action: pan-y` so the page still scrolls vertically over a
horizontally-draggable card.

**Dropped the full-bleed ambient backdrop** from the previous design. Every
card now carries its own photograph, so a blurred picture behind two decks
would compete with seven of them. Directional lamps kept (pure CSS).

**Removed a kicker that repeated its own heading** — it printed the last word
of the title ("MASTERCLASSES") directly above the title. Not in the brief's
card anatomy either; the icon moved onto the heading line.

**Copy fits everywhere.** All 7 descriptions measured in the live element:
1 heading line, 2 copy lines, 74px spare desktop / 55px mobile.

**Contrast AAA:** headings 12.02-13.34, body 9.28-9.78, badges 7.02-7.86
against each palette's lightest gradient stop. Min 7.02.

**PROBE TRAPS HIT THIS TURN:**
1. Card heights (420/410/383) did not match the seat scales — because
   `getBoundingClientRect` GROWS with rotation. The matrices were exact:
   scale 1/0.945/0.89, rotate 0/-1.5/1.2, y 0/22/44.
2. Body-copy contrast read 1.21. Tailwind emits `text-white/75` as
   `oklab(0.999994 ... / 0.75)`, and canvas `fillStyle` returns that string
   VERBATIM rather than normalising it, so the regex read 0.999994 as red.
   Fixed by rasterising the colour onto a known backdrop and reading the pixel
   with getImageData — immune to colour space. True value 9.78.
3. Addressing the top card by cycle suffix alone matched the first stale
   exiting card every time, so a 7-card sweep measured one card 7 times.

**Exit animations still unverifiable:** 0 rAF ticks, `visibility: hidden`.
Cards mount correctly because `AnimatePresence initial={false}` renders them
straight at their seat; everything after that needs a real tab.

**BUILD NOTE FOR NEXT TIME:** `next build | tail` writes NOTHING until the
build finishes — tail holds the pipe. An empty log read as a ten-minute hang
when the build was simply running. Redirect to a file instead.

## 2026-08-15 — Workshops: scroll-driven rows replace the gesture deck

Both rows are now pinned tracks whose cards are placed entirely by scroll
progress. No state, no timers, no gestures: `useScroll` on each track is the
only clock. Row 1 fans four cards out with a staggered window per card; row 2
brings three retreats forward in turn.

**THE SPRING IS ON THE PROGRESS, NOT ON EACH CARD.** One smoothed clock drives
every card in a row, so a fast flick cannot shear the row apart — which is
exactly what happens when each card springs independently. Kept gentle because
Lenis is already smoothing; two aggressive smoothers read as lag, not weight.

**TWO REAL BUGS CAUGHT BY MEASUREMENT — both would have shipped:**

1. **Fan overflowed at exactly 1024px** (-15px). Two compounding errors: the
   spread was measured against `clientWidth`, which INCLUDES the `px-6`
   padding; and it budgeted the card's WIDTH when the outermost card is tilted,
   and a rotated rectangle is wider than the rectangle — `w·cosθ + h·sinθ`. At
   4.5° a 260x380 card occupies 289px, not 260. Predicted footprint now matches
   measured exactly (289 = 289). Note the direction of the trap: TALLER cards
   overflow MORE, because height feeds the sinθ term. Swept 1024-1920: all fit,
   min gap 8px (the deliberate gutter).

2. **The retreats row went completely blank from progress 0.95 to 1.0.** The
   last card peeled away with nothing behind it — on a 300vh track that is a
   full pinned panel of empty stage to scroll past. Found by sweeping progress
   in 0.005 steps and recording the MAXIMUM opacity across all cards, not by
   looking. The last card now holds the front, which also gives the row a
   resting state. Re-swept both rows: zero empty frames, min peak opacity 0.676.

**FAN IS CENTRED ON THE STAGE, NOT GROWN FROM CARD 0.** Both the vertical
offset and the arc only push cards downward, so the group hung below the centre
line — 64px past the pinned panel on a 375x812 phone. Subtracting the mean
offset centres it: now 60px clear above, 20px below.

**REDUCED MOTION GETS A REAL LAYOUT, NOT A DISABLED ANIMATION.** Every card is
placed by scroll progress, so switching the transforms off would heap seven
cards on top of one another. The fallback is an ordinary grid — nothing pinned,
and no one scrolls three viewports to read three cards.

**`useDesktopViewport` STARTS FALSE ON PURPOSE.** The narrow layout is the one
that cannot overflow, so the unresolved first frame renders the safe
arrangement. Resolving the other way would let a phone paint a desktop-width
fan for one frame and blow out the horizontal scroll.

**Verified:** pin holds at exactly 96px through the whole track at every
sampled scroll position; 7 cards; badges 01-04 / 01-03; no horizontal overflow
at 375 or 1024-1920. Contrast now AAA throughout — headings 12.02-13.34, body
9.28-9.78, badges 7.02-7.86, row titles 15.87-16.45, row hint lifted from
`white/55` (6.30, AA only) to `white/65` (8.57).

**Still unverifiable here:** the motion itself. rAF is frozen in this pane, so
scroll-linked values never reach the DOM. Everything above was established by
replicating the transform maths and by forcing end states, NOT by watching it
move.

