(`data-track`, `data-pin`, `data-stage` all 0), every deck marker back (2 decks
at 444px, counters 01/04 and 01/03, 2 Next buttons, both live regions). Deck
cycles 01-02-03-04-01 with no key collisions and the grab cursor intact.

**The strongest single check: page height is 2972px, byte-for-byte the value
measured on the card-stack version before the scroll rewrite.** A layout that
lands on the identical total height is very unlikely to differ elsewhere.

The scroll-driven implementation is now gone from disk. Its design notes remain
in the entry above — including the two bugs it surfaced (the rotated-footprint
overflow at 1024px, and the blank stage from progress 0.95 to 1.0), both of
which are worth remembering if scroll-linked rows are ever revisited.

## 2026-08-15 — Workshops: scroll now drives the existing deck

Layout, typography and card design kept exactly as they were; only the thing
that ADVANCES the deck changed, from drag/click to scroll position. Verified
preserved: grid `632.667px 520px`, deck 444px, counter, badges, live regions —
all identical to the pre-change measurements.

**A REAL STICKY BUG, AND MY FIRST DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG.**

Symptom: the pinned panel never stuck. Its top ran 0 -> -347 -> -684 -> -1020
as the page scrolled — it was simply scrolling away.

First theory: the section wrapper's `overflow-hidden` was making an ancestor
the scroll container that `sticky` resolves against. Plausible, and a genuine
hazard, so it was changed to `overflow-x-clip` (which still cuts off the 480px
peel travel but does NOT create a scroll container — clip on one axis is the
one case where the other may stay `visible`). That change is worth keeping, but
IT WAS NOT THE BUG: the panel still did not stick.

Falsified properly by two controls:
  1. A bare `position:sticky;top:96px` div injected into the same parent ALSO
     failed — so nothing in my component could be the cause.
  2. `/services/fitness`, same body/html/Lenis, pinned at exactly 96px while
     its parent went to -300. So sticky is NOT broken environmentally either.

Actual cause: **I put the scroll budget on the track as `padding-bottom`.** A
sticky element is constrained to its containing block, which for a plain block
parent is the CONTENT box — and padding lies outside it. With the pin as the
only in-flow child, its containing block was exactly its own height, leaving
zero travel. Replaced with an in-flow sibling spacer of the same height. Now
pinned at 96px at EVERY sampled position in both rows.

Worth remembering: `padding` does not give a sticky child room; only in-flow
height does.

**COUNTER READS THE RAW SCROLL, NOT THE SPRING.** The cards should lag a little
— that is the weight. A counter should not: it is information, and a number
arriving a beat after the card it names reads as a bug rather than as physics.

**Discrete index is written to state only when it CHANGES.** Setting it every
scroll frame would re-render the whole row dozens of times a second for a value
that changes three times.

**Swept the transform maths before touching the browser:** 5 stops per card
(enters below the deepest seat, climbs ranks 2 and 1, takes the front, peels
away). Zero empty frames in either row, minimum peak opacity 1.0 — there is
always a fully opaque card — and the discrete counter reaches every index.
Card 0's early stops are negative and the last card's final stop exceeds 1;
useTransform clamps both, so the first card starts at the front and the last
never peels off to leave an empty deck.

**The brief's new lavender `#9D8CBF`** measures 5.10:1 on the card's lightest
gradient stop — AA for the 11px bold badge, a shade short of AAA, where the
previous lighter accent cleared it. Flagged rather than silently substituted.
Everything else AA or better: headings 12.02-13.34, body 9.28-9.78, row titles
15.87-16.45, eyebrows 6.73-9.69.

**PROBE TRAP AGAIN:** measured the eyebrow with `header span span` and got
1.19:1 — that selector grabs the `h-px w-8` RULE DIVIDER, which has no colour
of its own. Keyed on the `font-mono` class and confirmed the text: 9.69.

**Not verifiable here:** whether scroll actually advances the counter. 0 rAF
ticks, `visibility: hidden`; framer measures scroll inside its own frame loop,
so `scrollYProgress` never updates and synthetic scroll events do not help
because it polls rather than listens. Pinning IS verified (pure CSS) and the
maths IS verified (swept); the join between them needs a real tab.

## 2026-08-15 — /services rebuilt as the Master Experience Carousel

Replaced the white grid (ServicesHub) with five full-screen obsidian stages:
cinematic backdrop cross-fade, editorial copy left, glassmorphic sub-service
cards right, arrows plus a tablist selector along the bottom.

**NOTHING WAS ORPHANED — checked before building.** The brief lists five
categories while data/verticals.ts holds seven verticals, which looked like it
would strand Community and Workshops. It does not: `serviceCategories` already
carries exactly these five, and `relatedSlugs` reaches all seven (fitness ->
fitness+workshops, production -> entertainment+community). The through-link
chips on each stage are what keep the other two reachable, so they are load
bearing rather than decoration.

**ALL FIVE BACKDROPS ARE LOCAL PHOTOGRAPHY**, all kebab-case, all ~1024x560.
Modest for full-bleed, but they sit under a 7px blur and two scrims, which is
exactly where softness does not show.

**TWO REAL BUGS CAUGHT BY MEASUREMENT:**

1. **The arrows jumped up to 42px between categories** (measured 285-327px).
   Titles wrap to different depths and two of the five categories carry a
   second through-link, so controls stacked under the copy moved out from
   under the pointer mid-click. Fixed structurally, not with a tuned number:
   the left column now reserves the same height as the card well, centres the
   copy in what is left, and pins the controls to the bottom. Re-measured:
   0px shift, panel height constant at 440px across all five.

2. **On mobile the six celebration cards stood 678px tall inside a 420px
   well** and spilled over the selector bar. Absolutely positioned children do
   not expand their parent. Sizing the well for the worst case would instead
   leave a three-card category floating in ~360px of nothing, so the well is
   now a wide-screen device only: below `sm` the grid flows and the well takes
   its height from it; from `sm` up the grid floats and the well holds the
   stage steady.

**LAYOUT IN CSS, TIMING FROM THE HOOK.** The breakpoint split above is
expressed in Tailwind rather than from `useWideViewport`, because a media-query
hook starts false and corrects in an effect — being briefly wrong about a
transition duration is harmless, being briefly wrong about where something sits
is a visible jump. The hook is used ONLY to retire the outgoing grid instantly
below `sm`, where the grids flow and a lingering exit would shove the page.

**Dark frosted glass, not light.** Light glass borrows its brightness from
whatever photograph is behind it, so contrast would change per category and per
pixel. A dark pane sets the floor itself.

**Contrast is AAA against the worst case any photo can present** — pure white
under both scrims: headline 18.53, card title 18.62, through-link 14.99, lede
10.63, card body 9.39, idle tab 9.32, counter 8.81. Min 8.81.

**Keyboard: full tablist contract.** Roving tabindex (the whole bar is ONE tab
stop), arrows move and wrap, Home/End jump to the ends, focus follows
selection. `ArrowDown` is deliberately NOT swallowed, so page scrolling still
works. All verified by dispatch.

**ServicesHub.tsx LEFT ON DISK, unused.** Normally dead code to delete, but
there is still no version control and this section has now been redesigned six
times; it is the only one-step revert path for /services. Delete it once git
exists.

**Verified:** 5 stages cycle via arrows and tabs, backdrop/stage/selected-tab
all track; wide 1280x800 — grid absolute, well 440px matching the panel, 6
cards in 2 columns with 31px spare, stage exactly one viewport tall; mobile
375 — grid static, well 678px, cards clear the tab bar, no horizontal overflow
at either size.

**Not verifiable here:** the cross-fade and card-fan animations. rAF frozen, so
exiting elements never unmount — visible as the card count accumulating
6->10->13->16->20 across five changes, and as the copy block never swapping at
all because `mode="wait"` will not mount the incoming child until the outgoing
one finishes leaving. State itself IS verified via attributes outside the
AnimatePresence.

## 2026-08-15 — /services rebuilt as the Editorial Chapter Feed

Carousel replaced with five stacked chapters and a sticky pill bar. Native
vertical scrolling throughout — no horizontal transport to break on a phone.

**NAVBAR HEIGHT MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED: 77px, fixed, z-50.** The header does not
occupy layout, so the sticky bar has to be told to clear it explicitly and the
jump offset has to subtract it. A wrong value hides the top of every chapter
behind the navigation, and only on the jump — easy to miss in casual clicking.
Bar parks at exactly 77px, z-40, so the two never contend for the same strip.

**Sticky verified BY SCROLLING, after last turn's lesson.** Bar top held at
77px at scroll 400/1200/2400/3427. Also confirmed no ancestor sets
`overflow: hidden` — the chapters each clip their own artwork, which is safe
only because they are SIBLINGS of the bar rather than its parents. The
containing block is the tall page root, so the bar has room to travel.

**Every jump lands the heading clear of the bar** — measured across all five,
gap 149-177px. The gap is larger than the 28px constant because the offset
targets the SECTION top and the section's own `py` then does its job; the
landing sits on the chapter boundary, which is right.

**STRESS-TESTED THE "ZERO CLIPPING" CLAIM RATHER THAN ASSERTING IT — and it
failed the first time.** A 102-char unbreakable token in the title was handled
(`min-w-0` + `break-words`), but the DESCRIPTION clipped on a long URL: I had
guarded the heading, which looked most at risk, and left the body unprotected.
Added `break-words` to the paragraph. Re-tested: long URL, unbreakable title,
and both at once — no self-clipping, no column escape, no page overflow.

`min-w-0` is the load-bearing part: a flex child defaults to
`min-width: auto` and refuses to shrink below its longest unbreakable word, so
a long name widens the card past its grid column instead of wrapping.

**Responsive verified at 320 / 768 / 1440:** columns 1 / 2 / 3 exactly as
specified, zero horizontal overflow at every width, zero rows with unequal card
heights (`h-full` on the card), pill bar scrolls horizontally at 320.

**Contrast AAA against the worst case any photo can present** — pure white at
the artwork's 16% opacity under the section scrim: h1 and chapter title 19.73,
card title 19.01, through-link 14.11, pill 11.89, lede 9.73, card body 9.51,
chapter number 8.73. Min 8.73.

**BRIEF'S SUB-SERVICE NAMES DIFFER FROM THE DATA IN THREE PLACES** and the data
