## 2026-08-15 — /services rebuilt as the Split-Screen Editorial Index

Sixth design. Sticky left index (4 cols) driving a right-hand showcase (8 cols)
on desktop; the same chapters stack sequentially on mobile.

**THE CONTENT IS RENDERED ONCE AND PRESENTED TWICE — and this is the point.**
The brief describes a desktop split-screen and a mobile chapter stack, which is
normally two blocks of markup that then drift, because only one of them gets
looked at. Here each chapter exists exactly once: below `lg` they flow as a
vertical stack, at `lg` they become absolutely stacked layers that cross-fade
in place. Same DOM, no duplication, nothing to fall out of sync.

Note this is the OPPOSITE conclusion from the ChapterFeed entry, where I argued
for one layout over two. Same principle, different application: there the two
descriptions could collapse into one responsive layout; here they genuinely
cannot, so the duplication was moved from the MARKUP into the STYLESHEET, which
is the part that cannot drift.

**THE SWITCH IS PURE CSS, deliberately.** A media-query hook resolves one frame
late, which on a desktop would flash all five chapters stacked at once before
collapsing. Breakpoint classes are correct at first paint.

**`lg:invisible` IS DOING THE WORK OF `inert`, and it was verified.**
`visibility: hidden` removes an element from the tab order AND the
accessibility tree; opacity 0 does neither — opacity-0 content is still
focusable and still announced. Proven rather than asserted: 0 tabbable elements
inside inactive chapters, 1 inside the active one, and the browser refused
`.focus()` on a hidden link. It also transitions cleanly alongside opacity, so
the layer fades and only then stops existing for input.

**REAL BUG FOUND: the transition was unscoped.** `transition-[opacity,
visibility]` applied at every width, including mobile where these panels are
always visible and nothing should ever fade — a resize across the breakpoint
ran a 700ms fade on all five at once. Scoped to `lg:` with everything it
animates.

**REAL CONTRAST FAILURE: the idle index numeral measured 4.42:1** at
`rgba(255,255,255,0.45)` — 11px mono, so it needs 4.5, missing by the width of
a rounding error. Raised to 0.50 for 5.30. Everything else passed: chapter
titles 17.76, taglines 11.63, sub-services 15.63, badges/links 5.87-7.85,
index titles 7.70 idle / 20.38 active. Final min 5.30.

**Verified:** desktop 12-col grid with sticky index and all five chapters
absolutely stacked, exactly one visible; hover, click, ArrowUp, Home, End and
ArrowDown-wrapping all keep index, chapter and computed visibility in agreement;
mobile 375 stacks all five statically with zero clipped nodes, 20 services, 7
through-links (all verticals), no horizontal overflow.

**THREE PROBE TRAPS IN ONE TURN — all previously documented, all hit again:**
1. Dispatched `keydown` on the `<ul>` when the handler is on the BUTTON. Events
   bubble UP, not down, so nothing fired and the keyboard looked broken.
2. CSS transitions frozen: chapters 02-05 read `visibility: hidden` at 375px
   where the `lg:` rules do not even apply. Disabling transitions showed all
   five visible. THIRD time this trap has appeared — always disable transitions
   before measuring computed colour or visibility in this pane.
3. `[data-service] span:last-child` matched the flex WRAPPER, not the title,
   and reported 1.13:1 for text that actually measures 15.63. Key on a class
   only the target carries, and print its text to prove the match.

## 2026-08-15 — /services rebuilt as the Asymmetric Cinematic Carousel

Seventh design. Image stage left, overlapping glass panel right, arrows and
pagination dots below.

**REFERENCE URL NOT FETCHED.** Built from the brief's written description of the
21st.dev carousel rather than guessing at unseen code.

**THE OVERLAP IS TWO GRID ITEMS SHARING A ROW, not a negative margin.** Stage
occupies columns 1-7 and the panel 7-12, both on row 1, so they genuinely
intersect in column 7. Measured: 63px of horizontal overlap, 420px vertical,
panel at z-10 with backdrop-blur(24px) live. Grid placement keeps both in the
same flow, so nothing needs re-tuning when the copy or viewport changes — a
margin-based overlap has to be re-derived every time either does.

**NO AnimatePresence ON THE PANEL — deliberate.** An exiting copy block would
sit in the flow beside the incoming one and shove the panel for the length of
its exit; `mode="wait"` would leave the panel briefly empty instead. Keying the
block means React swaps it outright and only the entry animates. The IMAGE does
use AnimatePresence, because it is absolutely positioned and genuinely can
overlap — which is what a cross-fade requires.

**ZERO LAYOUT BREATHING, verified across all five slides:** panel height 420 on
every slide (6, 3, 4, 3, 4 services), panel top constant, stage top constant,
overlap constant at 63px. `lg:min-h` is what holds it.

**TWO REAL DEFECTS FOUND:**

1. **Dot targets were 24x44 while my own comment claimed "a full 44px
   target".** The comment was wrong, which is worse than the measurement.
   Widened to 32x44 and corrected the comment with the actual arithmetic: at
   375px there are 335 usable, two 44px arrows and gaps eat 128, leaving 167
   for five dots = 33 each. 44x44 would OVERFLOW the row on a phone, trading a
   real bug for a nominal one. 32 clears WCAG 2.5.8's 24px minimum. Measured
   after: controls span 328 of 375.

2. **Slide 05's badge measured 3.79:1** — under AA. The badge sits ON the
   stage, where the only thing between a 13px accent and the photograph is its
   own backing, because the stage deliberately keeps a light 25% wash so the
   image still reads. Deepened the BADGE to 0.85 rather than the stage, which
   keeps the photography bright: now 5.31. Same fix shape as the bento turn —
   when an accent fails over artwork, deepen the chip, not the picture.

**Final contrast AA throughout:** badges 5.31-7.65, panel title 15.71,
description 9.40, sub-services 11.68, kickers/links 5.19-7.47.

**Verified:** desktop overlap and z-order; arrows, dots, wrapping, and
ArrowLeft/Right/Home/End all keep dot, badge, title and live region in
agreement; mobile 375 stacks stage above panel with no overlap, zero clipped
nodes across all five slides, no horizontal overflow.

**Navbar: checked, not re-fixed.** Two turns ago's work still holds.

**SIX DEAD COMPONENTS NOW:** ServicesHub, MasterCarousel, ChapterFeed,
CategoryVaults, CinematicBento, SplitIndex. Seven designs for one page in one
session. Still no version control.

## 2026-08-15 — Navbar contrast (ROOT CAUSE) + scroll-driven carousel

### The navbar bug was a ROUTE LIST, not a colour

`data/site.ts` held `lightHeaderRoutes = ['/', '/events', '/blog', '/services/']`
— `/services/` WITH a trailing slash. That matches `/services/celebrations` but
NOT the `/services` hub. So on the hub the navbar chose its dark-text
treatment, over an obsidian page. Measured before touching anything: SIX of the
eight links at **1.19:1**. Only "Services" and "Book Event" were legible, and
only because they sit on plum chips.

This has been broken since the hub first went dark — seven designs ago — and I
never caught it because I was always measuring the PAGE, never the furniture
above it.

**Fixed by removing the branch, not by recolouring it.** The bar now carries
its own dark ground on every route (`bg-[#050505]/90`, `backdrop-blur-xl`,
`border-white/15`), so it never has to be told what is underneath. A navbar
that depends on a hardcoded list of "dark routes" in another file will go wrong
every time a page changes its background and nobody updates the list — which is
exactly what happened. `isLightHeaderRoute` and `onDark` deleted; the helper in
data/site.ts is now unreferenced.

Worst-case contrast after: **13.47:1**, measured against a pure-white page
under the 90% bar, so it holds on the ivory routes too.

**SITE-WIDE CHANGE** — every page's header now reads dark. Flagged to the user.

### Scroll-driven progression

Track wraps the carousel; the stage pins inside it; `useScroll` on the track
drives the division index.

**The scroll budget is an in-flow spacer, not padding** — the lesson from the
workshops turn. A sticky child is constrained to its containing block, which
for a plain block parent is the CONTENT box, and padding lies outside it.
Verified pinned at 93px (nav 77 + 16) at every sampled position.

**Index read from RAW progress, not a spring.** The artwork may lag — that is
weight. The index drives the badge, dots and announced position; a number
arriving after the picture it names reads as a bug.

**THE MOBILE CASE WOULD HAVE SHIPPED BROKEN.** Caught by reasoning rather than
by the pane: on a phone the stage and panel stack to ~850px, taller than the
viewport, so pinning puts the bottom of the panel permanently out of reach. And
with no spacer the track is barely taller than its content, so `go()` would
target the same pixel for every division — the carousel would have been STUCK
on 01, with scroll drift resetting it moments after any tap. Pin, spacer, the
scroll listener and `go()` are now all gated on `matchMedia('(min-width:
1024px)')`, read on demand so there is no first-frame disagreement with the
CSS. Verified at 375: pin static, spacer display:none, track 740 not 3279,
controls advance through all five, and a 600px scroll does NOT hijack the
selection.

**Reduced motion gets no spacer** — with the transforms off there is nothing to
progress through, and four extra viewports of scroll is just an obstacle.

**Verified without rAF:** every division's scroll target round-trips — target
-> progress -> index returns the same division for all five. That is the
strongest available check on the scroll/controls agreement when `useScroll`
itself cannot tick (0 rAF ticks; arrows are a no-op here only because
`lenis.scrollTo` animates on rAF, confirmed by scrolling to the same targets
directly and landing exactly).

## 2026-08-15 — Ambient gradients + tactile hover on the carousel

Layered gradient lighting, border glows and hover physics onto the existing
carousel. Layout, navbar and scroll mechanics untouched.

**SPLIT THE ACCENT INTO `accent` AND `ink` — the substantive change.**

The ambient tint raises the panel's luminance, and the per-slide accents had
barely half a point of contrast to spare above AA. Measured with the wash at
full strength over the copy: the deepest foil (slide 05 purple) fell to
**3.51:1**, with 02 and 04 at 4.29 — all failing.

Two ways out, and the arithmetic decided it. Diluting the gradient until the
old colours passed needed roughly 5% total tint — invisible, i.e. not the
feature that was asked for. So `accent` stays exactly as rich as intended for
borders, dots, glows and washes, and a lifted `ink` of the same hue carries the
small TEXT: badge, kicker, through-links. After: **min 6.00** across all five
slides under the same pessimistic assumption.

Worth keeping as a rule — when decoration and legibility want the same colour,
give them two.

**GRADIENTS BRIGHTEN VIA OPACITY, NOT BY SWAPPING THE GRADIENT.**
`background-image` is not an animatable property, so a hover that changes the
gradient itself pops rather than fades. Two stacked layers with one crossing
0 -> 1 gives the same effect on a property that genuinely interpolates.

**`rotateZ` IS KEPT TINY BECAUSE IT GROWS THE BOUNDING BOX.** The stage sits
flush against the page gutter; half a degree plus `scale(1.03)` widens it from
631 to 654, moving its left edge 80 -> 69 and right 711 -> 723 inside a 1265
client width. Verified by FORCING the hover end-state, since whileHover needs
rAF: no horizontal overflow at 1280 or at 375.

**`group/row`, NOT `group`.** The rows sit inside the panel, which is itself a
hover group. An unnamed group would light every row at once the moment the
pointer touched the card anywhere — which reads as a rendering fault, not a
response. Named groups scope each row to its own hover.

**The corner wash runs `to top left`,** so its strong stop is bottom-right and
the copy — kicker, heading and services, all top-left — sits on untinted
ground. The measurement above deliberately ignored that geometry and assumed
full overlap, because the through-links DO sit low in the panel and because
copy moves over time. Passing the pessimistic case means the geometry never has
to be re-argued.

**PROBE NOTE:** measuring all five slides had to be done at MOBILE width. On
desktop the controls now scroll the page rather than set state (correct, by
design), and `lenis.scrollTo` animates on rAF — so on desktop the probe only
ever saw slide 01. Below `lg` the controls own the state directly and all five
are reachable.

## 2026-08-15 — Gallery: both "bugs" diagnosed before styling anything

### 1. The white was not a leak — the page WAS ivory

`GalleryGrid`'s root was `bg-smaya-ivory ... text-smaya-charcoal`. Measured
before touching it: root background `rgb(250,249,246)`, text charcoal. Nothing
was bleeding through; the gallery was simply a light page. Now `bg-[#050505]
text-white`.

**The footer is NOT the culprit** — worth recording because it looks like one.
590px sit below the section, which reads as a big block at the bottom of the
page, but it measures `rgb(28,27,31)` charcoal. Dark already. I checked rather
than assumed and left it alone.

**Added a viewport-FIXED floor anyway.** `min-h-screen` is not a guarantee: the
body is ivory site-wide, so filtering down to one card collapses the section
below the viewport, and rubber-band overscroll on iOS exposes the body colour
regardless of height. A `fixed inset-0 -z-10` layer closes both.

### 2. The blank images were REMOTE URLS THAT NEVER ARRIVE

Every portfolio item pointed at `images.unsplash.com`. Measured: 8 of 10 images
still PENDING after three seconds, and a direct probe of the next/image proxy
to that origin ABORTED at four. next/image proxies remote files at request
time, so a slow origin yields a blank card rather than a slow one — this is the
same failure that has produced `upstream image response timed out` and 500s on
service routes all session.

Repointed all eight at local assets, matched by category. Verified: all 8 files
200, all 8 decode, zero remote URLs left.

**THIS ALSO FIXED THE HOME PAGE.** `portfolioPreview` slices the same array, so
`components/home/PortfolioPreview.tsx` was showing the same empty boxes. One
data change, two surfaces.

### The fallback is state, not a CSS trick

A broken `<img>` paints the browser's own empty-frame chrome, and no background
styling behind it removes that. `onError` unmounts the image and the textured
gradient takes the space. **Verified by actually failing one**: dispatching
`error` set `data-fallback=true`, removed the img, applied the
`#1a1714 -> #0a0908` gradient, and the card kept its height.

The gradient sits underneath the image at all times, so a slow load lands on
the placeholder rather than on grey.

**Contrast AA throughout:** card titles 5.92, badges 4.68-7.72, idle pills
9.83, active pill and h1 20.38. Min 4.68 (the Entertainment badge at 9px).

**Idle pills are `white/70`, not their accent** — seven accent chips in a row
compete with the work they filter, and at 11px the accent would also be the
weakest text on the page.

**FILTERING COULD NOT BE VERIFIED IN THE DOM** — 0 rAF ticks, so AnimatePresence
exits never start and all eight cards stay mounted at opacity 1. Verified the
predicate against the data instead: All 8, Weddings 2, Corporate 1, Fitness 2,
Trek 1, Community 1, Entertainment 1, and every filter returns only its own
category.

**`DragCarousel.tsx` is now unused** — the brief replaced the rail with a
masonry grid. Left on disk with the other dead components; still no VCS.

## 2026-08-15 — Atmospheric canvas: defined once, not pasted nine times

Replaced flat `bg-[#050505]` with a layered radial canvas plus ambient studio
lamps.

**THE INVENTORY DECIDED THE APPROACH.** `#050505` appeared in nine files — but
FIVE of them are dead components from earlier /services designs (MasterCarousel,
CinematicBento, SplitIndex, ChapterFeed, CategoryVaults). A paste-everywhere
change would have edited more code that never renders than code that does. So:

  - `@utility surface-obsidian` in globals.css — the canvas, defined once.
  - `components/ui/Atmosphere.tsx` — the lamps, defined once.

Only three live surfaces needed touching: GalleryGrid, AsymmetricCarousel,
WorkshopAtelier. The dead files keep their flat black and it costs nothing.

**DEDUPLICATED A REAL COPY.** WorkshopAtelier already carried its own warm/cool
lamps at very nearly the shared coordinates (18%/82% vs 12%/88%). Two
near-identical gradients in two files is exactly the drift the utility exists
to prevent, so its bespoke pair was replaced by `<Atmosphere />`.

**`intensity` IS A LAYER OPACITY, NOT BAKED INTO THE STOPS.** Baking it would
need five rgba values recomputed per call site and would silently shift the
warm/cool balance; one multiplier keeps the proportion identical at every
strength. /services runs at 0.55 because each slide already throws its own
accent glow — at full strength the shared lamps washed out the per-division
identity.

**`fixed` vs `absolute` is a real distinction, not a default.** Full-page
canvases (gallery, services) pin the lighting to the viewport so content scrolls
through it. WorkshopAtelier is a SECTION on a longer page, so its rig is
absolute — a fixed one would keep shining after the reader scrolled past.

**THE CANVAS IS LIGHTER THAN WHAT EVERY PRIOR MEASUREMENT ASSUMED — checked.**
`--surface-lift` (#141218) has luminance 0.00647 against flat black's 0.00152.
The warm lamp sits in the same upper-left corner as the lift and the copy, so
the true worst-case ground is all three compounded: **rgb(49,43,41)**, up from
rgb(5,5,5).

Re-measured against that: gallery h1 13.95, lede/idle-pill 7.62, gold eyebrow
6.17 (the floor); /services h1 16.12, eyebrow 10.86 at 0.55 intensity. AA
throughout, min 6.17.

Left a note in the utility itself: `--surface-lift` is the lightest point of the
whole canvas and therefore the ground every dark-page contrast figure is taken
against — raising it lowers every ratio on the site at once.

**Focus-ring offsets left at `#050505`.** They now sit two luminance units off
`--surface-mid` (#070609), which is imperceptible, and churning six files for
that would be noise.

## 2026-08-15 — Home: diagonal marquee replaces the Selected Works grid

Two counter-rotating rows on the obsidian canvas, tilted -3deg, cards with
category badges and hover scale.

**CSS KEYFRAMES, NOT A FRAMER TWEEN — and the reason is the brief's own.**
An infinite Framer animation runs a rAF callback for the life of the page,
main-thread, on the HOME page, whether or not the section is on screen. CSS
keyframes are compositor-driven and cost the main thread nothing, which makes
the brief's stated worry about "a performance hit on mobile" disappear rather
than get managed. It also turns pause-on-hover into one declaration instead of
imperative start/stop that resumes from wherever the tween happened to be.

**REAL BUG FOUND AND FIXED: the seam jumped every lap.**
With flex `gap`, a track of two six-card sets has ELEVEN gaps, so half its
width is six cards plus five-and-a-half gaps while one set is six plus six.
Measured: half-track 2292 against a set width of 2301 — a 9px jump per lap,
forever. Moved the spacing onto the cards as `mr-*` so every unit is identical.
Re-measured in LAYOUT coordinates: set 2304, half-track 2304, **error exactly
0**.

**PROBE TRAPS, TWO OF THEM:**
1. After the fix the rect-based figure still showed 3px off. That is the
   ROTATION: `getBoundingClientRect` measures rotated geometry, and
   cos(3°)x2304 = 2300.8. `offsetLeft` is layout position and unaffected —
   that is the right tool once anything is rotated.
2. `getComputedStyle(el).transform` read "none" and I briefly thought the tilt
   had not applied. Tailwind v4 uses the INDEPENDENT `rotate` property, not
   `transform`. It was -3deg all along.

**THE 6px / 10px PAGE OVERFLOW IS NOT MINE.** Differential-tested by hiding the
section and re-measuring: identical with and without it, at both 1280 and 375.
It pre-exists elsewhere on the home page. Flagged to the user rather than
quietly absorbed — a horizontal scrollbar on the phone home page is a real
defect, just not this brief's.

**MOBILE GETS A REAL SCROLLER, NOT A SLOWER MARQUEE.** Below `sm`: no tilt, no
animation, `overflow-x-auto` with momentum, duplicates `hidden`. Slowing the
marquee instead would leave a phone running a permanent compositor animation
for content the reader cannot pause without a hover they do not have. Verified:
animationName `none`, rows scrollable, 6 cards visible, 0 duplicates.

**The duplicate set is `aria-hidden`** — a screen reader should hear six works,
not twelve.

**FEATURED IS NAMED, NOT SLICED.** The old section took `portfolioPreview =
slice(0, 5)`, so the home page silently changed whenever anyone reordered the
portfolio array. Six titles are now listed explicitly.

**Verified:** keyframes present, pause-on-hover rule exists AND its selector
matches the track, `motion-reduce:!animate-none` rule generated and applied to
the track. Contrast AA — heading and CTA 13.95, eyebrow 6.17, badges 4.55-6.97,
titles 5.49.

**Brief lists "Massive Pool Party (Community)"; the data says Fitness.** Kept
the data so the badge matches the gallery. Flagged.

## 2026-08-15 — Events: dark glass cards, and a structured-data catch

**The white was the page shell**, same as the gallery: `app/events/page.tsx`
carried `bg-smaya-ivory` with charcoal text, and the cards were literal
`bg-white`. Measured before changing anything: shell rgb(250,249,246), card
rgb(255,255,255). PageHero was ALREADY dark, which is why the ivory only showed
below it — exactly where the list sits.

**All four images were remote Unsplash URLs** — the same origin that leaves
blank cards elsewhere in this project. Repointed to local assets matched by
subject (zumba / treks / parties / corporate-focus-retreats). Verified 4/4
decode, 0 broken, 0 remote.

**THE FIX BROKE THE STRUCTURED DATA, AND I CAUGHT IT.**
`eventsJsonLd` passes `image: event.img` straight through. That was a full
Unsplash URL and so happened to be a valid absolute URL by accident; local
paths made it `/images/zumba-training.jpg`, which a crawler resolves against
its OWN host and finds nothing. Now `${site.url}${event.img}`. Verified in the
rendered JSON-LD: `https://smayaevents.com/images/zumba-training.jpg`.

Worth remembering: changing an image path can silently break a consumer that
never renders.

**REAL CONTRAST FAILURE:** the "Wellness & Entertainment" pill measured 4.23:1
— under AA for 9px text. The pill sits ON the photograph, so its own backing is
the only thing between the accent and whatever the image does. Deepened the
PILL from 0.78 to 0.88 rather than the vignette, keeping the photography
bright: now 5.53. All pills 5.53-8.47.

**`minmax(0,…)` on the grid tracks, not `w-2/5` + flex.** A grid track defaults
to `min-width: auto` and refuses to shrink below its content — that is how a
long location string widens the column instead of wrapping inside it.

**`accentFor()` falls back to gold.** `type` is free text in data/events.ts
("Wellness & Entertainment", "Corporate Retreat"), so an unmatched value has to
degrade to a real colour rather than to `undefined` and a transparent border.

**Added the same fixed floor the gallery carries.** Measured first: shell 1838
+ footer 590 = 2428 = documentH exactly, so NO ivory is reachable by scrolling.
The floor closes only the iOS overscroll case — added for parity so the two
dark pages behave identically rather than because the page was leaking.

**Verified:** 4 events, dark glass cards, ISO dates on `<time>`, 4 CTAs, no
horizontal overflow; at 320px single column, zero clipped nodes, CTA 170x44.
Contrast AA — titles 18.40, desc 10.58, location 9.38, dates/CTAs 6.08-9.31.

## 2026-08-15 — Events: expanding deck, one property across two axes

**`flexGrow`, NOT the `layout` prop — and the reason is structural, not
stylistic.**

The deck is `flex-col` below `md` and `flex-row` above it, and `flex-grow`
distributes along whichever axis is current. So ONE animated property gives a
horizontal expansion on desktop and a vertical accordion on a phone, with no
breakpoint branch in the JS and nothing that can fall out of step with the CSS.

`layout` was the obvious choice and is wrong here for the third time this
session: framer performs layout animations by SCALING, which squashes the type
inside a panel for the whole transition — and the type is the payload.

Verified both axes by forcing the end state:
  desktop 1280: 702 / 118 / 118 / 118, summing exactly to the 1104px deck, for
    every one of the four open states
  mobile 375:  347 / 59 / 59 / 59, summing exactly to the 560px deck

**THE WHOLE PANEL IS ONE ABSOLUTE BUTTON, not a <button> wrapper.** The open
panel contains a "Register Now" LINK, and an anchor inside a button is invalid
HTML that browsers and screen readers each resolve differently. A full-bleed
button beneath the content gives the same target while the CTA stays a real
anchor above it — content layer is `pointer-events-none`, the CTA re-enables
them for itself.

**REAL BUG: the pill overflowed its collapsed panel.** "Wellness &
Entertainment" is 24 characters and a collapsed panel is 118px wide — measured,
it escaped and was clipped, so the category was unreadable exactly where it was
supposed to inform. Nothing shorter fixes it; the space is the constraint. The
pill now renders only when the panel is open, which is also what the brief
describes a collapsed card as showing ("a vertical title and atmospheric
thumbnail"). Re-measured: zero escaped nodes at 1280 and 375.

**`writing-mode` is scoped to `md`.** A collapsed panel is a narrow COLUMN on
desktop, where a horizontal title clips to a few characters; on a phone it is a
wide, short ROW, where vertical text would be the thing that breaks. Verified
`vertical-rl` at 1280 and `horizontal-tb` at 375.

**PROBE TRAP — I broke the layout with my own cleanup.** After forcing
`style.flexGrow` to measure the end states, I reset it with `style.flexGrow=''`
— which removes the value FRAMER OWNS, leaving `basis-0 shrink` with no grow
and collapsing every panel to 1px. The next measurement then reported the
collapsed title as not fitting. Restore the framework's value explicitly, or
reload, rather than clearing an inline style that a library is driving.

**Exactly one panel open, always.** A deck where all four can close shows four
identical slivers and no event details — a calendar page displaying no dates.

**Arrow keys accept BOTH axes** (Left/Right and Up/Down) rather than guessing
which layout the reader is looking at, since the same deck is a row or a column
depending on width.

## 2026-08-15 — Events: scroll progression + hover override

**TWO PIECES OF STATE, NOT ONE — this is what makes hover-override work.**

`sequenced` is where the scroll has reached; `hovered` is a temporary preview;
the displayed panel is `hovered ?? sequenced`. Collapsing them into one value
would strand the reader on whatever they last pointed at, because there would be
nothing left to revert TO. Verified: hovering panels 2, 3, 1 previews each in
turn, and leaving the deck returns to the scroll-derived 0.

**Selecting MOVES THE PAGE while pinned, rather than setting the index.** With
the sequence derived from scroll, an index set directly is overwritten by the
next scroll frame and the click appears to do nothing. Unpinned there is no
scroll to derive from, so the click owns the state outright.

**Scroll budget is real in-flow height (`md:h-[300vh]`), not padding** — a
sticky child is constrained to its containing block, which for a block parent
is the CONTENT box, and padding sits outside it. Verified pinned at frameTop 0
across the whole 3x track.

**`overflow-hidden` sits on the STICKY ELEMENT ITSELF, which is safe.** An
ANCESTOR with hidden overflow becomes the scroll container sticky resolves
against and the pin dies silently — that is the bug from the workshops turn.
Checked the ancestor chain: only BODY, which is the known false positive (its
overflow propagates to the viewport), and the pin demonstrably works.

**Every card's scroll target round-trips** — target -> progress -> index returns
the same card for all four, and a sweep of the track reaches all four. That is
the strongest available check while `useScroll` cannot tick (0 rAF here).

**MOBILE IS UNPINNED, and gated the same way as the carousel.** Below `md`:
track 672px rather than 3x viewport, frame `relative`, deck `column`, clicks own
the state, and a 500px scroll does NOT hijack the selection. Pinning there would
trap a phone in three viewports of scroll to read four events.

**Reduced motion drops the tall track entirely** — three viewports of scroll
with nothing animating is an obstacle, not an effect.

**PROBE TRAP, AGAIN: `mouseenter` with `bubbles:false` does nothing.** React
synthesises `onMouseEnter` from a BUBBLING `mouseover` at the root. Same trap as
the workshops turn; it made hover-override look broken when focus-preview
already proved the state path worked. Use `mouseover`/`mouseout` with
`bubbles:true`.

**Hover lift is `scale`, expansion is `flexGrow`** — a transform and a layout
property, so they animate on different systems and neither fights the other. A
scale also cannot disturb the deck's distribution, which is what stops a hover
nudging its neighbours.

## 2026-08-15 — Blog: dark editorial feed, and the links had nowhere to go

**Same two defects as gallery and events, same causes:** the shell carried
`bg-smaya-ivory` (the "white background" WAS the page) and all six images were
remote Unsplash URLs. Repointed to local assets by subject; 6/6 decode, 0
remote.

**THE BRIEF ASKED FOR "READ ARTICLE" LINKS AND THERE WAS NOWHERE TO SEND THEM.**

`app/blog/[slug]` did not exist, and the previous BlogGrid had NO href on any
card — the posts were not linked from anywhere. Adding the CTA the brief asks
for would have shipped six 404s.

Created the route. It renders ONLY what data/blog.ts actually holds — title,
date, category, image, excerpt — and says plainly that the full piece is not
published yet. **It invents no article prose**: there is no body copy anywhere
in the project, and writing some would be putting words in the client's mouth
on their own publication. The closing panel is the one thing to replace when
real bodies exist; nothing else needs to change.

Verified all six links return 200 (every one would have 404'd), and an unknown
slug returns 404 rather than rendering an empty shell at 200 — which is what
`notFound()` buys and what a crawler would otherwise index as a real page.

**Open Graph images are absolute** (`${site.url}${post.img}`) for the same
reason the events JSON-LD needed it: OG is read off-site, where a leading-slash
path resolves against the crawler's host.

**The spotlight is `posts[0]`, not a hardcoded slug.** data/blog.ts is in
publication order, so "the latest article" is a POSITION; naming the wedding
piece would quietly stop being true the moment anyone publishes and the page
would keep featuring last season's article.

**`mt-auto` on the card action** pins every "Read More" to the bottom of its
card regardless of excerpt length, so a row's actions sit on one line.

**Verified:** contrast AAA throughout (min 7.13 — titles 18.40, excerpts
9.38-10.58, badges 7.31-8.36, dates 7.13); 320px single column, zero clipped
nodes, 44px read targets, no horizontal overflow.

## 2026-08-15 — Blog: minimalist editorial index with hover reveal

Replaced the card grid with a spotlight banner over a full-width index list:
sequence number, category, large light-weight serif headline, date, arrow.

**THE HOVER REVEAL NEEDED TWO OTHER PATHS, not one.**

A cursor-following preview is invisible to a touch device (no cursor) and to
anyone tabbing through (no pointer). Neither is a nicety here — the imagery IS
the reason the list is a lookbook rather than a table of contents. So:
  - `lg+`  floating card follows the cursor
  - `<lg`  every row carries its own inline thumbnail (`lg:hidden`)
  - focus  places the preview beside the focused row and clears on blur
Verified all three: floating preview `display:none` at 375 with 5 inline
thumbnails taking over; every hover tracks its own row; focus tracks and blur
clears.

**MOTION VALUES, NOT STATE, FOR THE CURSOR.** A mousemove handler calling
setState would re-render the whole index dozens of times a second for one
decorative card. Motion values write straight to the transform and skip React.

**THE PREVIEW IS CLAMPED TO THE VIEWPORT.** It is `position: fixed`, and a
fixed element pushed past the right edge can still extend the document's scroll
width — which is exactly how a decorative flourish becomes the horizontal
scrollbar this project has been kept clear of. Clamping up front is cheaper
than finding it later.

**PROBE TRAP — DOM ORDER LIED, AND THE REASON IS SPECIFIC.**
`previewTracksRow` read false and `clearsOnLeave` read false, which looked like
a broken state path. Two things were happening: exits are frozen (0 rAF), so
stale previews stay mounted; AND AnimatePresence keys by slug, so re-hovering a
row REUSES its still-exiting element rather than mounting a new one. That means
neither "first in DOM" nor "last in DOM" is the current entry — there is no
positional heuristic that works. Fixed by mirroring the state onto a stable
element as `data-previewed`. Re-measured: every interaction correct.

Worth generalising: when AnimatePresence keys by content, DOM order carries no
recency information at all. Expose the state.

**REAL CONTRAST FAILURE:** the spotlight's "Read Article" measured 3.83:1 — the
CTA sits ON the banner, where the scrim's strength depends on how far down the
element lands. Gave it its own backing so the contrast is a property of the
BUTTON rather than of its position: now 8.28. Final min 4.69, AA throughout.

**Index rows number 01-05, the spotlight is unnumbered** — it is a banner, not
an index entry, and numbering it would either duplicate an entry or start the
list at 02.

**Verified:** 1 spotlight + 5 rows, 13/13 images decoded, 117px row tap
targets, zero clipped nodes, no horizontal overflow at 375 or 1280.

## 2026-08-15 — Blog: uniform cinematic grid (replaces the text index)

Back to a grid, and the brief's emphasis says why: the previous editorial index
gave five of six posts their imagery ONLY through a cursor-following preview —
no image at all on a touch device, and none in the page's own layout. A uniform
grid puts the same photograph in front of every reader on every device.

Verified the brief's headline requirement directly rather than assuming it:
every one of the 6 cards has an image that is present, DECODED (naturalWidth >
0), visible (not display:none / visibility:hidden / opacity:0) and rendered at
383x208. Zero fallbacks triggered, zero remote URLs.

**THE WHOLE CARD IS THE LINK, and the action is a SPAN.**
One anchor wrapping the card gives a target the size of the card and one tab
stop per post. The "Read Article" line is styled as an affordance, not a second
link — an `<a>` inside an `<a>` is invalid HTML that browsers resolve by
silently dropping one of them, which is how a card ends up with a dead region.
Verified: 0 nested anchors, 6 card links all 200, exactly 6 tab stops, action
elements are all SPAN.

**`shrink-0` on the image container.** Without it flex compresses the picture to
make room for a long excerpt — which would have quietly broken the one thing
the brief asked for most loudly.

**`mt-auto` on the action** pins it to the bottom of every card regardless of
excerpt length. Measured: every action sits 21px from its card's bottom, so a
row's actions align rather than stepping.

**Contrast AAA throughout:** titles 18.40, excerpts 9.38, dates/actions
8.13-9.31, pills 7.26-8.30. Min 7.26.

**Verified:** 3 columns at 1280, 1 at 320, 6/6 images decoded at both, zero
clipped nodes, no horizontal overflow.

**BUILD NOTE:** the previous build of this page FAILED on
`fonts.gstatic.com` (Playfair, 12 errors) — the second hard build failure this
session from that CDN, and it has now started failing in DEV too (exhausting
all 3 retries on a page load). Self-hosting the two fonts remains the highest
value outstanding fix; flagged to the user four times now.


================================================================================
2026-08-15 — /contact: invalid-field styling, and two probe traps that
produced a false "the form is broken" alarm
================================================================================

RETRACTION FIRST. Last turn I reported that the ContactForm subtree "is not
hydrating" and called it a shipping blocker. That was WRONG. The form works.
Two separate probe artifacts stacked into a convincing false positive:

  1. HYDRATION OF SERVER HTML IS BLOCKED IN THE PREVIEW PANE, NOT IN THE APP.
     React 19 marks a streamed Suspense boundary `<!--$~-->` (QUEUED) and
     promotes it to `<!--$-->` on a rAF-scheduled commit. This pane runs with
     visibilityState=hidden, hasFocus=false and ZERO rAF ticks — proved
     directly: an armed rAF loop registered 0 ticks while a setTimeout in the
     same call fired. So the boundary never gets claimed, the server HTML sits
     there un-hydrated, and `form.requestSubmit()` does a NATIVE submit.
     ALL THREE boundaries on the page were stuck at `$~`, not just the form's
     — which was the clue that this was environmental, not a code defect.
     Everything OUTSIDE a boundary (navbar, nav links, sidebar links: 154
     fibers) hydrated fine.

     PROOF the code is sound: navigate to /contact CLIENT-SIDE (click a navbar
     link) instead of loading it. That renders ContactForm fresh rather than
     hydrating streamed HTML — `suspenseMarkers: []`, form and tabs both carry
     `__reactProps$`, submit runs preventDefault, 4 fields go aria-invalid and
     5 validation messages render. Zero code changes were needed.

     COROLLARY: a client-side nav leaves the orphaned un-hydrated copy in the
     DOM, so /contact then has TWO <form>s. `document.querySelector('form')`
     can grab the dead one. Always filter by `__reactProps$` first.

  2. `getComputedStyle` IS FROZEN IN THIS PANE TOO. This is the bigger one.
     Setting `el.style.borderTopColor = 'rgb(0,255,0)'` and reading it back
     returns the OLD value. Style recalculation is part of the rendering
     pipeline, which is frozen along with rAF.
     => Any computed-style reading taken AFTER a post-load DOM/class change is
        STALE — it reports what the markup had at page load.
        Readings taken on a FRESHLY LOADED page are still valid, which is how
        every contrast figure in this log was taken. But "change a class, then
        measure" is not a valid method here and never was.
     This is what made the new red border look like it had failed to apply: I
     measured 1.52:1 (white/0.15) five times, which was the SERVER HTML's
     original border, not the error-state one.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE ACTUAL CHANGE: invalid fields now look invalid.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Found while testing the above — the first time the error states had ever
actually been rendered on screen. Every field carried `aria-invalid` and a
message underneath, so a screen reader was well served and WCAG 3.3.1 was met.
A sighted user was not: the border stayed white/0.15 in BOTH states, so someone
who submitted and failed four of eight fields had no mark to scan FOR, only
prose to read.

`fieldClasses(invalid)` composes through cn() — `border-red-400/70` has to BEAT
`border-white/15`, and twMerge resolves that by source order. Plain string
concatenation would leave both classes and let the cascade pick arbitrarily.
Verified in the CSSOM (the only channel not frozen): `border-white/15` is
absent from the rendered class list, `.border-red-400\/70` matches the element,
sits in the `utilities` layer, and is the ONLY rule setting border-color on it
— base preflight contributes `border: 0px solid` and nothing else competes.

The focus ring flips red too, otherwise focusing a failed field repaints it
gold and reads as "resolved" while the error text still stands.

CONTRAST — COMPUTED, NOT MEASURED, because getComputedStyle is frozen (above):
red-400 #f87171 at 70% over the panel (#121115 at 0.9 over --surface-lift
#141218, the lightest ground on the site) = rgb(179,84,85) on rgb(18,17,21)
= 3.87:1. WCAG 1.4.11 wants 3:1 for a non-text UI component, so it passes with
headroom — against the old 1.52:1, which failed.
Error TEXT was measurable (rendered at load-time state) and is strong:
field messages 9.88, summary alert 13.05.

NOT changed: eventDate, budget, guests keep the neutral border — they are not
validated, so they can never be in an error state. `requirements` passes
`error={fieldErrors.requirements}` to Field but nothing in contact-schema.ts
ever assigns that key, so the prop is inert; left alone rather than "fixed"
into a shape that implies validation that does not exist.

STILL OUTSTANDING (unchanged, and none of it touched this turn): self-hosting
Playfair + Inter (two hard build failures this session, now failing in dev
too); the Unsplash hero images in data/verticals.ts timing out on every
service-page load; the pre-existing 6px/10px horizontal overflow on the home
page; seven dead components kept only as revert paths; no VCS.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BUILD TRAPS (two new ones, both of which made a FAILED build look like a pass)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. NEVER RUN `next build` WHILE `next dev` IS RUNNING. They share `.next`, and
   the dev server rewrites it underneath the build. Symptom is a wall of
   `[Error [PageNotFoundError]: Cannot find module for page: /]` — one per
   route, including `/` and `/_not-found` — after "Compiled successfully".
   It looks like a catastrophic routing bug and is purely the collision.
   Fix: preview_stop the server, `rm -rf .next`, then build. Clean run went
   27/27 static pages with zero errors, so nothing was ever wrong with the app.

2. `cmd > log 2>&1; echo "EXIT=$?"` REPORTS THE ECHO'S STATUS, NOT THE BUILD'S.
   The harness read "exit code 0" off a build that had failed. Always grep the
   log for "Build error occurred" / "Failed to compile" rather than trusting an
   exit code that a trailing command has overwritten.

   Related, previously logged: `next build | tail` writes nothing until the
   build ends. Redirect to a file — but then actually READ the file.

3. A watcher `until grep -qE "Compiled successfully|..."` fires MID-BUILD.
   "Compiled successfully" precedes type-checking, page-data collection and
   static generation. Terminal markers are the route table, "Build error
   occurred", or "Failed to compile".

CLEAN BUILD RESULT (dev server stopped, .next cleared): 27/27 pages, no
gstatic/Playfair failure this time, /contact prerendered ○ Static at 6.63 kB /
161 kB First Load. This clears the build debt carried by the marquee and
cinematic-grid turns as well.


